- Are you already diagnosed with type 2 diabetes, and if so what was your last A1c and last diabetes medications (metformin, insulin, SGLT2, GLP-1, etc.)?

While you confirm, here are focused questions, tests, and considerations to bring up with Dr. Jennifer Lee (PCP, Harbor General) about diabetes management and whether you should get a referral to Northwest Orthopedics.

Questions about type 2 diabetes management
- Current status and targets
  - What should my individualized A1c target be, given my age (52), hypertension, retinopathy, and other conditions?
  - How frequently should we check A1c going forward?
- Medications and safety
  - Should we start or change diabetes medications (metformin first-line, or add SGLT2 inhibitors / GLP-1 agonists)? Which drug would be best given my cardiovascular/retinal/renal status?
  - Are any of my current meds interacting with diabetes drugs or affecting blood sugar? (Bring full med list.)
- Complications screening and coordination
  - My chart notes retinopathy — how often should I have a dilated retinal exam with ophthalmology/retina? Do you recommend or will you refer me to a retinal specialist?
  - Should I see an endocrinologist if my A1c is above target or if I need complex therapy changes?
- Kidney and cardiometabolic risk
  - Should we test urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio and eGFR now? (Important before/when considering SGLT2 therapy.)
  - Do I need a statin or aspirin for primary prevention given my hypertension and diabetes? (Discuss ASCVD risk and LDL target.)
- Self-monitoring and devices
  - Would continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) or structured SMBG (self-monitoring blood glucose) help in my situation?
  - Any recommended lifestyle or diabetes education referrals (dietitian, diabetes education program)?
- Preventive care
  - Immunizations (influenza yearly, Shingrix for >50, pneumococcal as indicated, COVID boosters) — are any due?
  - Foot exam frequency and neuropathy screening — how often? Should I see podiatry for exam or footwear advice?

Important labs / checks to request at the visit
- A1c
- Basic metabolic panel (electrolytes, creatinine, eGFR)
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR)
- Lipid panel
- CBC (you mentioned anemia)
- Iron studies / B12 / folate if anemia not explained
- Liver tests (ALT/AST) if considering certain diabetes meds
- TSH if clinically indicated
- Updated blood pressure readings (and home BP log if you have one)
- If applicable: INR re-check and anticoagulation review (please confirm the value)

Questions & considerations about referral to Northwest Orthopedics
When to consider an ortho referral
- Symptoms that warrant referral:
  - Persistent joint pain that limits daily activities despite conservative measures (NSAIDs, rest, PT)
  - Mechanical symptoms (locking, catching), instability, progressive deformity, or repeated falls
  - New neurologic deficits from back/spine problems (numbness, weakness, bowel/bladder changes)
  - Pain not improving after 6–12 weeks of conservative care or worsening quickly
- Age and risk factors:
  - At 52, referral is not automatic — referrals are symptom-driven. However, diabetes increases risk for falls and for poorer bone healing, and retinopathy/neuropathy can raise fall risk, so be proactive about functional concerns.
  - Consider bone health assessment (DEXA) if risk factors for osteoporosis are present (prior fragility fracture, long steroid use, low body weight, smoking, family history). At age 52 you may still need DEXA if those risks exist.
- What to ask your PCP about orthopedics
  - Given my age, diabetes, hypertension, retinopathy, and mobility/pain symptoms (describe any you have), do you recommend referral to Northwest Orthopedics or would you first try physical therapy / imaging?
  - Should I get X‑rays or MRI before seeing orthopedics? (If imaging will speed the referral, request it.)
  - Could a trial of physical therapy, weight management, or a steroid injection be tried first?
  - Any concerns about surgery risk given my diabetes and anticoagulation/INR status? (Ask PCP to evaluate surgical risk/optimize glycemic control and anticoagulation if surgery becomes an option.)
- Red flags for urgent referral (tell PCP / urgent care right away if:)
  - Sudden severe joint swelling or warmth with fever (possible infection)
  - Acute inability to bear weight after a fall or injury
  - Rapidly worsening neurologic symptoms (loss of bowel/bladder control, severe leg weakness)

