I can help you prepare. A few quick clarifying questions before I make specific recommendations and draft questions you can bring or send to Dr. Lee:
- Did you mean your last INR was 3.20 (not 320)? Are you on an anticoagulant (warfarin, DOAC)? If so, which one and what is your usual target INR?
- Do you have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes already? If so, what are your current diabetes medications and most recent A1c (if you know it)?
- Any joint symptoms (pain, stiffness, swelling, instability), recent injuries, or trouble with walking/stairs that make you think about Northwest Orthopedics?

While you answer, here are organized, ready-to-use items for your visit.

Checklist to bring to the appointment
- Up-to-date list of all medications (prescription, OTC, supplements — include omeprazole, calcium, vitamin D), doses, and how often you take them
- Recent home readings: blood pressures, home blood glucose logs (fasting, post-prandial), weight
- Any recent lab results (A1c, kidney tests, lipid panel, INR)
- A brief note about symptoms: vision changes, neuropathy (numbness/tingling), joint pain, falls
- Insurance/referral info if you want a specialist referral

Questions to ask about type 2 diabetes management
- Screening and monitoring
  - “Can we check an A1c today? How often should we repeat it given my age and health?”
  - “Please order urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio and a CMP to check kidney function.”
  - “How often do I need foot exams, monofilament testing, and neuropathy screening?”
- Targets and overall goals
  - “What A1c target do you recommend for me (considering my age, hypertension, anemia, retinopathy)?”
  - “What blood pressure and lipid targets should we aim for with my diabetes and retinopathy history?”
- Medications and options
  - “Do my current diabetes meds need adjustment? If my A1c is above target, what medication would you add and why (metformin, GLP‑1 RA, SGLT2 inhibitor, insulin)?”
  - “Which diabetes drugs would be best considering my retinopathy, cardiovascular risk, kidney function, and risk of hypoglycemia?”
  - “Are there interactions with my current meds or supplements (omeprazole, calcium, vitamin D) I should know about?”
- Retinopathy and referrals
  - “I have a history of retinopathy — do you recommend an immediate ophthalmology/dilated retinal exam? How often should I have it going forward?”
- Prevention and complications
  - “Should I start or continue an aspirin or statin for cardiovascular prevention? (If on a statin already, check intensity.)”
  - “Any additional vaccines or screenings I should get this visit (flu, pneumococcal, shingles, DEXA for bone health)?”
- Lifestyle and monitoring plan
  - “Can we review a plan for diet, physical activity, and weight management tailored for diabetes control?”
  - “How should I monitor glucose at home (frequency, targets), and when should I contact you for hypoglycemia or hyperglycemia?”
- Referral to Endocrinology
  - “Do you think an endocrinology referral would help manage my diabetes better given my age and retinopathy, or can we manage it in primary care?”

Questions / decision points about orthopedic referral to Northwest Orthopedics
- Red flags that make referral appropriate
  - Persistent joint pain that limits activities of daily living despite conservative care
  - Recurrent mechanical symptoms (locking, catching), instability, deformity
  - Suspected fracture, unexplained joint swelling, or neurologic deficits
  - Progressive functional decline, or consideration of surgery (joint replacement)
- Primary-care steps before orthopedic referral
  - “Can you order imaging first (X‑ray of the painful joint; MRI if soft-tissue concerns)?”
  - “Should I try a trial of physical therapy, NSAIDs (if safe with my meds), or joint injections first?”
  - “Given my age (52) and risk factors (bone health supplements noted), should I have a DEXA scan to evaluate for osteoporosis?”
  - “Do I need a fall-risk assessment or physical-therapy evaluation before seeing orthopedics?”
- Practical questions to ask if referring
  - “Based on the imaging and exam, will Northwest Orthopedics evaluate surgical vs conservative options and timing?”
  - “Would anticoagulation/INR management affect any potential procedures? How should that be coordinated?”

Other items related to your conditions
- Retinopathy: confirm annual dilated eye exams and ask whether tighter glucose control might temporarily worsen retinopathy (important when intensifying therapy).
- Anemia: ask for CBC, iron studies, and cause evaluation if not recently done — calcium and vitamin D are not treatments for anemia; clarify current treatment.
- Hypertension: confirm current BP meds and home averages; ask target BP (often <130/80 in diabetes if tolerated) and whether medication adjustment is needed.
- INR/anticoagulation: confirm actual INR value, anticoagulant name, current dosing, and monitoring frequency — this affects referrals, imaging decisions, and any procedures.
- Vaccinations and preventive care: update influenza, pneumococcal, shingles (if eligible), and check for colon cancer or other age-appropriate screenings.

Suggested short scripts you can say or email
- To ask about diabetes during the visit: “I want to focus on my type 2 diabetes today — can we check an A1c, urine albumin, kidney function, and discuss whether my current meds and A1c target are appropriate given my retinopathy?”
- To ask about orthopedics: “I’ve been having [brief symptom description]. Could you order imaging and/or refer me to Northwest Orthopedics if the imaging or exam suggests they would help, or recommend PT first?”
- To confirm INR: “My last INR was listed as 320 in my records — could you confirm the correct INR value and whether my anticoagulation plan needs review before any procedures?”

Would you like me to:
- Draft a concise message or email to Dr. Lee summarizing these questions and asking for the specific labs/referrals?
- Or draft a one-page checklist/printout you can bring to the appointment?

Also please confirm the INR value and tell me your current diabetes and blood-pressure medications so I can tailor the questions and medication-specific concerns.

