Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Great — I can help you get organized for the visit. First, two quick clarifying points I should flag so your PCP visit goes smoothly:
- When you say “omeprazole 20 mg daily” did you mean that’s your current medication for reflux? (Omeprazole isn’t an antihypertensive.) Please confirm your actual blood‑pressure medication(s) and doses.
- Did you mean your last INR was 3.20 (not 320)? If you’re on warfarin or another anticoagulant, that’s important to confirm.

Below are suggested tests, focused questions about type 2 diabetes management, and guidance on whether to ask for a referral to Northwest Orthopedics. You can copy these into your notes or bring them to the appointment.

Suggested tests / items to have updated at your annual physical
- A1c (and/or fasting glucose) — to check diabetes control.
- Basic metabolic panel (BMP) or CMP — kidney function (creatinine, eGFR) and electrolytes.
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (microalbumin) — for diabetic kidney disease screening.
- Fasting lipid panel — cardiovascular risk assessment.
- CBC (for anemia) and iron studies +/- B12/folate as indicated.
- INR (if on warfarin) and medication reconciliation for any anticoagulant.
- Urine analysis and/or urine ACR again if any concerns.
- Consider DEXA bone density scan if you’re at risk for osteoporosis (age, family history, low BMI, smoking, long steroid use) — ask PCP if indicated.
- Recent ophthalmology (retina) exam: confirm date of last retinal exam given history of retinopathy.
- If you have joint symptoms: targeted imaging (X‑ray of the painful joint) before or during orthopedics referral.

Questions to ask Dr. Lee about type 2 diabetes (pick the ones most important to you)
- What is my current A1c target given my age, overall health, and history of retinopathy?
- How often should I have A1c and kidney tests done?
- Based on my A1c and kidney function, should we change or add any glucose-lowering meds? (If appropriate, ask about SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 receptor agonists for CV/renal benefit.)
- Am I on the right blood‑pressure and cholesterol regimen for diabetes? Do I need an ACE inhibitor or ARB for kidney protection if urine albumin is elevated?
- Do I need a statin or a change in statin dose for cardiovascular risk reduction?
- Do my eye findings require referral to retina/ophthalmology more frequently than annual?
- Should I monitor blood sugars at home? If so, when and how often?
- Would you recommend a referral to a diabetes educator or dietitian (medical nutrition therapy) or to endocrinology?
- Any vaccine updates I should get (flu, pneumococcal, shingles, COVID booster)?

When to consider a referral to orthopedics (Northwest Orthopedics)
Ask for a referral if any of the following apply:
- Persistent joint pain or stiffness that limits daily activities or work despite conservative care (≥6–12 weeks).
- Significant functional decline (trouble walking, climbing stairs, dressing).
- Mechanical symptoms like locking, catching, or giving way.
- Severe pain not controlled with OTC meds, or pain that wakes you at night.
- Prior imaging showing advanced osteoarthritis, fracture, or structural problems.
- Progressive deformity or neurological symptoms (numbness, weakness).
- If you’re considering surgery (joint replacement or reconstructive procedure), ask PCP to refer and to coordinate preoperative optimization (BP control, anemia management, anticoagulation plan).

Conservative steps to try before referral (you can ask your PCP about these)
- Physical therapy and targeted exercise program.
- Weight management and activity modification.
- NSAID trial (if safe with your meds/medical history) or topical analgesics.
- Intra-articular steroid or hyaluronic acid injection (if appropriate).
- Get a recent X‑ray (or MRI if indicated) and share results with PCP.

What to ask specifically about referral logistics and risks
- “Given my age and medical history (hypertension, anemia, retinopathy, and my anticoagulation status), do you think I should see orthopedics now or try more conservative care first?”
- “If I need surgery, how will my anticoagulation (INR) and anemia be managed pre‑op?”
- “Can you order X‑rays or imaging first and, if appropriate, refer me to Northwest Orthopedics for a consult?”

Visit checklist (things to bring or prepare)
- Current, complete medication list (prescriptions, OTCs, supplements) with doses.
- Any recent lab results (A1c, INR, BMP, etc.) or dates/locations so PCP can pull them up.
- Short symptom list: onset, severity, what makes it better/worse, effect on daily life.
- Note of last retinal exam and last eye provider name.
- Goals you want from visit (e.g., tighten glucose control, refer to orthopedics, update vaccines).

Would you like:
- A short, polished list you can print or text to Dr. Lee before the visit?
- A draft email or message to send to Dr. Lee requesting specific tests and a possible orthopedic referral (I can draft it for you if you want me to send or save as a draft)?

If you want me to draft questions or an email, tell me whether you want a direct ask for an orthopedics referral now or a request to obtain imaging first. Also confirm the anticoagulant name and the correct INR value and your BP medication so I can include accurate details.

