Great — I can help you get organized for the visit. A few quick clarifying questions before I prepare anything you might want to send to Dr. Lee or bring to the appointment:
- A couple of the details you listed look like they may be typos: omeprazole is usually for reflux (not hypertension), calcium + vitamin D aren’t treatments for anemia, and “INR 320” looks like it might mean 3.20. Can you confirm your current medication list (names + doses) and the correct INR value (and whether you’re on warfarin or another anticoagulant)?  
- Do you already have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes? If so, when was your last A1c and what was it?

Below are (A) focused questions to ask about type 2 diabetes management, (B) what to check if you’re thinking about an orthopedics referral, (C) tests/labs to request before/at your physical, and (D) a short email template I can draft to Dr. Jennifer Lee if you want to request these ahead of time.

A. Questions to ask Dr. Lee about type 2 diabetes management
- What should my personalized A1c target be given my age, history of retinopathy, hypertension, and other conditions?  
- When was my last A1c and what does it tell us about whether we should change therapy?  
- Are my current glucose-lowering medications still appropriate? (If you’re on metformin, SGLT2 inhibitors, GLP-1 receptor agonists, insulin, etc., ask about benefits/risks and whether any changes are recommended.)  
- Given my history of retinopathy, do I need more frequent retinal screening or an ophthalmology referral now?  
- Do I need a referral to a diabetes educator or registered dietitian for advanced self-management and meal planning?  
- Should we check for diabetic complications now (kidney, neuropathy, vascular) and how often going forward?  
- What are my blood-pressure and lipid targets with diabetes and retinopathy, and do my current BP/cholesterol meds meet those goals?  
- Do I need daily aspirin or any antiplatelet therapy given my other risks?  
- Are there medication interactions or side effects I should watch for given my other medicines (e.g., PPI, supplements, any anticoagulants)?  
- What’s the plan if I have low blood sugar (symptoms, rescue medications, when to contact you)?

B. Questions/considerations about a referral to Northwest Orthopedics
- Have my symptoms/signs (pain, stiffness, instability, limited function, falls) progressed enough to consider imaging or specialist evaluation? If so, which symptoms are the most justifying for referral?  
- Given my age (52) and history of diabetes/retinopathy (which can raise fall risk and slow healing), would you recommend an orthopedic consult earlier than usual?  
- Would a trial of conservative care first be appropriate? (Examples: physical therapy, targeted home exercise, weight management, orthotics/bracing, short course of analgesics if safe.)  
- Should I get imaging first (X-ray or MRI) before an ortho referral? If so, which imaging would you order?  
- Any need for bone-health assessment (DEXA) or labs to check for osteoporosis given my age and supplement use?  
- Are there red flags that need urgent orthopedics review now (rapidly worsening pain, deformity, neuro deficits, signs of infection, inability to walk)?

When to consider referral (useful talking points)
- Persistent pain limiting daily activities or work despite 6–12 weeks of conservative care.  
- Recurrent falls, gait instability, or poor balance.  
- Suspicion of structural issues (rotator cuff tear, meniscal injury, advanced osteoarthritis) or neurologic deficits.  
- Imaging showing moderate–severe pathology, or if surgery is being considered.

C. Tests & items to request before/at the annual physical
- Labs: A1c (or fasting glucose), basic metabolic panel (electrolytes, creatinine, eGFR), lipid panel, CBC (for anemia), iron studies or ferritin if anemia unclear, TSH (if indicated), vitamin D if not recently checked, urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (kidney damage screen).  
- If you’re on warfarin: a current INR. If “INR 3.20” is correct, confirm target range and any adjustments.  
- If musculoskeletal issues: targeted X-ray of the symptomatic joint(s) (ankle, knee, hip, shoulder) before/at referral. MRI only if soft-tissue detail needed or ortho asks.  
- DEXA scan if you have osteoporosis risk factors (age, prolonged steroid use, low body weight, family history, prior fracture).  
- Eye: confirm date of last retinal exam; if >1 year or retinopathy progressing, get ophthalmology appointment.  
- Foot exam and monofilament testing for peripheral neuropathy.  
- Vaccinations: influenza, pneumococcal (if indicated), shingles (age-appropriate), and update tetanus as needed.  
- BP measurement, orthostatic vitals if dizzy/falls, and fall-risk screening.

