I can help — first, a couple quick clarifying questions so my suggestions fit your situation:

- Do you already have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes, or are you mainly worried about risk/screening?
- Are you currently on anticoagulation (warfarin) — and did you mean INR 3.20 (not 320)?
- Do you have any joint pain, limited mobility, or recent injuries that make you think about orthopedics?  

While you answer, here are focused items you can bring up at your annual physical (organized so you can copy/paste or print):

Important pre-visit checks / things to bring
- Current medication list (name, dose, frequency) — include omeprazole, calcium, vitamin D, any blood thinners, antihypertensives, diabetes meds, OTC meds and supplements.
- Last INR value and date + name of anticoagulation clinic/provider if applicable.
- Recent blood pressure readings (home), recent glucose readings if you check them.
- Notes about vision problems or last ophthalmology/retina visit.
- Any joint symptoms: which joints, pain level, limits in activities, swelling, instability, falls, how long it’s been going on, what helps/worsens it.
- Any recent labs or imaging (CBC, CMP, A1c, lipid panel, urine albumin, DEXA, X-rays).

Questions to ask Dr. Jennifer Lee about type 2 diabetes (screening & management)
- Screening / diagnosis
  - “Should I be screened for type 2 diabetes now (A1c or fasting glucose)? Given my age and conditions, how often should screening be repeated?”
- Goals & monitoring
  - “If I have diabetes, what A1c target would you recommend for me given my age and other conditions?”
  - “How often should I have A1c, fasting glucose, and urine albumin-to-creatinine checked?”
- Prevention & risk reduction
  - “What lifestyle changes (diet, activity) do you recommend specifically for diabetes prevention or early management?”
  - “Do I need a referral to a diabetes educator or dietitian?”
- Medications & escalation
  - “If medication is needed, what would you start with and why? (e.g., metformin first-line?)”
  - “Given my history (hypertension, retinopathy, current meds), are there any diabetes drugs I should avoid or consider because they reduce cardiovascular or kidney risk (SGLT2i, GLP-1 RA)?”
- Complication screening & coordination
  - “I have a history of retinopathy — how often should I see ophthalmology now, and does that schedule change if I develop/diagnosed with T2D?”
  - “Do I need baseline kidney testing (creatinine/eGFR and urine albumin) and neuropathy/foot exam at each visit?”
- Other medical issues affecting glucose
  - “Could any of my current meds (omeprazole, supplements) or conditions affect my blood sugar?”
  - “Given my blood pressure and lipids, should we review/chang e therapy to reduce diabetes-related cardiovascular risk?”

Specific tests to request at the visit (if relevant)
- A1c (or fasting glucose) — screening/monitoring
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (for kidney microalbumin)
- Basic metabolic panel (creatinine, electrolytes, eGFR)
- Lipid panel
- CBC (you mentioned anemia) with ferritin if iron-deficiency is suspected
- Vitamin D level if on supplementation and bone health concerns
- Consider DEXA scan (see below)
- Consider ECG if cardiovascular risk is a question
- Ask for referral to diabetes educator if abnormal results or new diagnosis

Notes about your retinopathy
- Keep up regular ophthalmology/retina follow-up; glycemic and BP control slow progression.
- Ask ophthalmologist whether schedule should change based on current eye status and any new diabetes diagnosis.

Orthopedics referral — how to decide and what to ask
When to consider referral to orthopedics (Northwest Orthopedics)
- Symptoms that usually justify referral:
  - Persistent joint pain that limits daily activities despite conservative care (NSAIDs, PT, injections).
  - Mechanical symptoms (locking, catching), recurrent instability, or deformity.
  - Significant functional decline (can’t walk required distance, climb stairs).
  - Imaging (X-ray or MRI) shows advanced arthritis or structural problems and you’re discussing surgical options.
- If you have only mild or intermittent pain: start with conservative measures first (physical therapy, weight management, targeted exercise, analgesics) and ask PCP for PT referral and X-rays.
- If you’re on blood thinners or have an elevated INR: discuss coordination with anticoagulation clinic before any procedures; this affects surgical risk and timing.

Questions to ask PCP before an ortho referral
- “Given my age and risk factors, do you recommend a referral to orthopedics now or should we try conservative care first?”
- “Can we get standing X-rays of the affected joint(s) and a brief physical therapy trial before referral?”
- “Are there medical reasons (e.g., anemia, anticoagulation/INR, uncontrolled BP, retinopathy/diabetes) that would increase my surgical risk and need optimization before possible orthopedic procedures?”
- “Should I have a DEXA scan or other bone-quality testing before seeing orthopedics?”

Questions to ask an orthopedist (if referred)
- “What is causing my pain/limitations (based on exam and imaging)?”
- “What non-surgical treatments should I try and for how long?”
- “What are realistic goals/outcomes of surgery vs conservative care for my condition and activity goals?”
- “What preoperative optimization do you need (labs, medication changes, INR control, cardiology clearance)?”
- “What is the expected recovery time and rehabilitation plan?”

Other considerations and red flags
- INR: if your INR is indeed 3.20 or you’re on warfarin, this is important to tell every provider — it affects bleeding risk for injections and surgeries. If INR was a typo, confirm the correct value.
- PPI use (omeprazole): long-term PPI therapy can affect calcium absorption and has been associated with possible fracture risk — worth reviewing necessity and dose with PCP.
- Anemia: get a clear workup (CBC, ferritin, iron studies, B12) rather than assuming supplements; anemia can affect surgical risk and healing.
- Bone health: at age 52, consider DEXA if you’re at risk (menopausal status, long-term steroid use, low body weight, history of fractures). Ask PCP if DEXA is indicated.
- Vaccinations: confirm flu, COVID, shingles (age-dependent), and pneumococcal as appropriate.

Offer I can do for you right now
- Draft a concise message or portal note to Dr. Lee summarizing these concerns and requesting specific labs/referrals (A1c, urine albumin, lipid, CBC, DEXA, PT referral or ortho referral).
- Create a one-page checklist you can bring to the appointment with exact questions to read aloud.
- If you want, tell me whether you have diabetes now and confirm the INR and anticoagulant — I’ll tailor the checklist and the draft message accordingly.

Would you like me to draft a short message to Dr. Lee that requests screening labs and asks whether you should see Northwest Orthopedics, or would you prefer a printable checklist for your visit?

