Sure — I can help you organize the right questions and tests to bring to your annual physical. A few quick clarifying questions first (they’ll change what to prioritize):

- Do you have a formal diagnosis of type 2 diabetes now? If so, what are your current diabetes medications (e.g., metformin, insulin, SGLT2 inhibitor, GLP-1 RA)?
- Is your INR really “320” or did you mean 3.20? Are you taking warfarin (or another anticoagulant)?
- Are you currently having joint pain, limited mobility, falls, or functional limitations that make you consider an orthopedic referral?
- Your med list included omeprazole (that’s for reflux) and calcium + vitamin D (help bone health) — do you also take any BP meds (ACE inhibitor/ARB), statin, iron, or diabetes meds?

While you answer, here’s a focused checklist of questions, tests, and talking points you can use at your visit with Dr. Jennifer Lee.

A. Questions about type 2 diabetes management
- What is my target A1c given my age, comorbidities (hypertension, retinopathy), and risk of hypoglycemia?
- When was my last A1c and can we order an A1c at this visit (or within the next few weeks)?
- Do I need daily blood glucose monitoring or a continuous glucose monitor (CGM) based on my treatment and risk of hypoglycemia?
- Given my retinopathy, should we intensify glucose control now, or balance more conservatively to avoid hypoglycemia?
- Do I need medication changes (start or add metformin, SGLT2 inhibitor, GLP-1 RA, insulin)? If I’m already on meds, are there alternatives safer for my kidneys/eyes/heart?
- Do I need screening for diabetes complications now—neuropathy check (foot exam), urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR), and kidney function (BMP/creatinine, eGFR)?
- Should I start or continue a statin and an ACE inhibitor/ARB for cardiovascular and kidney protection (given hypertension and retinopathy)?
- Do I need B12 testing (if on metformin), and any adjustments for vitamin D/calcium?
- What lifestyle changes should I prioritize (diet, exercise, weight) and can you refer me to diabetes education or nutrition counseling?
- Vaccination review: should I get annual flu, pneumococcal, Tdap booster, COVID booster, and consider Hep B (discuss based on age/diabetes status)?

B. Tests and measurements to request
- A1c
- Basic metabolic panel (BMP) including creatinine and electrolytes
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR)
- Lipid panel
- Complete blood count (CBC) — follow up on your anemia
- Liver function tests
- Vitamin B12 (if metformin use)
- TSH if there are symptoms or as part of routine
- If on warfarin: INR now and plan for monitoring
- Annual dilated eye exam (or referral to ophthalmology/retina if you haven’t had one recently)

C. Retinopathy-specific items
- Confirm date of last dilated eye exam and next recommended frequency (annual is typical with established retinopathy).
- Ask whether tighter glucose/BP control or retinal treatments are recommended now.
- If vision changes (blurry vision, floaters), request urgent ophthalmology referral.

D. Orthopedics referral — when to ask for it
Consider asking for a Northwest Orthopedics referral if any of the following apply:
- Persistent joint pain (hip, knee, shoulder) interfering with daily activities despite conservative care (NSAIDs, PT, exercise, weight loss).
- Mechanical symptoms (locking, giving way) or progressive loss of function/mobility.
- Imaging (X-ray/MRI) might be helpful — ask PCP to order appropriate imaging if you have structural concerns.
- Frequent falls or high fracture risk (especially if you have low bone density or on anticoagulation).
If you do not have significant symptoms, alternatives to immediate referral include: trial of physical therapy, targeted imaging ordered by PCP, falls-risk assessment, bone density testing (DEXA) if not recently done.

E. Medication reconciliation & safety
- Review all meds: confirm which blood pressure medicine you take, diabetes meds, statins, antiplatelet/anticoagulant, and supplements (calcium, vitamin D).
- Clarify the INR: if you’re on warfarin, confirm current INR (3.20 is plausible; 320 is not). Ask about interactions (new diabetes meds, antibiotics) and monitoring frequency.
- Consider drug interactions (PPIs like omeprazole can affect absorption of some meds—PCP can review).

F. Practical items to bring or prepare for the visit
- Printed/phone list of current meds, doses, and OTC/supplements.
- Recent blood sugar logs or CGM printout.
- Any recent lab results or imaging.
- A short description of joint symptoms (when started, what makes it better/worse, how it limits you).
- Note any vision changes, neuropathy symptoms (numbness/tingling), or falls.

G. Sample phrasing you can use with Dr. Lee
- “Given my retinopathy and hypertension, what should my A1c target be, and do you recommend any changes to my diabetes treatment?”
- “Can we check A1c, UACR, BMP, lipid panel, and CBC today to update my diabetes and anemia monitoring?”
- “I’ve had [describe joint symptom]. Do you recommend physical therapy, imaging, or a referral to Northwest Orthopedics?”
- “My last INR reading was [confirm value]. Am I on warfarin, and do we need to update monitoring or medication interactions with any diabetes drugs?”

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft an email or message to Dr. Jennifer Lee listing these questions and requesting the lab orders/referrals you want.
- Create a one-page printable checklist you can bring to the visit.
- Help you decide on an A1c target or walk through pros/cons of starting specific diabetes medications if you tell me your current A1c and meds.

Which of those would you like me to do next, and can you answer the clarifying questions (diabetes meds, true INR value, and whether you have joint symptoms)?

