Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Great — I can help you get organized for your annual physical with Dr. Jennifer Lee. I’ll (A) flag a few points I need you to confirm, (B) list focused questions to bring up about type 2 diabetes management, (C) list tests/exams to request, and (D) outline when an orthopedic referral to Northwest Orthopedics would make sense and what to ask about that. At the end I’ll offer to turn this into a one-page checklist or a short message you can send to Dr. Lee.

A — Quick clarifications (please confirm)
- Do you have an existing diagnosis of type 2 diabetes, and if so, what is your most recent A1c and fasting glucose?  
- Your note says “hypertension (omeprazole 20 mg daily)” — omeprazole is for reflux/GERD. What antihypertensive medication(s) are you actually taking?  
- You mentioned “anemia (calcium and vitamin D)” — are you actually taking calcium/vitamin D for bone health and a separate treatment for anemia (iron/ferrous sulfate, B12, etc.)? What was the cause of the anemia?  
- Last INR “320” looks like a typo — did you mean INR 3.20? Are you on warfarin or another anticoagulant?  
- Are you having joint pain, stiffness, limited mobility, falls, or other orthopedic symptoms now? Any specific joints involved?

B — Questions to ask about type 2 diabetes management
- Screening and monitoring
  - “Can we check an A1c today (or review my most recent A1c)? What is my target A1c given my age and other conditions?”
  - “How often should I have A1c, fasting glucose, and urine albumin checked?”
  - “Can we screen for diabetic neuropathy (monofilament test) and review foot care?”
- Eye and kidney care
  - “I have a history of retinopathy — when is my next dilated retinal exam and does my retinopathy change how we set glucose targets?”
  - “Should we check urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio and kidney function (creatinine/eGFR) today?”
- Medication review and options
  - “Are my current medications (list them) optimal for diabetes and for reducing cardiovascular and kidney risk? Should we consider metformin, an SGLT2 inhibitor, or a GLP-1 receptor agonist given my age/comorbidities?” (Note: benefits/risks depend on kidney function, heart disease history, weight, and cost/coverage.)
  - “Are there interactions between my blood pressure, anticoagulant (if any), and diabetes meds we should review?”
  - “Do I need treatment changes to reduce hypoglycemia risk (if applicable)?”
- Lifestyle and complications prevention
  - “What specific exercise and diet goals should I follow? Any referral to diabetes education or nutrition counseling?”
  - “Do I need immunizations (influenza, pneumococcal, shingles, updated COVID booster) related to diabetes?”
- Follow-up
  - “How often should we follow up for medication adjustments or if I change symptoms?”

C — Tests and exams to request (useful to have for the visit)
- Labs
  - A1c
  - Fasting glucose (if indicated)
  - Basic metabolic panel (BMP) including creatinine/eGFR and electrolytes
  - Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (spot urine)
  - Lipid panel
  - Complete blood count (CBC) — evaluate anemia
  - Iron studies / B12 / folate depending on anemia cause
  - INR recheck (if on warfarin) and review anticoagulation management
- Exams and referrals
  - Dilated eye exam (ophthalmology) — especially with history of retinopathy
  - Foot exam (monofilament/vibration)
  - Referral to diabetes educator or nutritionist if needed
  - Vaccination review (influenza, pneumococcal, shingles, COVID)

D — Should you get a referral to Northwest Orthopedics?
When a PCP commonly refers to orthopedics:
- Indications that favor referral:
  - Persistent joint pain or stiffness that limits daily function despite several weeks/months of conservative care (exercise, NSAIDs as appropriate, physical therapy, weight loss)
  - Suspected mechanical problems (instability, locking, significant deformity)
  - Imaging that shows severe osteoarthritis, suspected fracture, or other structural abnormality
  - Neurologic signs (weakness, progressive numbness) that suggest nerve impingement
  - Planning for possible surgical interventions (e.g., joint replacement) or specialized injections
- What your PCP will often try first:
  - Office exam, plain X-ray of the joint, trial of PT, activity modification, weight management, topical or oral analgesics, and steroid injections if appropriate
  - DEXA scan if bone health/osteoporosis is a concern (you’re 52 and taking calcium/vitamin D — this may be reasonable depending on sex, menopausal status, fracture risk)
- Questions to ask Dr. Lee about orthopedics:
  - “Given my age and risk factors, do you recommend imaging (X-ray, MRI) or a DEXA before considering an orthopedic referral?”
  - “Should I try physical therapy or other conservative measures first, or would you refer me now to Northwest Orthopedics?”
  - “Are there red flags that should prompt an urgent referral to orthopedics?”

E — Practical visit checklist to bring
- Updated medication list (include dose: omeprazole, calcium/vit D, blood pressure meds, anticoagulants, etc.)
- Any recent lab results (A1c, INR, CBC, BMP)
- Notes about symptoms: vision changes, neuropathy (numbness/tingling), joint pain (which joints, how long, what limits you), falls
- Goals you want to discuss (A1c target, pain/function goals)
- Ask for written care plan and follow-up schedule

Safety note and recommendations
- Please confirm the INR value and whether you’re on warfarin — if you are anticoagulated, make sure INR is monitored and tell Dr. Lee before any procedures or when changing meds.
- Because you have retinopathy, retinal exams and careful glucose control are important — mention this early in the visit.

Would you like:
- A one-page printable checklist for the visit with the exact items above?  
- A short message/email drafted to Dr. Lee requesting these tests and a discussion about diabetes medication optimization and orthopedic referral?

If you confirm the medication list, INR, and whether you have a formal diabetes diagnosis and recent A1c, I can tailor the checklist and suggested questions even more precisely.

