Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is a good time to get organized before your annual. A few quick clarifying questions first so I can tailor the recommendations and suggested questions:

- Do you have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes already, or are you asking about screening/early management because of risk factors?
- What sex are you (male/female)? (Affects bone/DEXA screening and some risk thresholds.)
- You listed “omeprazole 20 mg daily” under hypertension — omeprazole is a PPI (for reflux). Is that just a medication list item rather than your BP medicine? If so, what blood-pressure medicine are you taking?
- You wrote “anemia (calcium and vitamin D)” — are calcium + vitamin D supplements what you take, or is there a specific anemia treatment (iron, B12, etc.)?
- “My last INR was 320” — did you mean 3.20 (INR usually reported as 3.2)? Are you taking warfarin or another anticoagulant?

Once I have those, I can make the suggestions more precise. Meanwhile, below are general, practical things to consider and sample questions you can bring to Dr. Jennifer Lee at Harbor General.

Suggested baseline tests/measurements to request at the visit (relevant to diabetes risk and your history)
- HbA1c (or fasting glucose) — screening/monitoring for diabetes
- Fasting lipid panel (CV risk management)
- Basic metabolic panel / serum creatinine and eGFR (kidney function)
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (early diabetic kidney disease)
- CBC (to re-evaluate anemia)
- TSH if anemia or other symptoms suggest thyroid disease
- Liver tests if starting/modifying diabetes meds
- Blood pressure and weight/BMI measurement
- Foot exam and monofilament screening (neuropathy)
- Retinal screening confirmation (or referral to ophthalmology) because of your retinopathy history
- DEXA scan discussion if you’re at risk for osteoporosis (age, low BMI, long-term steroid use, fracture history)

Key diabetes-management questions to ask your PCP (tailor these to whether you already have diabetes)
- Do I currently meet criteria for type 2 diabetes or prediabetes? What were my HbA1c/glucose targets?
- What A1c target do you recommend for me, given my age, retinopathy, hypertension and other conditions?
- Do I need medication now (or should current diabetes meds be adjusted)? If yes — pros/cons of options (metformin, SGLT2i, GLP-1 RA, insulin) especially given kidney/cardiac status.
- Given my cardiovascular risk (hypertension, lipids, age), should I be on an SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 receptor agonist for heart/kidney protection?
- How often should I check A1c and other labs?
- Are there interactions between my current medications (including anticoagulants, PPIs) and diabetes medications?
- Do I need a referral to an endocrinologist for tighter control or complex medication changes?
- How should we screen for and manage diabetic complications: neuropathy, nephropathy (urine ACR), and repeat retinal exams? When should I see ophthalmology again?
- Lifestyle: Are my diet, exercise, and weight goals appropriate? Would a diabetes education program or dietitian be helpful?
- Immunizations: are any vaccines recommended for me (flu, pneumococcal, Tdap, shingles)?

Questions and discussion points about an orthopedics referral (Northwest Orthopedics)
- Do you have specific symptoms? Ask yourself: persistent joint pain, stiffness limiting activities, instability/falls, loss of function, or failure of conservative treatment (PT, injections, meds)?
- If you have chronic joint pain or mobility loss, these are good PCP talking points:
  - How long has this been going on and what conservative care has been tried (PT, OTC meds, injections, activity changes)?
  - Do you need imaging (X-ray, MRI) before referral?
  - Given my age and risk factors (diabetes, possible bone-health issues), would you refer me to orthopedics now or try more conservative therapy first?
  - If surgery is a possibility, is my current control of blood sugar, blood pressure, anemia, and anticoagulation (INR) appropriate for elective procedures?
- When to definitely refer: severe pain interfering with daily life, progressive loss of mobility or function, suspected fracture, or neurovascular compromise.
- Consider a referral to physical therapy or sports medicine first if symptoms are mild–moderate and there’s no red flag.

Other important considerations tied to your history
- Retinopathy: ensure regular ophthalmology follow-up — retinopathy progression is a major reason to prioritize glucose and BP control.
- Anticoagulation: if you’re on warfarin (INR ~3.2), that affects decisions about injections, joint procedures or surgery. Confirm anticoagulant and target INR.
- Anemia: clarify the cause and treatment — if iron deficiency or another treatable cause, that should be investigated before procedures.
- Bone health: calcium + vitamin D supplementation is fine, but ask about DEXA if you have other risk factors (menopausal status, steroids, low BMI, prior fractures).
- BP and CV risk: with diabetes or prediabetes, BP and lipid targets are important — clarify targets (often individualized; many aim for <130/80 if tolerated).

Sample short script to use with Dr. Lee (or ask me to turn into an email)
“Hi Dr. Lee — I’m preparing for my annual physical and would like to review screening and management for type 2 diabetes given my age and retinopathy history. Could you order HbA1c, fasting lipids, CMP (including eGFR), urine albumin/creatinine, CBC, and consider DEXA if appropriate? Also, I have some joint concerns and want your opinion about referring to Northwest Orthopedics — should we get imaging first or try PT? My current meds: omeprazole 20 mg daily, calcium + vitamin D; last INR was [please confirm]. Can we review my BP meds and whether an endocrinology or orthopedics referral is needed? Thanks.”

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short email to Dr. Jennifer Lee asking for the listed tests and referrals? (I can draft it and you can review before sending.)
- Or, would you prefer a printable checklist/one-page script of the questions to bring to the appointment?

If you answer the clarifying questions I asked, I’ll make the recommendations and sample messages more specific to your situation.

