- When was my last A1c and what is the plan for monitoring (frequency if stable vs if changing therapy)?  
- Do I need changes to my medications (metformin, SGLT2 inhibitors, GLP‑1 RA, insulin) given my retinopathy, cardiovascular and kidney risk? Which agents would you consider and why?  
- Given my retinopathy, how often should I have retinal screening with ophthalmology? Should I see an endocrinologist?  
- Do I need screening for diabetic neuropathy (foot exam, monofilament) or referral to podiatry?  
- Should we check urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio and eGFR now to assess kidney involvement?  
- Do I need a statin or dose change for cardiovascular risk reduction?  
- What hypoglycemia risks should I watch for with my current/possible medications, and how should I monitor glucose at home?  
- Any specific lifestyle targets you recommend now (weight, activity, diet)? Do you recommend diabetes education or a nutrition referral?  
- Are there vaccinations I should get or update given diabetes and age (flu, pneumococcal, shingles)?

2) Recommended labs & tests to request at the annual visit (diabetes-focused)
- A1c (or point-of-care A1c)  
- Basic metabolic panel (BMP) including creatinine and electrolytes; calculate eGFR  
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) for kidney screening  
- Lipid panel (fasting or nonfasting per clinic preference)  
- CBC (you mentioned anemia) and iron studies if indicated  
- Liver enzymes (ALT/AST) if starting certain diabetes meds  
- TSH (if clinically indicated) and vitamin B12 (especially if on metformin)  
- Retinal eye exam (coordinate with ophthalmology)  
- Foot exam / monofilament test  
- Consider HbA1c every 3 months if therapy changes, otherwise every 6 months when stable

3) Diabetes medication considerations related to your comorbidities
- SGLT2 inhibitors can have kidney and cardiovascular benefits in many patients with T2D, but check kidney function first and discuss risks (e.g., genital infections).  
- GLP‑1 receptor agonists help with A1c and weight and have cardiovascular benefit for many patients — discuss suitability and access/coverage.  
- If on metformin, monitor kidney function and vitamin B12.  
- If you’re on anticoagulation or have retinopathy, discuss bleeding or eye‑related risks if any procedures/therapies are considered.

4) Questions about a referral to Northwest Orthopedics (what to ask / consider)
Ask your PCP and yourself these screening questions to decide whether a referral is needed:
- Do I have persistent joint pain, swelling, stiffness, instability, or progressive loss of function that limits daily activities? (If yes → consider referral.)  
- Have conservative treatments been tried and failed (physical therapy, targeted exercise, bracing, weight management, NSAIDs, cortisone injection)? If you haven’t tried these, PCP may recommend them first.  
- Do I have signs suggesting structural damage (locking, giving way, deformity) or neurologic symptoms (numbness, weakness)? These would favor referral.  
- Are there imaging results (recent X‑ray or MRI) that suggest osteoarthritis, rotator cuff tear, ligament injury, or other surgically treatable conditions? If not, ask whether imaging is appropriate before or during referral.  
- Given my age (52) and risk factors (hypertension, possible anticoagulation, retinopathy, anemia), is surgical risk or bone health a concern? If you’re on long‑term anticoagulation or have bleeding risk (INR issues), make sure the orthopedist is informed — that affects procedural planning.  
- Would a trial of PT or an ortho sports/physiatry consult be appropriate before surgical evaluation?

When to definitely ask for a referral:
- Pain that persists despite months of conservative care and limits ADLs or work.  
- Mechanical symptoms (catching/locking), progressive deformity, or suspected fracture/tendon rupture.  
- Preoperative clearance needed for joint replacement or other planned procedures.

5) Bone health & fall risk (relevant to orthopedics)
- Ask about a DEXA scan if you have osteoporosis risk factors (postmenopausal, prior fragility fracture, chronic steroid use, low BMI, smoking). Age 52 may not automatically trigger screening unless risk factors exist. Your PCP can decide.  
- Because of retinopathy and possible neuropathy, discuss fall risk assessment and home safety evaluation.

6) Medication reconciliation & safety points to raise
- Clarify and list all meds (including over-the-counter supplements): blood‑pressure meds, omeprazole, calcium, vitamin D, any anticoagulant, diabetes meds.  
- If you are on warfarin (or any anticoagulant), confirm INR history (please confirm whether "320" was 3.20) and let PCP/orthopedist know for procedural planning.  
- If anemia is ongoing, ask about the cause and whether iron/B12/other workup is required before procedures.

7) Practical next steps I can help with
- I can draft a concise message or email to Dr. Jennifer Lee summarizing your concerns and asking for the tests/referrals (A1c, BMP, UACR, lipid panel, retinal exam, DEXA if indicated, and orthopedics referral if you meet criteria).  
- I can create a short checklist you can bring to the appointment with suggested questions and space for results.  
- Or, if you prefer, I can draft an appointment script you can read during the visit.

Which would you like me to do next — draft an email/message to Dr. Lee, prepare a one‑page checklist for your visit, or both? And please answer the clarifying questions above (especially about INR and current diabetes meds/status) so I can tailor everything precisely.

