Thanks — I can help you organize everything to bring up at your annual with Dr. Jennifer Lee. A few quick clarifying questions before I make a draft message or checklist for your visit:
- Do you have a formal diagnosis of type 2 diabetes now, or are you asking about diabetes prevention/risk management? If you have diabetes, what are your current diabetes medications and most recent A1c (if you know it)?
- Are you taking an anticoagulant (warfarin)? You wrote your last INR was “320” — do you mean 3.20?
- What antihypertensive(s) are you on (you listed omeprazole but that’s for reflux)?
- Do you currently have joint pain, stiffness, falls, difficulty walking, or prior orthopedic diagnoses/surgeries that make you worry about an ortho referral?

While you answer, here’s a focused, practical list you can use at your visit (and I can turn this into an email to Dr. Lee if you’d like).

Questions to ask about type 2 diabetes management
- Do I have type 2 diabetes (or prediabetes)? What is my most recent A1c and target A1c for me, given my age, hypertension, retinopathy, anemia and overall health?
- Which glucose-monitoring approach do you recommend (A1c frequency, home glucose checks, or continuous glucose monitor)?
- Do my kidney function (eGFR) or urine albumin/creatinine ratio need to be checked before changing/adding diabetes medicines?
- Given my comorbidities (hypertension, retinopathy), would a GLP-1 receptor agonist or an SGLT2 inhibitor be appropriate for me? If so, which and why?
- Do my blood pressure and lipid goals change because of diabetes risk? Should I be on or change a statin or BP medicine?
- Are there any medication interactions with my current meds (omeprazole, calcium/vitamin D, and any BP medicines or anticoagulants)?
- Can I meet with a diabetes educator or dietitian? Are there local/telehealth programs you recommend?
- How often should I have diabetic eye exams, foot exams, and neuropathy screening?
- Any need for vaccination updates (influenza, pneumococcal, shingles, COVID boosters)?

Recommended labs and checks to request at the visit (diabetes-focused and general preventive)
- A1c
- Basic metabolic panel (BMP) including creatinine and electrolytes; calculate eGFR
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio
- Lipid panel (fasting or nonfasting per clinic preference)
- CBC (for your anemia) and iron studies, ferritin, B12 and folate as indicated
- Liver function tests (before some diabetes meds)
- TSH if clinically indicated
- Hb/Hct follow-up and cause of anemia if not recently worked up
- If bone health is a concern: DEXA scan discussion (see below)

Diabetic retinopathy / eye care
- Because you have a history of retinopathy, confirm frequency of dilated retinal exams (usually at least annually; may be more often based on severity).
- Ask whether ophthalmology needs to co-manage any changes before medication changes (some agents require retinal stability monitoring).

Blood pressure, lipids, and cardiovascular risk
- Clarify your BP target (many patients with diabetes aim for <130/80 if tolerated; individualized).
- Confirm whether you should be on a statin for primary prevention (age, diabetes, and risk factors matter).
- Ask about aspirin for primary prevention only if specifically indicated (not routine for everyone).

Anemia and bone health
- Clarify the cause of your anemia and whether your current supplements (calcium and vitamin D) are the right therapy.
- At age 52, DEXA is not automatic for everyone, but consider it if you’re postmenopausal, have risk factors (steroids, low BMI, prior fracture, family history, smoking) or are worried about bone loss. Ask Dr. Lee if a DEXA is appropriate and whether referral to endocrinology or bone health clinic is recommended.

When to consider Orthopedics (Northwest Orthopedics) — questions and red flags
You don’t need an automatic ortho referral just because of age, but consider referral if you have any of:
- Persistent joint pain that limits daily function despite conservative measures (NSAIDs, PT, weight loss, home exercise)
- Mechanical symptoms: locking, catching, instability, or giving way
- Progressive loss of range of motion or mobility
- Recurrent falls or high fall risk
- Prior injury, severe deformity, or suspected fracture
- Pain that interferes with work or sleep, or pain not responding to reasonable non-surgical treatment
If you’re on anticoagulation (e.g., warfarin with INR monitoring), mention it — orthopedics will need to coordinate peri-procedure anticoagulation management if surgery is ever considered.

Suggested questions for Dr. Lee about orthopedics
- Given my age and overall risk factors, do you think I should see orthopedics for X (hip/knee/back) symptoms?
- Would you recommend imaging first (X-ray or MRI) or trial of physical therapy?
- If I wanted a second opinion about joint replacement or procedures, would you refer me to Northwest Orthopedics? Any specific surgeon or clinic contact?
- Should I have a bone density (DEXA) or orthopedic clearance before any invasive procedure given my INR/anticoagulation status or anemia?

Red flags to bring up at the visit (tell Dr. Lee if any apply)
- New or worsening vision changes
- New numbness/tingling in feet, ulcers, or non-healing sores on feet
- Chest pain, unexplained shortness of breath
- Unexplained weight loss, blood in stool, or very heavy bleeding (for anemia)
- New joint swelling, warmth, fever (possible infection)

Practical items to bring / prepare for the appointment
- Up-to-date medication list including doses (antihypertensives, diabetes meds, supplements, anticoagulants)
- Recent lab results if you have them (A1c, BMP, CBC, lipids)
- Home BP log or glucose readings if available
- List of symptoms: joint pain location, severity, how it limits activity, and when it started
- Note about retinopathy and last ophthalmology visit
- Ask whether you want labs drawn before the appointment so results are available during the visit

Suggested next steps I can help with
- Clarify the INR and current meds, and I’ll draft a concise email to Dr. Lee requesting the tests/agenda and asking whether she recommends an ortho referral to Northwest Orthopedics.
- Draft a one-page checklist/printout you can bring to the appointment with the top questions and items to discuss.
- If you want, I can draft short scripts for phone calls (e.g., to ophthalmology, orthopedics, or to schedule a DEXA or labs).

Would you like me to:
1) Draft an email to Dr. Jennifer Lee summarizing these questions and requesting specific labs and referrals, or
2) Create a one-page checklist you can print and bring to your visit?

Also please confirm: do you have diagnosed type 2 diabetes (and current diabetes meds), are you on an anticoagulant, and did you mean INR 3.20? Once you confirm, I’ll draft the email/checklist.

