Based on what you gave me, here are focused things to bring up at your annual with Dr. Jennifer Lee and questions to ask about diabetes management and about whether you need a referral to Northwest Orthopedics.

Pre-visit checklist (bring/ask)
- Current medication list (including dose/frequency) — include OTCs, supplements (calcium, vitamin D). Note anticoagulants.  
- Recent lab results or dates: A1c, fasting glucose, lipid panel, basic metabolic panel (creatinine, electrolytes), CBC, INR. If you don’t have recent A1c or labs, ask to order them.  
- Last ophthalmology visit and retinal exam report (because of history of retinopathy).  
- Any recent imaging of joints (X-ray, MRI) if you have joint problems.  
- Blood pressure readings taken at home (last week).  
- Notes about functional limitations: pain level, activities limited, falls, difficulty climbing stairs, walking distance.

Tests you may want ordered at the visit
- A1c (or fasting glucose) — to guide diabetes control.  
- Fasting lipid panel and CMP (including creatinine, eGFR).  
- Urine albumin/creatinine ratio (diabetic kidney damage screen).  
- CBC (for anemia monitoring) and vitamin D/calcium if relevant.  
- If on warfarin: INR check and medication review for interactions (omeprazole can affect INR modestly).  
- Consider DEXA bone density testing if you have osteoporosis risk factors (prior fracture, low BMI, long-term steroid use, parental hip fracture, smoking, alcohol).  
- Referral for retinal exam if not seen in the last year.

Questions to ask about type 2 diabetes management
- What is my personalized A1c target given my age, comorbidities, and hypoglycemia risk? (Typical targets range from <7% for many to higher if older/more comorbid.)  
- What was my most recent A1c and fasting glucose? If I don’t have one, can we order it now?  
- If my control needs improvement, what medication changes do you recommend? (Ask specifically about metformin first-line, and about SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 receptor agonists if cardiovascular or kidney protection would help.)  
- Given my history of retinopathy, should I be on any specific diabetes medication that’s safer or preferred?  
- How often should I have A1c checked going forward? (Usually every 3 months if changing therapy or not controlled; every 6 months if stable.)  
- What is my blood pressure and lipid target with diabetes? Do my current BP meds need adjustment? (Aim often <130/80 depending on individual factors.)  
- Do I need an ACE inhibitor or ARB for kidney protection if urine albumin is elevated or because of diabetes/hypertension?  
- How should we monitor kidney function (eGFR/albuminuria) and how often?  
- Foot care: do I need a formal foot exam or podiatry referral?  
- Vaccinations to update: influenza, pneumococcal, and shingles—are any due?  
- Are there lifestyle or referral resources (diabetes education, nutrition counseling, exercise/weight loss programs) you recommend?

Questions/criteria to discuss about referring to Northwest Orthopedics
- Describe any joint symptoms: where is the pain, how long, what makes it worse/better, effect on function (walking, stairs, work)? If you don’t have symptoms, orthopedics is usually not necessary.  
- Ask: Based on my age and medical history, do you recommend a referral to orthopedics for evaluation (hips/knees/shoulders)? Why or why not?  
- If you have pain: Have we tried conservative measures (physical therapy, weight management, NSAIDs as appropriate, corticosteroid injection)? If not, can we try them first?  
