I can help you get organised for your annual physical. A few quick clarifying questions before I lay out suggested questions and items to bring/check:

- Do you have an established diagnosis of type 2 diabetes now, or are you concerned about risk / screening only?
- Are you currently taking an anticoagulant (warfarin)? Your “last INR was 320” — did you mean 3.20?
- Can you list your current medicine list (names + doses) if different from what you wrote? (You listed omeprazole 20 mg for hypertension — omeprazole is a PPI, and calcium + vitamin D aren’t usual anemia treatments, so I want to be sure the med/condition pairings are correct.)
- Do you have joint pain, prior fractures, limited mobility, or other orthopedic symptoms that prompted the Northwest Orthopedics question?

Below are practical, focused items and suggested questions you can use at your visit with Dr. Jennifer Lee.

Pre-visit checklist (useful to request before the appointment)
- Confirm meds and bring an up-to-date list (including OTC, supplements: calcium, vitamin D, iron, etc.). Note any recent changes.
- Clarify anticoagulation status and correct INR (if on warfarin, request recent INR check).
- Ask the clinic to draw these labs before the appointment (or have them ready during the visit):
  - A1c (or fasting glucose) — for diabetes screening/management
  - Basic metabolic panel (kidney function; important if starting diabetes meds)
  - Lipid panel (cardiovascular risk)
  - Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (kidney damage screening for diabetes)
  - CBC (for anemia)
  - Vitamin B12, iron studies if anemia unclear
  - Vitamin D level (if you’re taking supplements)
  - TSH (if clinically indicated)
  - Consider DEXA scan order if bone-health risk or age/sex appropriate
- Bring records of recent eye exams (retinal specialist) and any orthopedics imaging or notes if available.

Questions about type 2 diabetes (screening vs management)
- Screening / diagnosis
  - “Do I currently have diabetes, or do my labs show prediabetes? What is my most recent A1c and what does it mean?”
  - “Should we recheck A1c or fasting glucose now or schedule it regularly? How often?”
- Targets & monitoring
  - “What A1c target is appropriate for me given my age, comorbidities (hypertension, retinopathy, anemia), and lifestyle?”
  - “If I have or develop diabetes, how often should I have A1c, kidney tests, and urine albumin checked?”
  - “Should I do home glucose monitoring, and what levels should prompt concern or a call?”
- Medication options & safety
  - “If medication is needed, which would you recommend first-line for me and why (metformin, SGLT2 inhibitor, GLP-1 agonist, etc.)?”
  - “Are there medications that would be better given my heart, kidney, and eye history?”
  - “What are the side effects and interactions with my current meds (including warfarin if applicable, omeprazole, supplements)?”
  - “If I’m on or start an agent that can cause hypoglycemia, how should I prevent and treat it?”
- Cardiovascular and kidney risk
  - “Do I need to start/continue a statin or aspirin for cardiovascular prevention based on my risk?”
  - “Do I need an SGLT2 or GLP-1 for kidney or heart protection if I have diabetes or high CV risk?”
- Lifestyle and referrals
  - “Can I see a dietitian or diabetes educator? Would structured lifestyle programs or group classes be helpful?”
  - “Do I need an endocrinology referral, or can you manage diabetes care here?”
- Eye and foot care
  - “With my history of retinopathy, how often should I have ophthalmology follow-up?”
  - “Should I be screened for peripheral neuropathy or referred to podiatry?”

Questions and criteria about orthopedics / Northwest Orthopedics
- When to consider an orthopedic referral
  - New or worsening joint pain that limits activities or sleep
  - Persistent pain >3 months despite conservative care (PT, meds, injections)
  - Mechanical symptoms (locking, catching, instability)
  - Difficulty walking, falls, or progressive loss of function
  - Suspected fracture, deformity, or severe osteoarthritis on X-ray
  - Consideration of surgical options (joint replacement) or specialized procedures
- Bone health / fracture risk
  - “Given my age and supplements, do I need a DEXA scan and a formal osteoporosis risk assessment?”
  - “Do I have fall-risk factors that need addressing (home safety, balance/strength training)?”
- Sample phrasing to ask Dr. Lee for a referral:
  - “I’m 52 and concerned about my bones/joints given my age and risk factors. I have [describe symptoms: e.g., chronic knee pain, limited mobility, recent falls]. Do you recommend a referral to Northwest Orthopedics or should we try X (imaging / PT / medication) first?”
  - If no symptoms: “I don’t currently have severe joint symptoms — should I still be referred for screening because of age or other risk factors?”

Other important items to cover at the visit
- Retinopathy: confirm ophthalmology follow-up schedule (usually annual if diabetic retinopathy).
- Vaccinations: flu, shingles (age-appropriate), pneumococcal if indicated, and update tetanus as needed.
- Blood pressure and lipid management: confirm targets and medications for hypertension and whether BP is controlled.
- Anemia: clarify cause, review iron/B12/folate status, and plan for treatment or specialist referral if needed.
- Medication reconciliation: correct any mismatches (omeprazole vs antihypertensive), review supplements, possible PPI side effects (B12/iron malabsorption over long term).

Safety notes and items to confirm
- If you are on warfarin (INR target), discuss interactions with diet and any new prescriptions — and make sure INR target is up to date.
- If you start SGLT2s or GLP-1s, discuss side effects, kidney function monitoring, and rare risks (e.g., urinary infections, dehydration).
- If you have retinopathy, tell the PCP about any vision changes immediately.

Would you like:
- A suggested script/email to send to Dr. Lee requesting pre-visit labs and listing the issues you want addressed?
- A printable one-page checklist you can bring to the appointment?
- Help drafting the exact wording to request a referral to Northwest Orthopedics (if you already have specific joint symptoms)?

If you confirm whether you already have diabetes and correct the INR/medication details, I’ll tailor the questions precisely and can draft a message you can send to Dr. Lee.

