- Do you have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes already? If so, what diabetes medications (if any) are you currently taking and what is your most recent A1c?
- Are you on warfarin (or another anticoagulant)? You wrote your last INR was “320” — did you mean 3.20?
- Do you take any blood‑pressure medications (you wrote “hypertension (omeprazole 20mg daily)” — omeprazole is for reflux, not BP) and do you track home BP readings?
- Are you female and post‑menopausal (relevant for DEXA timing)?

Once you confirm, I can customize further. Meanwhile, here is a general, ready‑to‑use guide.

Questions about type 2 diabetes management (ask Dr. Lee)
- Do I have diabetes, and what is my current A1c goal given my age, comorbidities (hypertension, retinopathy, anemia), and life expectancy? (Rationale: goals vary; retinopathy often leads to individualized targets.)
- When was my last A1c and should we run one now? Can you review trends over the past year?
- Should we check urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio and basic kidney function (BMP or eGFR) now? (Rationale: kidney status affects drug choices.)
- Given my retinopathy, any special cautions about medications that may affect the eyes (for example some GLP‑1 agonist trials noted retinopathy signals)? Should I coordinate changes with my ophthalmologist?
- Are there medication changes you recommend (start/stop/add) — e.g., metformin, SGLT2 inhibitor, GLP‑1 receptor agonist, or others — and why? What are the benefits/risks for me specifically?
- If starting a new glucose‑lowering med, how quickly will we monitor A1c and possible side effects? (Rationale: rapid A1c drop can transiently worsen retinopathy risk.)
- Do I need refresher counseling on hypoglycemia recognition and prevention (if on insulin or sulfonylureas)?
- How often should I have foot exams, neuropathy screening, and referrals to podiatry?
- Do I need updated vaccinations (influenza, pneumococcal, hepatitis B) for diabetes?
- Should I see an endocrinologist or diabetes educator for medication optimization, especially if A1c is above target or if there are complications?
- Any recommended lifestyle/referral supports (nutritionist, diabetes education program, exercise program)?

Questions about whether you need a referral to Northwest Orthopedics
- Do you have joint pain, decreased mobility, recurrent falls, mechanical symptoms (locking or catching), or pain that limits daily function? If yes, mention specifics (which joint, how long, severity).
- Have you tried conservative care (physical therapy, NSAIDs if safe, activity modification, orthotics/braces)? If not, would PT be an option first?
- Given my age (52) and bone health supplements (calcium + vitamin D), do you recommend a DEXA scan to assess osteoporosis/osteopenia risk before considering orthopedics? (Rationale: fractures or severe osteoporosis change treatment.)
- Do I have risk factors that make surgical evaluation more likely useful (advanced radiographic osteoarthritis, persistent pain despite conservative care, instability or progressive deformity)?
- If I have chronic joint pain, would you like to get plain X‑rays first, or refer directly to orthopedics?
- Are there any red flags that would prompt urgent orthopedic referral (severe limitation, worsening neuro signs, inability to bear weight, suspected fracture or infection)?

Recommended tests and screenings to ask for at the visit
- A1c (if diabetes or to screen), fasting glucose if not sure
- CMP/BMP (electrolytes, creatinine, eGFR, liver enzymes)
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (kidney diabetic screening)
- Lipid panel
- CBC (for anemia monitoring) and iron studies if anemia etiology unclear
- TSH (if fatigue or thyroid history)
- INR recheck now if you’re on warfarin (and clarify target range)
- Urinalysis if indicated
- DEXA scan (if postmenopausal female, long-term steroids, history of fragility fracture, or other osteoporosis risk)
- Plain X‑ray of any symptomatic joint prior to orthopedics referral (as appropriate)
- Referral for dilated retinal exam (if history of retinopathy, ensure ophthalmology follow-up frequency is current)

What to bring or track before the visit
- List of all medications and supplements with doses (including omeprazole, calcium, vitamin D, OTCs)
- Recent home BP logs and any home glucose readings
- Any recent lab results you have (A1c, BMP, lipids, INR)
- Notes on joint symptoms: location, duration, activity that worsens or helps, effect on daily life
- Vision changes or new eye symptoms since last retina visit

Red flags to contact Dr. Lee or go to urgent care
- Sudden or rapid vision loss or new flashes/floaters
- Sudden severe joint swelling with fever (possible infection)
- INR out of range with bleeding or bruising (if on warfarin)
- New numbness, weakness, or inability to walk

Suggested wording you can use to message Dr. Lee or prepare for the visit
- “Hi Dr. Lee — for my upcoming annual physical: please order A1c, BMP (creatinine/eGFR), urine albumin/creatinine, CBC, lipid panel, and INR recheck. I have a history of retinopathy — please confirm schedule for dilated eye exam. Also please advise whether a DEXA would be appropriate and whether I should be referred to orthopedics for [describe joint pain if present]. Happy to provide more details. Thank you.”

