Good plan — I can help you get organized for the visit. Below is a focused checklist and suggested questions to bring to your annual physical with Dr. Jennifer Lee, plus guidance on when an orthopedics referral is appropriate. A few quick clarifying questions are at the end.

Type 2 diabetes — questions to ask your PCP
- Do I have (or am I at high risk for) type 2 diabetes based on my labs and risk factors? Should we check A1c and fasting glucose now?  
- What A1c target should we aim for given my age, hypertension, and history of retinopathy? (I know targets are individualized.)  
- If my A1c is elevated, what first-line treatment do you recommend (lifestyle, metformin, other meds)? What are the pros/cons for me given my conditions/meds?  
- Would an SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 receptor agonist be appropriate for me (cardio/renal benefits) or are there reasons to avoid them?  
- How should I monitor glucose at home (frequency, target glucose ranges)? Would a continuous glucose monitor (CGM) or structured self-monitoring be useful?  
- Could my medications (e.g., omeprazole) or other conditions affect glucose control or vitamin/B12 absorption?  
- How will we coordinate diabetes care with my eye care (retinopathy) and with other specialists?  
- Do I need diabetes education (nutrition, exercise counseling) or a referral to endocrinology for complex management?

Labs and checks to request at/around the visit
- A1c (for long-term glucose control) and fasting glucose  
- Basic metabolic panel (creatinine/eGFR, electrolytes) and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (diabetic kidney screening)  
- Lipid panel (cardiovascular risk)  
- CBC and iron studies / B12 / folate if you’re being treated for anemia (to assess the cause and response)  
- Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) if clinically warranted  
- Medication review (including OTC supplements) — omeprazole can affect B12 absorption over time  
- Retinopathy follow-up: confirm date of last retinal specialist / eye exam and whether ophthalmology follow-up is needed more often

Specific items related to your history of retinopathy
- Because you have retinopathy, ask if tighter glucose control is appropriate or if changes should be gradual to avoid worsening retinopathy.  
- Confirm you’re on a retina/ophthalmology follow-up schedule (yearly or as recommended).

When to consider a referral to orthopedics (Northwest Orthopedics)
- Consider asking for a referral if you have any of the following:  
  - Persistent joint or bone pain that limits daily activities or sleep  
  - Progressive loss of function or mobility despite conservative care (NSAIDs, activity modification, PT)  
  - Recurrent falls, instability, or mechanical symptoms (locking, catching)  
  - Suspected serious structural problems (possible fracture, advanced osteoarthritis) or neurological signs (numbness, weakness)  
- Before referral, PCP may order imaging (X-ray or MRI) or try a trial of conservative treatment (physical therapy, injections, analgesics). If conservative measures fail, orthopedics is appropriate.  
- Because you’re 52, also ask about bone health: do you need a DEXA scan (earlier than usual if you have risk factors) and osteoporosis prevention/treatment? (You’re on calcium + vitamin D — good to confirm dosing.)

Practical phrasing you can use with Dr. Lee
- “Can we check an A1c and urine albumin-creatinine ratio today to screen for diabetes and kidney involvement?”  
- “Given my history of retinopathy and hypertension, what A1c target do you recommend for me?”  
- “If my A1c is above target, what medication(s) would you consider and why?”  
- “I have some joint/orthopedic discomfort (describe location, severity, duration). Should I try PT/imaging first, or would you refer me to Northwest Orthopedics?”  
- “Would you like me to get any labs or imaging before the appointment?”

Important clarifications I need from you
- You said your last INR was “320.” Did you mean INR = 3.20, or was that a different lab (e.g., platelet count 320)? Are you on warfarin or another anticoagulant? (This affects testing and medication choices.)  
- Do you already have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes, or are you asking because of risk factors or symptoms?  
- Any current joint symptoms (where, how long, how limiting)? That will change the orthopedics recommendation.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short email or message to Dr. Lee requesting the A1c and other labs and asking whether a referral to Northwest Orthopedics is appropriate (I can prepare that and send/draft it for you), or  
- Make a printable checklist you can take to the appointment?

Tell me the answers to the clarification questions and which of the two options above you’d like — I’ll draft the message or checklist for you.

