I can help — this is a good set of items to prepare before your annual physical. Before I lay out suggested questions and tests, I noticed a few things that look like they might be mixed up or need clarification so my recommendations are accurate:

Quick clarifying questions
- Are you being treated for hypertension with any blood pressure medicines? (You wrote “omeprazole 20 mg daily” — omeprazole is a PPI for reflux, not a blood‑pressure med. Is omeprazole one of your current meds?)
- Are you taking iron, B12, folate or any other meds/supplements specifically for anemia? You mentioned “calcium and vitamin D” (those aren’t anemia treatments), so I want to make sure I understand.
- Is your INR actually 320 or did you mean 3.20? Are you on warfarin or another anticoagulant?
- Do you have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes currently? If so, what was your last A1c and when was it done?
- Any current joint pain, stiffness, loss of function, falls, or prior orthopedic diagnoses/imaging that make you worry about needing an orthopedic referral?

When you confirm those, I can tailor things further. Meanwhile below is a practical, ready-to-use list of questions and requests to bring to your PCP visit and guidance about when an orthopedic referral (like to Northwest Orthopedics) is reasonable.

What to bring to the visit
- Up‑to‑date list of medications (dose/frequency), supplements, and any recent labs or imaging
- Last A1c, fasting glucose, lipid panel, creatinine/eGFR, urine albumin if available
- Date of last dilated eye exam (important with history of retinopathy)
- Notes about joint symptoms (which joint, onset, what makes it better/worse, effect on activities)
- Any recent falls or mobility changes

Suggested questions to ask your PCP about diabetes management
- Do I currently meet criteria for type 2 diabetes (or prediabetes)? Can we review my most recent A1c and glucose results?
- What should my individualized A1c goal be given my age, hypertension, retinopathy, and other conditions?
- Do I need any medication changes (start metformin, add GLP‑1 or SGLT2, start insulin, etc.) to reach that goal and lower my cardiovascular risk?
- Given my history of retinopathy, how often should I have dilated eye exams and do I need a referral to ophthalmology now?
- Should we screen for diabetic kidney disease now (urine albumin‑to‑creatinine ratio and creatinine/eGFR)? How often?
- Can we review my blood pressure and lipid targets and whether I need statin or BP med adjustments for cardiovascular protection?
- Do I need screening for diabetic peripheral neuropathy or a foot exam? Any need for podiatry referral?
- Do I need vaccinations (influenza, pneumococcal, Tdap, shingles) updated?
- Do you recommend home glucose monitoring? If so, frequency and targets?
- If glucose control is not optimal, what are the next steps (medication intensification, referral to endocrinology, diabetes education)?

Diabetes-related tests and checks to request at/around your annual
- A1c (or fasting glucose if A1c not available)
- Fasting lipid panel
- Basic metabolic panel (electrolytes, creatinine, eGFR)
- Urine albumin‑to‑creatinine ratio (spot)
- CBC (you mentioned anemia) plus iron studies / ferritin, B12, folate if anemia is suspected
- TSH if clinically indicated
- Vaccination review
- Referral for dilated retinal exam if not done in past year

Notes about INR/anticoagulation
- If your INR is actually 3.20 and you’re on warfarin, confirm your target INR and monitoring schedule with your PCP (and note any bleeding or clotting events).
- If you’re not on warfarin, please confirm what the “INR 320” figure refers to.

When to consider an orthopedic referral (Northwest Orthopedics)
Consider asking for a referral if any of the following apply:
- Persistent joint pain or stiffness that limits daily activities (walking, stairs, work) despite several weeks/months of conservative care (rest, NSAIDs if safe, physical therapy, weight management)
- Mechanical symptoms such as locking, catching, or instability of a joint
- Severe pain at rest or night pain that interferes with sleep
- Progressive loss of function or significant mobility decline
- X‑ray or other imaging showing advanced osteoarthritis or structural damage that may be surgically treatable
- Recurrent falls or deformity that affects gait
- If you have diabetes and foot/ankle problems (ulcers, Charcot changes) — consider podiatry or ortho foot & ankle sooner

When NOT to immediately refer
- Mild, intermittent joint pain that improves with activity modification, short course of PT, or simple analgesics — try conservative care first (PT, home exercises, weight loss, steroid injections if appropriate).
- If symptoms are primarily muscular or related to generalized deconditioning, start PT and re‑evaluate.

Questions to ask an orthopedist if you get a referral
- What is the likely cause of my symptoms and what imaging/tests do you recommend?
- Can conservative treatments (PT, injections, bracing) be effective and how long should I try them before considering surgery?
- If surgery is a reasonable option, what are the expected outcomes, risks, recovery time, and alternatives?
- How will my diabetes or retinopathy affect surgical risk and recovery (wound healing, infection risk)?
- Do I need preoperative optimization (blood sugar control, cardiac clearance, bone health optimization)?

Practical next steps I can help with
- If you want, I can draft a short message/email to Dr. Jennifer Lee summarizing your concerns and asking for specific labs (A1c, urine albumin, CBC, iron studies, BMP, lipids), confirmation of INR and anticoagulation plan, and a referral to ophthalmology (for retinopathy) and/or orthopedics if you report joint symptoms.
- Or I can produce a one‑page checklist you can print and bring to your visit with the key questions above.

Would you like me to:
1) Draft an email to Dr. Lee now requesting those labs and referrals (if so, tell me if you want to request an ortho referral now or only if PCP advises)?  
2) Make a concise printable checklist for your appointment?  
3) Or first confirm the clarifying details (meds, INR, A1c, joint symptoms) so I can tailor recommendations?

Tell me which and provide the clarifications above and I’ll prepare the next step.

