I can help — this is a good set of issues to prepare for your annual physical. Before I give tailored questions and a checklist, two quick clarifying items so I don’t miss anything:

- Do you have an established diagnosis of type 2 diabetes? If so, what was your most recent A1c and fasting glucose (and when)?  
- You wrote your last INR was “320.” Do you mean 3.20 (or 2.0 or 1.3)? If you’re on warfarin or another anticoagulant, I’ll include appropriate questions about monitoring.  
- When you say “retinopathy,” is that diabetic retinopathy? Do you already see an ophthalmologist regularly?

If you want, I can draft an email or a short script for your visit to Dr. Jennifer Lee. Below are focused questions and a checklist you can use at the visit.

Questions about type 2 diabetes management
- Current control and monitoring
  - What is my most recent A1c and fasting glucose? How often should we repeat A1c?  
  - What is my individualized A1c target given my age (52), other conditions (hypertension, retinopathy), and lifestyle?
  - Should I be doing home blood glucose monitoring (frequency, target ranges, and what to do for high/low readings)?

- Medications and adjustment
  - Are my current diabetes medications optimal for my cardiovascular and kidney risk? (Ask about benefits/risks of metformin, SGLT2 inhibitors, GLP-1 receptor agonists, insulin.)  
  - Given my hypertension and other meds, are there drug interactions or side effect risks to watch for?  
  - If weight management or cardiovascular risk is a priority, would you consider/ recommend GLP-1 or SGLT2-class drugs?

- Complications screening and prevention
  - I have a history of retinopathy — do I need a referral for a dilated eye exam now (if you don’t already see ophthalmology annually)?  
  - Please check urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio and eGFR annually (kidney function and albuminuria).  
  - Do I need a baseline ECG, lipid panel, or cardiology evaluation given my age and hypertension? Any changes to my statin or BP plan?

- Cardiovascular risk & immunizations
  - What is my current 10-year cardiovascular risk and do my diabetes and hypertension change management?  
  - Are my vaccinations up to date (influenza, COVID booster if due, pneumococcal if indicated, shingles)?

- Lifestyle and other supports
  - Any recommended nutrition counseling, diabetes education classes, or referral to endocrinology/diabetes educator?
  - If weight loss is a goal, what resources or medication options do you recommend?

Questions and considerations about referral to Northwest Orthopedics
You don’t need an automatic referral just because of age — it depends on symptoms and functional limitations. Ask Dr. Lee these items:

- Symptoms-based triggers
  - I have (describe any joint pain, decreased mobility, persistent knee/hip/back pain, difficulty with activities, recent falls). Given this, do you recommend orthopedic evaluation?
  - Are symptoms progressive or limiting daily activities or sleep?

- Conservative care first
  - Before referral, can we try conservative measures (physical therapy, targeted injections, NSAIDs or topical therapies, weight management)? How long should we try those before referring?
  - Would you recommend imaging first (X-ray/MRI) and, if so, which study and region?

- Risk / bone health evaluation
  - At age 52, should I get a DEXA scan now given my use of calcium/vitamin D and any other risk factors (family history, early menopause, smoking, steroid use)?  
  - If I’m at risk for osteoporosis, should I see endocrinology or orthopedics, or start treatment?

- Surgical thresholds and alternatives
  - If imaging shows osteoarthritis or structural issues, what are non-surgical options and when should I consider orthopedic referral for possible procedures?

Annual physical checklist and recommended tests to bring up
- Labs/tests to request (or bring recent results)
  - A1c and fasting glucose (or discuss home monitoring records)  
  - Basic metabolic panel (BMP) including eGFR, electrolytes  
  - Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (microalbumin)  
  - Lipid panel  
  - CBC (you mentioned anemia) and iron studies if needed  
  - Liver function tests if on diabetes meds that require monitoring  
  - INR check and review of anticoagulation plan if you are taking warfarin (clarify target INR)  
  - TSH if clinically indicated

- Screenings & referrals
  - Dilated retinal exam (if retinopathy history — typically every 6–12 months)  
  - Foot exam and neuropathy screening  
  - DEXA scan discussion if osteoporosis risk factors present  
  - Consider endocrinology referral if A1c is above target despite therapy or if you want medication optimization  
  - Consider orthopedics referral only if you have persistent, limiting musculoskeletal symptoms or imaging abnormalities

- Vitals and routine preventive care
  - Blood pressure check, BMI, waist circumference  
  - Smoking/alcohol screening, fall risk assessment  
  - Age-appropriate cancer screenings (mammogram, cervical, colorectal as appropriate), PSA discussion if relevant  
  - Vaccination review (flu, Tdap if due, shingles, pneumococcal, COVID booster)

Suggested phrasing you can use with Dr. Lee
- “I’d like to review my diabetes control and current A1c; what is an appropriate A1c target for me considering my hypertension and retinopathy?”  
- “Can we screen my kidney function and urine microalbumin today and review whether an SGLT2 or GLP-1 would be beneficial?”  
- “I have a history of retinopathy — do you want to refer me for a dilated eye exam now or check my ophthalmology follow-up schedule?”  
- “I sometimes have (describe joint pain / stiffness / falling / difficulty with stairs). Should I try PT and imaging first, or would you prefer a direct referral to Northwest Orthopedics?”  
- “My last INR was listed as [please confirm value]. If I’m on warfarin, what target INR should we be maintaining and how frequently should it be checked?”

Next steps I can help with
- Clarify the INR and whether you have a diabetes diagnosis (reply with those details).  
- Draft a short email to Dr. Lee or a concise talking-point list you can bring to the appointment.  
- Create a personalized lab/visit checklist you can print or save to your phone.

