I can help — this is a great set of things to prepare before your annual physical. A few quick clarifying questions first so my suggestions are accurate:
- Do you have type 2 diabetes already, or are you asking about diabetes prevention/concerned about developing it?  
- Could you confirm your current medication list (you wrote “hypertension (omeprazole 20 mg daily)” and “anemia (calcium and vitamin D)” — omeprazole is for reflux and calcium/vitamin D treat bone health, not standard treatments for hypertension or anemia).  
- Did you mean your last INR was 3.20 (not 320)? Are you on a blood thinner such as warfarin?

Once you confirm, I can tailor things further. Meanwhile, here’s a practical checklist and suggested questions to bring to Dr. Jennifer Lee at Harbor General for your annual physical focused on type 2 diabetes management and whether you should see Northwest Orthopedics.

Pre-visit checklist (bring/ask your PCP to order)
- Updated medication list (all prescription and OTC meds, supplements). Ask the PCP to review interactions.  
- Recent labs to have available or ordered before the visit:
  - A1c (to assess average glucose control)  
  - Fasting glucose (if relevant)  
  - Basic metabolic panel (electrolytes, kidney function)  
  - Lipid panel (cardiac risk)  
  - CBC (for anemia)  
  - Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (kidney damage screening in diabetes/hypertension)  
  - INR recheck (if you are on warfarin — clarify the 320 value)  
  - TSH (if clinically indicated)  
  - Vitamin D and calcium levels if you are supplementing and for bone health  
  - Consider DEXA scan (bone density) if you are 50+ with risk factors
- Recent ophthalmology notes (you said you have retinopathy — get most recent retinal exam report)
- Any imaging or notes about joint pain (X-rays, MRIs) if you already have them

Questions to ask about type 2 diabetes management
- Do I currently have diabetes or prediabetes based on my most recent A1c/glucose? (If unknown, request A1c.)  
- What is my A1c target given my age, other conditions (hypertension, retinopathy, anemia), and overall health? (Targets are individualized — e.g., <7% for many, but higher targets may be appropriate if hypoglycemia risk or comorbidities.)  
- Do I need a change in medication? If I have diabetes: should I start or intensify metformin, or consider newer agents (SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 receptor agonists) for heart and kidney protection? (These are considered especially if cardiovascular or kidney disease risk exists.)  
- How often should I check A1c and blood glucose at home? When should I do fingerstick checks vs. A1c-only monitoring?  
- What is my cardiovascular risk and do I need a statin or aspirin for prevention?  
- Do I need kidney-protective monitoring or medications (urine albumin, eGFR, consideration of SGLT2 if albuminuria or CKD)?  
- Given my history of retinopathy, do I need an urgent ophthalmology retina follow-up? How often should retinal screening occur?  
- Any specific diet, exercise, or weight-management referrals (nutritionist/diabetes educator)?  
- Vaccinations to update (flu, pneumococcal, hepatitis B if indicated, COVID boosters).  
- How do my other conditions (hypertension, anemia) affect diabetes targets and medication choices?

Why each matters (brief)
- A1c + urine albumin + kidney function = overall diabetes control and organ protection.  
- SGLT2/GLP-1 drugs can reduce heart and kidney events in many patients; suitability depends on kidney function, side effects, cost/coverage.  
- Retinopathy requires at least annual ophthalmology follow-up; tighter glucose and BP control slow progression.

Questions and criteria about referral to Northwest Orthopedics
- Do I need an ortho referral now? Ask: “Given my age (52), activity level, and any joint pain or functional limitations, would you recommend a referral to orthopedics or try conservative therapy first?”  
- Key reasons to refer to orthopedics:
  - Persistent joint pain limiting daily function despite conservative care (PT, meds) for 6–12 weeks  
  - Mechanical symptoms: catching, locking, giving way, or instability  
  - Significant imaging findings (advanced osteoarthritis, fracture, large meniscal tear)  
  - Progressive deformity or neurological deficits (weakness, numbness)  
  - Consideration of surgical options if conservative measures fail
- Conservative measures to try first (often by PCP or physiatry):
  - Physical therapy focused on strengthening and mobility  
  - Weight loss and activity modification  
  - NSAIDs or topical analgesics (if safe with your meds)  
  - Intra-articular steroid or hyaluronic acid injections when appropriate  
  - Orthotics, bracing, or assistive devices
- Other relevant assessments before referral:
  - Recent X-rays or MRI if symptoms suggest structural disease  
  - Bone density (DEXA) given age and your calcium/vitamin D use  
  - Fall risk and functional assessment

Other items to raise with Dr. Lee
- Review blood pressure targets and current antihypertensive regimen (omeprazole does not treat hypertension — confirm your BP meds).  
- Clarify anemia cause and workup (CBC, iron studies, B12, folate) — calcium/vitamin D won’t treat anemia if iron deficiency or chronic disease is the cause.  
- Medication safety: if you’re on anticoagulation (INR), that affects decisions about injections, procedures, or surgery referrals.  
- Coordinate care for retinopathy — request ophthalmology note and ensure timely follow-up.

Suggested short script to use in the visit or message to Dr. Lee
- “Hi Dr. Lee — I’m preparing for my annual physical. Can you please review my A1c and order A1c, BMP, lipid panel, CBC, urine albumin/creatinine, and INR (confirm if I’m on anticoagulation)? I have a history of retinopathy — should I get an updated ophthalmology referral? Also I have some joint concerns — do you recommend trialing PT and conservative care first, or should I have an orthopedics referral to Northwest Orthopedics now? Finally, please review my current med list for interactions (I take omeprazole 20 mg daily, calcium and vitamin D). Thanks.”

Next steps I can help with
- Clarify your meds/INR and whether you have diabetes now.  
- Create a concise message or email to Dr. Jennifer Lee requesting the labs and asking about an ortho referral (I can draft and send it if you want).  
- Make a one-page visit checklist you can bring to the appointment.

Would you like me to draft that message/email to Dr. Lee (and include the exact lab orders and referral request)? If so, confirm your current medication list and whether you meant INR 3.20.

