While you answer, here are practical items and question prompts you can use at your annual physical with Dr. Jennifer Lee (Harbor General).

What to bring / prepare
- Updated medication list (dose, frequency), including OTCs, supplements (calcium/vitamin D), and anticoagulants.
- Recent home BP logs, weight changes, and recent lab results (A1c, BMP, lipids, INR).
- Short list of symptoms (joint pain, mobility limits, vision or neuropathy symptoms, dizziness, falls).
- Any relevant previous imaging or specialist notes (retina/ophthalmology, prior orthopedics).

Diabetes-related topics and questions to ask
- Confirm diagnosis and targets
  - “What should my A1c target be given my age, retinopathy, and other conditions?”
- Current control and testing
  - “Can we check a current A1c and fasting glucose today?”
  - “Do I need more frequent glucose monitoring or a continuous glucose monitor (CGM)?”
- Medication review and optimization
  - “Given my cardiovascular and kidney risk, should I start or switch to any of the newer agents (SGLT2 inhibitor, GLP‑1 agonist)?”
  - “Are there medications I should avoid because of interactions (e.g., with my anticoagulant or omeprazole)?”
- Complication screening/prevention
  - “When was my last retinal specialist visit? Should I schedule a diabetic eye exam now given my history of retinopathy?”
  - “Can we check urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio and kidney function (creatinine/eGFR) today?”
  - “Please check a foot exam for neuropathy, and advise about neuropathy screening (monofilament) and foot care.”
- Cardiovascular risk
  - “Do I need adjustments to my statin or blood pressure plan for CV risk reduction?”
- Hypoglycemia and safety
  - “How should we balance tighter control vs. hypoglycemia risk for someone my age?”
- Referrals
  - “Would you recommend an endocrinology referral for medication optimization or complex glucose management?”

Tests to consider ordering at the visit
- A1c, fasting glucose (or CGM data if available)
- Basic metabolic panel (electrolytes, creatinine, eGFR)
- Lipid panel
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio
- Urinalysis (if indicated)
- CBC (since you mentioned anemia) — and clarify what’s being used for treatment
- INR or coagulation panel if on warfarin (and confirm correct recent value)
- Consider DEXA scan for bone density (age 52 + vitamins) if not done recently

Orthopedics referral: when it’s reasonable and what to ask
Ask for an orthopedics referral (Northwest Orthopedics or similar) if any of the following apply:
- Persistent joint pain limiting daily activities despite several months of conservative care (PT, NSAIDs, activity modification).
- Mechanical symptoms (locking, catching, instability) or deformity.
- Progressive loss of function or mobility affecting independence.
- Recurrent falls, suspected fracture, or imaging that shows advanced osteoarthritis.
- Pain severe enough that you’re considering procedural options (joint injections or surgery).

Questions to ask your PCP about orthopedics
- “Given my age and risk factors, do you think I should see orthopedics for my knee/hip/back pain (or specify joint)?”
- “Should we get X‑rays or MRI first, or would you prefer I see orthopedics before imaging?”
- “Can we try a course of physical therapy or injections first? If so, what’s reasonable before referring?”
- “Do I need a DEXA or other bone health workup before any orthopedic procedures, given my vitamin D/calcium use and retinopathy (fall risk)?”

Other items to cover at the annual visit
- Vaccinations (influenza, shingles if eligible, pneumococcal per guidelines, COVID booster if due).
- Blood pressure control plan and home monitoring goals.
- Medication reconciliation — confirm omeprazole, any antihypertensives, anticoagulants, iron or anemia therapy.
- Vision follow-up: yearly retinal exams if diabetic retinopathy history.
- Fall risk assessment and home-safety counseling if vision or balance issues.

