I can help — this is a good set of topics to prepare before your annual. A few quick clarifying questions first so my suggestions are accurate:

- Do you have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes already? If so, what are your current diabetes meds and your most recent A1c (and date)?
- Are you on anticoagulation (warfarin)? You wrote “last INR was 320” — did you mean 3.20?
- You listed “hypertension (omeprazole 20 mg daily)” — omeprazole is for reflux; can you confirm your blood‑pressure meds?
- For anemia you wrote “calcium and vitamin D” — are you actually taking iron, B12, or folate for anemia, or are calcium/vit D for bone health?
- Has your retinopathy been attributed to diabetes? When was your last ophthalmology/retina visit and eye exam?

With those clarified, here are focused things to discuss with Dr. Jennifer Lee and questions to bring to your visit about diabetes management and possible orthopedic referral.

Questions about type 2 diabetes management
- Status and targets
  - “What is my current A1c and what A1c target should we aim for given my age, comorbidities, and risk of hypoglycemia?”
  - “What is my blood pressure target given diabetes and hypertension?”
- Testing and monitoring
  - Request A1c, fasting glucose (if helpful), and review of any home glucose logs. Ask about continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) if you have hypoglycemia or variable readings.
  - Kidney monitoring: serum creatinine/eGFR and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR).
  - Lipids: fasting lipid panel for cardiovascular risk.
  - Routine labs: CMP, CBC (for anemia), LFTs (if starting/changing meds).
- Medication review and options
  - “Should I be on metformin or other first-line therapy (if not already)?”
  - “Would an SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP‑1 receptor agonist be appropriate for me given my cardiovascular or renal risk?” (SGLT2 and GLP‑1 agents are considered when ASCVD/CKD or for weight benefit.)
  - “If I’m already on medications, should we adjust doses or change therapy based on my A1c/side effects/weight?”
  - “Are there interactions between my current meds (including any anticoagulant) and diabetes drugs?”
- Complications and prevention
  - Retina: “I have a history of retinopathy — do I need a referral to ophthalmology/retina or an updated dilated eye exam this year?”
  - Neuropathy: request foot exam and documentation of sensation, monofilament testing, and foot care counseling.
  - Cardiovascular risk: ask about aspirin, statin therapy, and whether your BP meds (ACE/ARB) are optimized for kidney protection.
- Lifestyle and vaccinations
  - Discuss diet, exercise, weight‑loss options, and referral to diabetes education if desired.
  - Vaccinations: influenza, pneumococcal, shingles (age‑appropriate), and COVID boosters.

When you might ask for an endocrinology referral
- A1c is hard to control despite lifestyle + two or more oral agents
- Consideration of GLP‑1/insulin initiation and you want specialist input
- Complex comorbidities (advanced CKD, recurrent hypoglycemia, or rapid progression of complications)
- Unclear cause of hypoglycemia or large glucose variability

Labs and checks to consider ordering at the annual
- A1c
- CMP (electrolytes, creatinine)
- CBC (for anemia)
- Lipid panel
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio
- TSH, B12/iron studies if relevant to anemia or neuropathy
- INR (if you are on warfarin)
- EKG if new cardiac symptoms or as your PCP recommends

Retinopathy-specific
- If you have diabetic retinopathy: confirm when your last dilated eye exam was. Annual dilated retinal exam is usually recommended; urgent referral to retina if vision changes, macular edema, or proliferative changes.

Thinking through a referral to Northwest Orthopedics
- You do not need an ortho referral just because of age. Consider referral if any of these apply:
  - Chronic joint pain (hip/knee/shoulder) that limits daily activities or sleep and has persisted despite 6–12 weeks of conservative care (NSAIDs as tolerated, physical therapy, weight loss, activity modification).
  - Recurrent falls, instability, or progressive loss of function.
  - Imaging (X-ray, MRI) that shows moderate–severe osteoarthritis, meniscal tears, rotator cuff tears that may be surgical.
  - Structural problems or deformities, or inability to do physical therapy due to pain.
- Before referral, typical steps: focused history/exam, plain X‑rays of the painful joint, trial of physical therapy, NSAIDs or injections if appropriate.
- If referred, useful questions to ask Northwest Orthopedics:
  - “Based on my imaging and symptoms, what are the non-surgical and surgical options?”
  - “What are expected functional outcomes and recovery time for each option?”
  - “How would my anticoagulation (if on warfarin) or high INR be managed around procedures?”
  - “Is physical therapy or steroid injection a reasonable next step before considering surgery?”

Peri‑procedural considerations (if surgery is discussed)
- If you are on warfarin with INR ~3.2, that will need peri‑op planning (bridging or holding warfarin) — ask both PCP and the surgical team how to manage anticoagulation.
- Optimize diabetes control and BP before elective procedures to reduce complications.

Suggested agenda for your visit with Dr. Jennifer Lee
- Confirm and update medication list (including BP meds, anticoagulant, iron or supplements, omeprazole).
- Order labs: A1c, CMP, CBC, lipid panel, UACR, INR if on warfarin.
- Discuss diabetes control and whether to change meds or refer to endocrinology.
- Confirm eye exam status and arrange ophthalmology/retina referral if due or if vision changed.
- Discuss joint symptoms and whether to trial PT or order imaging; if warranted, ask for referral to Northwest Orthopedics.
- Discuss immunizations and preventive health (colon screening status, DEXA if indicated).
- Plan for follow-up after labs and any referrals.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a concise message/email to Dr. Jennifer Lee requesting the labs and asking about referrals (endocrinology, ophthalmology, orthopedics) and mentioning the INR and anticoagulation concerns.
- Or create a printable one‑page checklist you can bring to the appointment.

Which would you prefer? And please confirm the clarifying details above (diabetes meds/A1c, whether you’re anticoagulated and the correct INR, and your actual BP meds/anemia treatment) so I can tailor the notes and the draft message.

