Great — I can help you prepare. Below I’ll (1) list the clarifying questions and tests to bring up with Dr. Jennifer Lee about type 2 diabetes management, (2) give guidance on when a referral to Northwest Orthopedics makes sense, (3) flag a couple of important clarifying items (the INR value and current meds), and (4) offer a ready-to-use email template to send to your PCP (I can draft it for you if you want).

1) Quick clarifying points for me before anything else
- Do you have an established diagnosis of type 2 diabetes already? If so, what is your current A1c and what diabetes meds (if any) are you taking?
- Are you on any blood thinner (warfarin / coumadin or another anticoagulant)? You mentioned “last INR was 320” — do you mean INR = 3.20? (An INR of 320 would be a data error.) If you are on anticoagulation, we should include that in any orthopedic/surgical planning discussion.
- Do you have symptoms from your joints (pain, stiffness, limited function) or a history of fractures or prior joint surgery?
- Do you currently see an eye specialist for retinopathy, and when was your last retinal exam?

2) Questions and topics to bring up about type 2 diabetes (for annual physical)
- Diagnosis & targets
  - Can we confirm my diabetes diagnosis status and what my current A1c target should be given my age, retinopathy, and hypertension?
  - Please review my most recent A1c (if not available, order it today).
- Current therapy and safety
  - Review current medications that affect blood sugar (names, doses) and whether any changes are needed.
  - Are any of my other meds (like omeprazole or supplements) interacting with diabetes meds?
- Cardiovascular and BP risk
  - Given my hypertension, what is my blood pressure target? Do my BP meds need adjustment?
  - Do I need or should we intensify statin therapy or other CV prevention measures?
- Kidney and labs
  - Order or review: CMP (creatinine, eGFR), urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR).
  - Any concerns about kidney function that should change diabetes management?
- Retina & neuropathy screening
  - Given my history of retinopathy, do I need a retinal clinic appointment now? (Confirm frequency of ophthalmology visits.)
  - Perform neuropathy screening (monofilament / foot exam) and ask about any numbness or neuropathic symptoms.
- Vaccinations and general health
  - Are my vaccinations up to date (flu, pneumococcal, shingles if eligible)?
- Lifestyle and monitoring
  - Discuss home glucose monitoring plan (if appropriate), weight management, diet, and exercise recommendations tailored to your other conditions.
- Labs to order at this visit
  - HbA1c, fasting glucose (if needed)
  - CMP, lipid panel, UACR
  - CBC if anemia is being followed (and iron studies if not already done)
  - Consider ECG if indicated by symptoms or cardiovascular risk
- Referral considerations
  - Ask whether referral to endocrinology is recommended if A1c is above the goal or if management is complex (multiple meds or hypoglycemia risk).

3) How to decide about an orthopedic referral (Northwest Orthopedics)
When a referral is reasonable
- Persistent joint pain or stiffness limiting activities despite conservative care (NSAIDs, PT, activity modification).
- Reduced mobility or functional decline (trouble walking, climbing stairs, dressing).
- Recurrent falls or instability, mechanical symptoms (catching, locking).
- Imaging (x-ray/MRI) shows significant degenerative changes correlating with symptoms.
- You’re considering joint injections or surgery OR conservative measures have failed.
Additional considerations given your history
- If you are on anticoagulation (INR concern), that changes peri-procedure planning — inform the orthopedist and coordinate with your PCP/anticoagulation clinic.
- Diabetes and retinopathy by themselves don’t automatically require ortho referral, but diabetes can slow healing and increase infection risk after surgery — useful to bring up during the referral so the orthopedist can consider medical optimization first.
Practical questions to ask PCP before referring
- Do my symptoms warrant x-rays or other imaging first? Can you order imaging and physical therapy trial before we refer?
- If a referral is recommended, can you refer me to Northwest Orthopedics and note any special considerations (anticoagulation, diabetes, anemia)?

4) Things to bring / have on hand for your appointment
- A complete list of current medications (prescription, OTC, supplements — include dosages).
- Recent home BP/glucose logs if you keep them.
- Last labs (A1c, lipid panel, kidney function) or have PCP order them before/at the visit.
- A short symptom summary: any joint pain locations, duration, severity, mobility limits, falls.
- Confirmation of anticoagulation status and last INR value (and date).

5) Suggested questions to ask Dr. Lee, phrased simply
- “Can we review my A1c and set a target that’s safe for me given my retinopathy and hypertension?”
- “Do you recommend any medication changes for diabetes right now?”
- “Please order A1c, CMP, lipid panel, and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio today if they’re not recent.”
