I can help — below I’ve put together (1) clarifying questions for you so I can tailor this further, (2) a focused checklist and specific questions to bring to your annual physical about type 2 diabetes management, and (3) guidance on when to ask for a referral to Northwest Orthopedics (and what to request first). If you’d like, I can also draft an email to Dr. Jennifer Lee at Harbor General or a one-page visit agenda you can print and bring.

First — a couple of clarifying points
- Do you have an established diagnosis of type 2 diabetes? (You referred to diabetes management, but didn’t state current A1c or meds.)
- Please confirm current medications (I noticed you wrote “hypertension (omeprazole 20 mg daily)” — omeprazole is a PPI, not an antihypertensive). What blood‑pressure medicine(s) do you take?
- You wrote “anemia (calcium and vitamin D)” — calcium/vitamin D are usually for bone health, not anemia. Do you take iron or any other meds for anemia?
- Your last INR listed as “320” — did you mean 3.20? Are you on warfarin or another anticoagulant?

Answering those will let me make the list more specific. Meanwhile, here’s a practical checklist and wording you can use at your visit.

1) Questions to ask about type 2 diabetes management
- Current control and targets
  - “What is my most recent A1c and what target A1c should I aim for given my age and other conditions (hypertension, retinopathy)?”
  - “How often should we re-check A1c and at-home glucose monitoring strategy (frequency/times)?”
- Medications
  - “Are my current diabetes medications still the best option? Should I consider adding or switching to an SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP‑1 receptor agonist for heart/kidney protection or weight benefit?”
  - “Do any diabetes medicines need dose-adjustment given my kidney function (eGFR/creatinine)?”
  - “Am I at risk for hypoglycemia with my regimen? How should I manage or prevent it?”
- Screening for complications
  - “Given my retinopathy history, has my retinal exam been done this year? Do I need referral to ophthalmology (retina specialist) or more frequent eye exams?”
  - “Please order urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio and serum creatinine/eGFR if not done recently to screen for diabetic kidney disease.”
  - “Can you document a foot exam today and advise on neuropathy screening? Should I get monofilament testing or referral to podiatry if numbness/pain?”
- Cardiovascular risk & prevention
  - “What are my blood pressure and lipid targets given diabetes and HTN? Should I start or continue a high-intensity statin?”
  - “Do I need aspirin for primary prevention based on my cardiovascular risk?”
- Vaccines, lifestyle, and education
  - “Am I up to date on influenza, pneumococcal, and COVID vaccines?”
  - “Would you recommend diabetes self-management education or referral to a nutritionist / diabetes educator?”
- Technology and monitoring
  - “Would continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) or a structured glucose meter schedule help me?”
- Practical follow-ups
  - “How often should I follow up for medication adjustments, and when should I see an endocrinologist?”

2) Tests to request or bring recent results for the annual visit
- A1c (if not recent)
- Fasting lipid panel
- Serum creatinine and eGFR
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (microalbumin)
- CBC (since you noted anemia)
- INR and list of anticoagulants (if applicable)
- Recent blood pressure logs (home readings)
- Eye exam report (retina) or referral if no recent exam
- Foot exam documentation and neuropathy screening
- Vaccination records

3) When to ask for a referral to Northwest Orthopedics
Consider asking for an orthopedic referral if any of the following apply:
- Persistent joint pain limiting daily activities or sleep despite conservative care (NSAIDs as tolerated, rest, icing, activity modification) for several weeks/months
- Functional decline: difficulty climbing stairs, rising from a chair, walking short distances, or inability to perform usual activities
- Mechanical symptoms: episodes of locking, catching, instability, frequent giving way
- Visible deformity, significant swelling, or suspected fracture
- Prior imaging showing moderate-to-severe osteoarthritis, meniscal tear, or other structural problem requiring surgical consideration
- Failed conservative therapy including physical therapy, weight management, bracing, and injections (if appropriate)

What to request before or with a referral
- Ask your PCP to order targeted imaging first (usually X‑ray of the problematic joint; MRI only if indicated) so the ortho referral includes imaging.
- If you are on anticoagulation with an elevated INR (e.g., 3.2), tell both PCP and ortho — that affects surgical planning and some procedures.
- If bone health is a concern (you’re taking calcium & vitamin D), request a DEXA scan (bone density) — helps assess fracture risk and guides orthopedics and bone‑health treatment.
- If weight or mobility are concerns, ask for a referral to physical therapy before surgery referrals — PT often helps avoid or delay surgery.
- If you have neuropathy or poor wound healing risk (diabetes, retinopathy), mention that — it affects postoperative risk and healing plans.

4) How to phrase a referral request to Dr. Lee (example)
- For diabetes: “Could you evaluate whether my diabetes medications should be adjusted and whether I’d benefit from an endocrinology referral or diabetes education? Please order A1c, urine microalbumin, CMP (kidney function), lipid panel, and schedule a retinal exam if not done in the last year.”
- For orthopedics: “I’ve had persistent [knee/hip/shoulder/low back] pain for [duration] that limits [activities]. Could you order an X‑ray and consider referral to Northwest Orthopedics if imaging or exam suggests structural disease? Also please check DEXA for bone health and confirm my INR/anticoagulation status before any procedures.”

5) Safety and coordination points to raise
- Confirm anticoagulation status and recent INR (if on warfarin) — important for procedures and some injections.
- Mention history of retinopathy so PCP ensures frequent ophthalmology follow-up.
- Ask about renal function before any new diabetes medications (SGLT2s require eGFR checks).
- Bring a full medication list including supplements (calcium, vitamin D, PPIs).

If you’d like next steps
- I can draft a concise message/email to Dr. Jennifer Lee listing these requests (A1c, urine microalbumin, lipid, creatinine, DEXA request, X‑ray order and ortho referral wording, retinal referral, and INR clarification).
- Or I can create a one-page visit agenda/checklist you can bring to the appointment.

Which would you prefer? And please confirm (or correct) the med/INR details so I can personalize the draft.

