- Do you have specific joint pain or mobility problems right now that make you wonder about an orthopedics referral?

While you confirm, here are clear, practical items to take to your appointment and suggested questions to ask Dr. Jennifer Lee (Harbor General).

Pre-visit tasks / tests to consider asking for
- Labs: A1c, fasting glucose (if appropriate), CMP (kidney and liver function), lipid panel, urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (ACR), CBC, and coagulation panel/INR confirmation if you take warfarin.  
- If you haven’t had one recently: ophthalmology retina exam (given history of retinopathy) and foot exam for neuropathy.  
- Consider a DEXA scan discussion if you have osteoporosis risk factors (age, low body weight, family history, steroids, early menopause) — you’re 52 so it may come up depending on your risk profile.  
- Bring: up-to-date medication list (including OTCs and supplements — you mentioned omeprazole, calcium, vitamin D), a copy of any recent home blood pressure or glucose logs, and any recent imaging or notes about joint problems.

Questions to ask about type 2 diabetes management
- Where do you think my A1c target should be, given my age, retinopathy, hypertension, and other conditions? (Targets are individualized — often <7% for otherwise healthy adults, but looser targets may be appropriate with complications.)  
- Based on my most recent labs and complications, should we start or change any diabetes medications? (Ask specifically about metformin, GLP-1 receptor agonists, SGLT2 inhibitors — these can have cardiovascular and kidney benefits in many patients.)  
- Do I need additional kidney testing or closer monitoring (eGFR, urine ACR) given my medications and retinopathy?  
- How often should I have dilated eye exams and foot exams? (Retinopathy typically warrants at least annual ophthalmology follow-up.)  
- Should we check for diabetic neuropathy and discuss fall risk or foot care?  
- If I monitor glucose at home, what targets and frequency do you recommend? When should I treat hypoglycemia?  
- Any medication interactions or concerns with my current meds (omeprazole, calcium, vitamin D) or with anticoagulation if I’m on it?  
- Do I need referral to diabetes education, nutrition counseling, or a diabetes specialist/endocrinologist?

Questions and decision points about an orthopedics referral (Northwest Orthopedics)
- Consider asking for a referral if any of the following apply:
  - Persistent joint pain that limits daily activities or sleep despite conservative care (PT, activity modification, analgesics) for several weeks to months.  
  - Mechanical symptoms (locking, catching), instability, or decreased range of motion.  
  - Repeated falls, progressive disability, or concern for structural problems on X-ray/MRI.  
  - Suspected fracture or rapid worsening of symptoms.  
  - If you’re considering surgery (e.g., joint replacement) as an option.  
- Before referral you can ask PCP to:
  - Order plain X-rays or initial imaging to guide whether orthopedics is needed.  
  - Trial conservative care: physical therapy, supervised exercise/weight-loss counseling, assistive devices, and topical analgesics. (Be careful with oral NSAIDs if you’re anticoagulated — discuss safety.)  
  - Refer to orthopedics sooner if conservative measures fail or imaging shows significant joint degeneration.

Other items to bring up
- Blood pressure and lipid targets in the context of diabetes and retinopathy — do any med changes make sense to reduce cardiovascular risk?  
- Vaccinations (flu, pneumococcal, shingles, Tdap) and whether any are due.  
- Bone health screening if appropriate.  
- Confirm appointments with ophthalmology and any needed lab/follow-up intervals.

Red flags to seek urgent care or contact PCP about
- Sudden vision changes, chest pain, sudden weakness/speech changes, severe hypoglycemia, signs of infection (fever with a wound), or very high/very low blood glucose readings.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short message/email to Dr. Lee summarizing these concerns and requesting specific labs/referrals (I can draft it for you to review), or  
- Make a printable checklist of the questions above you can bring to the visit?

Also please confirm the INR value and whether you have a formal diabetes diagnosis and current diabetes meds so I can tailor the questions further.

