I can help — this is a good set of issues to prepare for your annual. Before I give specific recommended questions and items to bring, two quick clarifying points so my suggestions are accurate:

- You wrote “hypertension (omeprazole 20 mg daily).” Omeprazole is a proton‑pump inhibitor (for reflux) not a blood‑pressure drug. Could you confirm your actual blood‑pressure medication(s) and doses?
- You wrote “anemia (calcium and vitamin D).” Calcium/vitamin D aren’t treatments for anemia—do you take iron, B12, or folate supplements instead? Any known cause for the anemia (iron‑deficiency, chronic disease, etc.)?
- “My last INR was 320.” Do you mean INR 3.20? And are you taking warfarin (and if so, what is the indication)?

If you confirm those three items I’ll tailor recommendations. Meanwhile, here are general, high‑value questions and actions to prepare for your visit focused on type 2 diabetes management and whether you need an orthopedics referral.

Questions to ask your PCP (Dr. Jennifer Lee) about type 2 diabetes management
- Do I currently meet criteria for type 2 diabetes, and what is my most recent A1c? (If you don’t have a recent A1c, ask them to order one.)
- What is an individualized A1c target for me given my age, hypertension, retinopathy, and any other conditions?
- Based on my kidney function (eGFR) and heart disease risk, should I start or change diabetes medications (metformin, SGLT2 inhibitor, GLP‑1 receptor agonist, etc.)? What are the pros/cons for me?
- Given my history of retinopathy, are there any diabetes medications you would avoid or monitor more closely? (Ask whether more frequent retinal follow‑up is needed if therapy changes.)
- What symptoms of hypo‑ or hyperglycemia should I watch for, and what precautions should I take with new diabetes medicines?
- Do you recommend screening tests now: urine albumin/creatinine ratio (kidney), lipid panel, baseline liver tests, and foot exam/neuropathy screening?
- Should I be referred to a diabetes educator or nutritionist for medication training, carbohydrate counting, or weight management?
- How often should we check A1c, kidney labs, and urine albumin going forward?
- If I’m on anticoagulation (warfarin) with INR ~3.2, how does that affect diabetes or ophthalmology procedures (e.g., eye injections, retinal laser)? Any special precautions?

Tests and data to bring or request at the visit (diabetes focus)
- Recent labs: A1c, fasting glucose, CMP or BMP (kidney function, electrolytes), lipid panel, urine albumin/creatinine, CBC (for your anemia), LFTs.
- Recent INR value and anticoagulant name/dose (if applicable).
- Recent ophthalmology/retina note or dates of last dilated eye exam.
- Home blood glucose log (if you monitor), recent blood pressure readings, and a current medication list (names, doses).
- Vaccination status (influenza, Tdap, shingles, pneumococcal if indicated).

When to consider escalation of diabetes therapy or specialist referral
- A1c above individualized target on lifestyle alone or current meds.
- Significant kidney disease (reduced eGFR or albuminuria) — may need SGLT2 or nephrology input.
- Cardiovascular disease or high CV risk — SGLT2 / GLP‑1 can be beneficial.
- Recurrent hypoglycemia or complex regimen — endocrinology referral.

Questions to ask / things to consider about an orthopedics referral (Northwest Orthopedics)
- Do I need an ortho referral now, or should we try conservative management first (PT, NSAIDs if safe, activity modification, weight loss)?
- If I have joint pain: how severe is it (functional limitation), is it progressive, do I have instability, mechanical locking, or prior trauma?
- Would you like X‑ray or MRI before referral? (Most orthopedists want weight‑bearing X‑rays for suspected osteoarthritis.)
- Given my age (52) and diabetes/retinopathy/anticoagulation history, will my diabetes affect surgical risks or wound healing if surgery is recommended?
- If referred, what conservative options should I try first and for how long before considering surgery?
- If bone health is a concern, should I have a DEXA scan (bone density) now?

Indications that make an orthopedics referral more likely appropriate
- Pain that significantly limits daily activities or work despite reasonable conservative care.
- Mechanical symptoms (locking/catching), persistent instability, deformity, or suspected acute injury.
- Imaging showing advanced joint degeneration on X‑ray and progressive function loss.
- Failure of an appropriate trial of physical therapy and medications.

Other items to address at your annual
- Eye care follow‑up: with existing retinopathy you’ll likely need a retinal specialist / ophthalmology follow‑up schedule (often at least annually, more frequently if active disease).
- Foot exam and neuropathy screening.
- Vaccinations and cancer screening appropriate for age/sex.
- Review of anemia: get CBC plus iron studies/folate/B12 as indicated and clarify your supplements.
- Review blood pressure control and home BP readings; update antihypertensive plan.
- Medication review for interactions (for example, PPI effects on absorption; interactions with anticoagulants).

If you want, I can:
- Draft a concise message or email to Dr. Lee listing these specific questions and requesting the lab orders (A1c, urine albumin, BMP, lipid panel, CBC/iron) and an opinion on orthopedics referral.
- Create a one‑page checklist you can bring to your appointment.
- Tailor the questions further once you confirm your current meds (BP meds, anticoagulant, iron/B12), which joint(s) — if any — are symptomatic, and whether you have a current diabetes diagnosis and recent A1c.

Which would you like me to do next, and can you confirm the medication/INR details I asked about?

