I can help you prepare—here’s a focused checklist and suggested questions to bring to your annual physical so you and Dr. Lee can cover type 2 diabetes management and whether an ortho referral is needed.

First — a couple quick clarifying questions
- Do you have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes already? If so, what is your most recent A1c (and date)?  
- Are you taking any blood-thinners (warfarin) or anticoagulants? You wrote “last INR was 320” — do you mean INR 3.20, and are you on warfarin?  
- What medications are you taking for blood pressure (names/doses)? (You listed omeprazole 20 mg, which is a PPI — not an antihypertensive.)  
- Do you have current joint pain, stiffness, decreased mobility, or falls that make you think you might need orthopedics now?

If you’d like, I can draft an email or a visit script to Dr. Lee once you confirm those details.

Recommended pre-visit labs and checks to request (so results are available at the visit)
- HbA1c (if you have diabetes or risk factors)  
- Fasting glucose (optional if not fasting)  
- CMP (electrolytes, liver, creatinine) — calculate eGFR for med safety (metformin, SGLT2i)  
- Lipid panel  
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (diabetic kidney damage screen)  
- CBC (you mentioned anemia)  
- INR check (if you’re on warfarin — confirm target range)  
- TSH (optional, sometimes part of annual labs)  
- Consider DEXA (bone density) if you have osteoporosis risk factors or are female age ≥65 (or earlier with risk factors)  
- Recent ophthalmology report for retinopathy (or request referral/annual diabetic eye exam if not current)

Diabetes-management questions to ask Dr. Lee
- Given my age (52), retinopathy history, hypertension and other risks, what A1c target do you recommend for me? (Ask how that target balances preventing complications vs. hypoglycemia risk.)  
- What is my most recent A1c and trend over the last year? How often should we check it?  
- Should I start or change any glucose-lowering medications? (If you’re not currently on diabetes meds, ask whether metformin is appropriate; if on meds, ask about GLP‑1 receptor agonists or SGLT2 inhibitors for CV/renal benefit and whether they’re appropriate for you.)  
- What are the risks/benefits of intensifying treatment given my retinopathy and hypertension?  
- Any kidney function limits or interactions we need to consider for metformin, SGLT2 inhibitors, or other agents? (Ask to review eGFR.)  
- Should I be referred to a diabetes educator or nutritionist for medication teaching, glucose monitoring or lifestyle counseling?  
- Do I need tighter blood pressure control or change in antihypertensive class to protect my kidneys and eyes? (Ask about ACE inhibitors/ARBs if not already on one.)  
- Do I need a retinal specialist referral or more frequent eye exams because of my history of retinopathy? (Ask when the last ophthalmology visit was and whether any follow-up is urgent.)

Questions and criteria about a referral to Northwest Orthopedics (or any ortho)
- If you’re considering an ortho referral, discuss these with Dr. Lee:  
  - Describe your symptoms: location(s) of pain, duration, severity, what limits your daily activities, any swelling, instability, “catching” or locking, or night pain.  
  - Ask whether initial conservative steps are needed/appropriate before referral: physical therapy, home exercise, weight loss, NSAIDs, joint injections, orthotics, or imaging (X-ray/MRI).  
  - Ask whether an X-ray or other imaging should be ordered before an orthopedics appointment (this speeds triage).  
  - Ask: Given my age and comorbidities (HTN, anemia, retinopathy, possible anticoagulation), would a direct ortho referral be recommended now, or only if conservative care fails?  
  - If you already have functional limitations (trouble walking, climbing stairs, work limitations) or progressive deformity, request a referral sooner.

When an orthopedics referral is more strongly indicated
- Persistent, function-limiting pain >6–8 weeks despite conservative care (PT, meds)  
- Mechanical symptoms (locking, instability) or deformity  
- Suspected significant structural damage on imaging (severe osteoarthritis, fracture, ligament tear)  
- Difficulty performing daily activities or increased fall risk

Other items to cover at the visit
- Medication reconciliation: confirm all current meds (including OTCs, supplements — calcium, vitamin D), interactions, and whether omeprazole should continue. Ask specifically about interactions with diabetic or anticoagulant meds.  
- Vaccinations (influenza, Tdap, shingles if eligible, pneumococcal if indicated)  
- Foot exam (peripheral neuropathy, pulses) and referral to podiatry if needed  
- Blood pressure goal and whether home BP monitoring is needed; bring home readings if you have them  
- Lifestyle counseling (diet, exercise) and referral to nutrition/diabetes education as needed  
- If you’re on anticoagulation, confirm bleeding risk and how any new meds (e.g., aspirin) affect INR/bleeding risk

Red flags to mention urgently
- New vision changes or sudden worsening of vision (urgent ophthalmology)  
- New or worsening chest pain, severe shortness of breath, sudden weakness, or signs of stroke (911/ER)  
- Signs of major bleeding if on anticoagulation (black stools, coughing blood, large bruises) — contact PCP urgently

How I can help next
- If you want, I can:  
  - Draft a concise message or email to Dr. Lee summarizing these questions and requesting the labs/imaging before your visit.  
  - Create a one-page printout or script you can use at the appointment.  
  - Help you word a referral request to Northwest Orthopedics if you decide you want one.

Tell me the answers to the clarifying questions (diabetes diagnosis/A1c, whether you’re on warfarin and the correct INR, current BP meds and any joint symptoms) and I’ll draft the email/visit script for you.

