I can help — this is a good set of things to prepare before your annual physical. A few quick clarifying questions first so I tailor recommendations and any message to your PCP correctly:

- Do you already have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes? If so, what is your most recent A1c (and date)?
- Are you taking any blood thinner (warfarin) — you mentioned an INR of “320” (did you mean 3.20)? If you are on warfarin, when was your last dose/INR check?
- When you wrote “hypertension (omeprazole 20 mg daily)” and “anemia (calcium and vitamin D)”, I want to confirm — is omeprazole actually for reflux (and do you take any antihypertensive meds)? Are the calcium + vitamin D for osteoporosis/bone health rather than for anemia?
- Do you have any joint pain, stiffness, or functional limitations right now that prompted thinking about an orthopedics referral? Any history of fractures or prior orthopedic surgery?
- Are you postmenopausal (this affects bone-health screening recommendations)?

While you answer, here’s a focused checklist, suggested questions, tests, and wording you can use at your visit or to email Dr. Jennifer Lee.

1) Key items to review for type 2 diabetes management (if you have diabetes)
- Goals and monitoring
  - Ask what your individualized A1c target should be (typical targets ~7% for many adults, but may be tighter or looser depending on comorbidities, hypoglycemia risk, life expectancy).
  - Request most recent A1c (or draw one at the visit if not recent).
  - If you self-monitor blood glucose, ask whether to continue and recommended frequency (including pre/post-prandial checks or alarm for hypoglycemia).
- Medications
  - Review current diabetes meds and side effects. Ask whether metformin is appropriate (if not already on it) and whether SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 receptor agonists may be beneficial given cardiovascular/renal risk.
  - If on insulin or sulfonylureas, discuss hypoglycemia prevention and action plan.
- Kidney and cardiovascular risk assessment
  - Request lab tests: basic metabolic panel (BMP) with creatinine/eGFR, urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR), fasting lipid panel.
  - Check BP and discuss BP target for someone with diabetes (often <130/80 depending on guideline and tolerance).
- Complication screening
  - Retinopathy: you already have a history of retinopathy — confirm you have ongoing retina/ophthalmology follow-up and ask whether the retina specialist wants anything from PCP (e.g., tighter glucose control).
  - Neuropathy: ask for a formal foot exam (monofilament testing), and whether you need referral to podiatry if numbness/ulcers.
  - Foot care education and footwear review.
- Vaccinations and lifestyle
  - Confirm influenza, pneumococcal, hepatitis B (if indicated) and shingles immunizations are up to date.
  - Discuss smoking cessation (if applicable), weight management, dietitian referral, and an exercise plan.
- Action plan
  - Ask for a clear plan of when to repeat labs and when to return for medication changes.

2) Labs and tests to consider at the annual physical (based on your history)
- A1c (or fasting glucose)
- BMP (electrolytes, creatinine, eGFR)
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio
- Fasting lipid panel
- CBC (for anemia follow-up) and iron studies / ferritin / B12 if anemia persists
- Vitamin D level if monitoring supplementation
- TSH if clinically indicated
- If you’re on warfarin: INR (confirm current value)
- Retinal exam note / ophthalmology status
- Consider DEXA scan for bone density (see bone-health section below)

3) Bone health / orthopedic referral considerations (Northwest Orthopedics)
- You do not automatically need an orthopedic referral just because of age (52) — referrals are appropriate when:
  - You have persistent joint pain or stiffness limiting daily activities despite conservative measures (rest, physical therapy, NSAIDs where safe).
  - Pain with mechanical signs (locking, giving way), progressive deformity, or suspected significant structural damage (torn meniscus, ligament injury).
  - Prior imaging suggests advanced osteoarthritis or fracture, or you want surgical evaluation.
  - You have signs of significant bone loss or fractures — consider DEXA and discuss osteoporosis management.
- What your PCP can evaluate/offer first:
  - Focused musculoskeletal exam, baseline X‑ray of symptomatic joint(s) if indicated.
  - Trial of conservative care: physical therapy referral, activity modification, topical/oral analgesics (avoid NSAIDs if contraindicated by BP/renal issues), intra-articular steroid injection if appropriate.
  - Order DEXA scan if risk factors for osteoporosis (postmenopausal, prolonged steroid use, low body weight, prior fracture). Calcium + vitamin D are good supports but DEXA will guide further treatment.
- When to consider sending to Northwest Orthopedics:
  - Persistent disabling pain after conservative care (6–12 weeks), progressive symptoms, abnormal imaging suggesting surgery might help, or acute injuries (suspected fracture, dislocation, severe ligament tear).
- If you are on anticoagulation (warfarin) or have retinopathy/diabetes, be sure to mention these — they affect surgical risk and wound healing discussions.

4) Practical questions to bring to Dr. Lee (short list you can read or include in an email)
- For diabetes:
  - “Given my age and health history, what should my A1c target be? Can we check an A1c today?”
  - “Can we review my current diabetes medications and whether I should start/change any (metformin, SGLT2, GLP-1)?”
  - “Please order BMP, fasting lipid panel, and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio today.”
  - “I have a history of retinopathy — is my ophthalmology follow-up up to date and do I need any change in management?”
- For orthopedics/bone health:
  - “I’m 52 and taking calcium + vitamin D. Should I have a DEXA scan now?”
  - “I have [describe any joint symptoms if present]. Would you recommend X-ray or trial of PT, or should I see Northwest Orthopedics directly?”
  - “Given my diabetes and any anticoagulation, what should we consider if I need surgery?”
- For medications / labs:
  - “Can you confirm my current BP meds (and whether omeprazole is listed correctly) and whether I need an INR check or other monitoring?”
  - “Please check CBC and iron studies for my anemia and vitamin D level as needed.”

5) Sample brief wording if you want me to draft an email to Dr. Jennifer Lee (choose send vs draft)
If you’d like, I can draft an email to Dr. Lee that:
- Summarizes your conditions (hypertension, anemia, history of retinopathy, current meds),
- Requests specific labs (A1c, BMP, lipid panel, UACR, CBC/iron studies, DEXA if appropriate),
- Asks whether she recommends referral to Northwest Orthopedics for joint evaluation (or PT first),
- Clarifies whether you’re on warfarin and asks for INR confirmation.

6) Safety and other notes
- Clarify the INR value (3.20 vs “320”). If you’re on warfarin, an INR of 3.2 may be therapeutic depending on indication; an INR of 320 is not possible and suggests a typo.
- Omeprazole is not an antihypertensive — please confirm your actual blood‑pressure medications so PCP can adjust therapy to meet targets.
- Because of retinopathy and potential neuropathy, emphasize eye and foot exams annually if diabetic.

Next step
- Tell me the answers to the clarifying questions above (especially whether you have diagnosed type 2 diabetes and whether you are on anticoagulation), and whether you want me to draft an email to Dr. Lee summarizing these requests and asking about a referral to Northwest Orthopedics. If yes, say whether to send it now or save as a draft.

