I can help — this is a good list to prepare for your annual. Before I lay out suggested questions and talking points, two quick clarifying items so I give the most useful recommendations:

- Can you confirm whether you already have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes and, if so, your most recent A1c and current diabetes medications?  
- A couple of details look like they might be mixed up: omeprazole is a PPI (not a typical hypertension med), calcium + vitamin D treat bone/deficiency (not usually anemia), and your INR reading — did you mean 3.20 (or 2.0 or 320)? If you’re on warfarin, the exact INR value matters.

Once you confirm those, I can tailor the list. Meanwhile, here are general, high-value questions and checklists you can use at your visit.

Questions to ask about type 2 diabetes prevention/management (adapt if you already have diabetes)
- Do I have diabetes or prediabetes based on my recent labs? When should we check an A1c (or repeat it)? What is my target A1c given my age, retinopathy, and other conditions?  
- How often should I have A1c testing, fasting glucose, and/or home glucose monitoring? Should I consider a continuous glucose monitor (CGM) or structured home checks?  
- Do my blood pressure and cholesterol targets change because of diabetes risk? Should I start or continue a statin or aspirin for cardiovascular risk reduction?  
- Given my history of retinopathy, how often should I have ophthalmology/retina follow-up for diabetes-related eye disease?  
- Do I need kidney testing (serum creatinine, eGFR) and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR)? How often?  
- Are any diabetes medications recommended for me (metformin, SGLT2 inhibitors, GLP-1 receptor agonists)? Which are appropriate given my other conditions (retinopathy, hypertension, INR/anticoagulation, anemia)?  
- If I already take glucose-lowering drugs: are they working well? Any side effects or risks (hypoglycemia, weight, kidney function, interactions with my other meds)?  
- With retinopathy already present, are there specific medication risks or benefits (e.g., GLP-1 class—rare reports of rapid vision change) we should consider?  
- Should I have a foot exam now and annual neuropathy screening (monofilament, vibration testing)?  
- Lifestyle: are there specific diet, exercise, or weight goals you recommend? Any structured program or referral (diabetes educator, dietitian, exercise program)?  
- Vaccination: am I up to date on flu, pneumococcal, and shingles vaccines (important in diabetes)?  
- When should I call you vs go to ER for high or low blood sugars? (Get explicit thresholds.)

Questions to ask about whether you need an orthopedics referral (Northwest Orthopedics) given age/risk factors
- I’m 52 and have risk factors (hypertension, retinopathy, possible medications). Should I be evaluated by orthopedics for joint pain, mobility, or fall risk?  
- Are my symptoms (describe: pain location, duration, activity limitations) consistent with needing imaging (X-ray, MRI) before a referral?  
- Could conservative treatments (physical therapy, steroid injection, activity modification, weight management) be tried first — and for how long — before referring to orthopedics?  
- Given my history of retinopathy and any anticoagulation (if applicable), are there special precautions for surgeries or procedures?  
- Do I need bone health testing first (DEXA scan) given age, calcium/vitamin D use, and anemia? Should I see endocrinology or osteoporosis clinic instead?  
- If I already have fractures, joint deformities, or progressive loss of function, can you refer me directly to Northwest Orthopedics? If not, what specific red flags should prompt a referral?

Practical items to bring/prepare for your visit
- Up-to-date medication list (name, dose, reason). Note any anticoagulant (warfarin) or diabetes meds.  
- Recent lab results: A1c, fasting glucose, BMP (kidney function), lipid panel, urine microalbumin if available, and the INR test result (date + value).  
- Dates of last eye exam (who/where) and any retina treatments.  
- Brief symptom list: joint pain (where, how long, severity, what makes it better/worse), falls or balance issues, vision changes, numbness/tingling in feet, any hypoglycemia episodes.  
- Functional goals you want (e.g., walk X minutes, return to a sport, reduce pain so you can garden).

Suggested phrasing to ask Dr. Lee (short script)
- “Hi Dr. Lee — for my annual I’d like to review diabetes risk/management and discuss whether I should be referred to Northwest Orthopedics. My labs show [insert A1c if known] and my last INR was [confirm value]. Given my history of retinopathy and my age (52), what diabetes screening/treatment steps do you recommend now (A1c target, eye and kidney testing, med options)? Also, I have [describe joint symptoms or say ‘no major joint pain but concerned about risk’]. Do you recommend PT, imaging, DEXA, or a referral to Northwest Orthopedics now or only if X/Y/Z occurs?”

Quick notes on a few items you mentioned
- INR: if you’re on warfarin, an INR of 3.20 is within or slightly above some therapeutic ranges — if you meant “320” please confirm (that value isn’t physiologic). If you are anticoagulated, make sure surgical/procedural precautions are discussed before any orthopedic procedures.  
- Retinopathy: regular ophthalmology follow-up is important; tell your PCP the date of your last eye exam.  
- Medication clarifications: omeprazole is a PPI (for reflux), and calcium + vitamin D are bone supplements — please confirm your antihypertensive and anemia treatments so I can include any drug-specific questions (e.g., interactions with diabetes drugs).

Do you want me to:
- Draft a short message/email you can send to Dr. Lee with these questions?  
- Create a one-page checklist you can print and bring to the visit?  
- Or tailor these questions if you confirm whether you already have diabetes, current A1c, exact INR, and your sex (for DEXA screening guidance)?

If you confirm the few details above, I’ll tailor the list and can draft the exact message to send to your PCP.

