I can help — this is a good time to organize what to review with Dr. Jennifer Lee at your annual physical. Before I give specific questions and a checklist, a couple of quick clarifying points so my suggestions are accurate:

- Do you already have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes, or do you want questions about screening/risk and early management?  
- Can you confirm your medications and conditions? You wrote “hypertension (omeprazole 20 mg daily)” and “anemia (calcium and vitamin D)” — omeprazole is a PPI (not a blood‑pressure med) and calcium/vitamin D aren’t treatments for anemia, so I want to be sure I have the right meds/conditions. Please list current prescription meds (and OTC supplements) and which conditions they relate to.  
- You reported “last INR was 320.” Do you mean INR = 3.20, or something else? Also: are you taking warfarin or another anticoagulant? If you are anticoagulated and the INR is very high (>4) or you have bleeding, contact your clinician urgently.

Once you confirm, here are focused items to discuss at the visit and suggested questions you can use.

A. Type 2 diabetes — topics to cover and questions to ask
- If you already have diabetes, bring your most recent A1c and glucose readings (or ask for these tests). If you do not, discuss screening.
- Tests to request or review:
  - A1c (or fasting glucose) and, if indicated, a baseline fasting lipid panel
  - Kidney function (BMP or CMP, eGFR) and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR)
  - Liver enzymes if you’re on certain diabetes meds
  - Foot exam and monofilament testing
  - Ophthalmology/retinopathy follow-up (you have a history of retinopathy — make sure retinal screening is up to date)
- Questions to ask Dr. Lee (phrases you can use)
  - “What is my current A1c and target A1c for my age and health status?”
  - “Given my blood pressure, retinopathy history, and other health factors, what glucose target and treatment approach do you recommend?”
  - “Do I need to start or change medications (metformin, SGLT2 inhibitor, GLP‑1 receptor agonist, or insulin) based on my labs and risk profile?”
  - “Do any of my current meds or supplements interact with diabetes medications?”
  - “Should I have a urine albumin test and an eGFR check now to screen for diabetic kidney disease?”
  - “How often should I have retinal and foot exams given my retinopathy history?”
  - “Are there lifestyle interventions (diet, exercise plan, weight goals) you recommend and can you refer me to diabetes education or nutrition counseling?”
  - “Are there cardiovascular risk‑reduction steps I should take (statin, aspirin?), given diabetes and hypertension?” (ask this especially if diabetes is confirmed)
- Practical items to discuss:
  - How to self-monitor glucose (if needed) and when to contact the clinic for high/low readings
  - Vaccinations relevant to diabetes (flu, pneumococcal, COVID booster as appropriate)

B. Orthopedics referral — when it’s reasonable and what to ask
- Consider referral to Northwest Orthopedics (or PT/orthopedics) if you have any of:
  - Persistent joint pain that limits daily activities despite conservative care (rest, NSAIDs, physical therapy)
  - Mechanical symptoms (locking, giving way, catching), instability, or suspected structural injury
  - Worsening mobility affecting quality of life or falls
  - Suspected rotator cuff tear, meniscal tear, or significant degenerative arthritis on exam or X‑ray
  - Symptoms not improving after a trial of conservative management (4–6 weeks, depending on condition)
- Things that increase the need to evaluate bone/joint health:
  - Age 50+, long-term PPI use (omeprazole) which can be associated with bone density concerns, history of fractures, long-term corticosteroid use, low BMI, or other osteoporosis risk factors
- Questions to ask Dr. Lee:
  - “Based on my age and symptoms, do you recommend imaging (X‑ray or MRI) or referral to orthopedics?”
  - “Would a trial of physical therapy, injections, or other conservative treatments be appropriate before referral?”
  - “Do I need a DEXA scan for bone density screening given my age, PPI use, and any other risk factors?”
  - “If I’m on anticoagulation, how would that affect procedures like joint injections or surgery?”
- If you’re considering a referral, ask for a specific reason on the referral (e.g., “evaluation for chronic knee pain limiting walking”); it speeds the process.

C. Things to bring or have ready for the appointment
- Up-to-date medication list (prescription + OTC supplements) with doses and what condition each treats
- Recent home BP readings, recent labs (A1c, fasting glucose, lipids, BMP), and date of last retinal appointment
- Any imaging reports (X‑rays, MRIs) or DEXA results
- A one-week log of symptoms: pain location, severity, what makes it better/worse, functional limits
- Clarify anticoagulant status and most recent INR (if applicable)

D. Safety notes
- If you are on warfarin and your INR is truly >4 (or you’re bleeding), contact your clinician or urgent care immediately. If you meant 3.20 (an INR of 3.2), that’s mildly elevated but depends on the anticoagulation target range — bring this up with Dr. Lee.
- If you are taking long-term PPI (omeprazole), mention it — it’s relevant to bone health and certain nutrient absorption (B12, magnesium, calcium) and may impact DEXA decisions.

Next step I can help with
- I can draft a concise list of questions/notes you can bring to the appointment or an email to Dr. Lee requesting specific tests/referrals. Would you like:
  - A short “appointment script” you can read during the visit, or
  - A draft message/email to send to Dr. Lee (I can include labs to request and a referral request if you want)?

Also please confirm the medication/condition details and the INR number so I can tailor the recommendations precisely.

