Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help you prepare—great idea to go into your annual physical with specific questions. A few quick clarifying items first so I give the most useful suggestions:
- You wrote “omeprazole 20 mg daily” under hypertension — omeprazole is a stomach acid medicine, not for BP. Do you have a separate BP med you want included?  
- You listed “anemia (calcium and vitamin D)” — calcium + vitamin D are usually for bone health/osteopenia, not anemia. Are you taking anything specifically for anemia (iron, B12, etc.)?  
- You said “last INR was 320” — do you mean INR = 3.20 (i.e., 3.2)? If you’re on warfarin/anticoagulation, that’s important to note because it affects referrals, procedures and some medication choices.

If any of those need correction, tell me and I’ll update the questions. Below are targeted questions and actions you can bring to your PCP (Dr. Jennifer Lee), grouped by topic, plus a short email template I can draft/send if you’d like.

Questions and items to cover about type 2 diabetes management
- Current status and targets
  - What is my current A1C and what A1C target should we aim for given my age (52), hypertension, retinopathy and other conditions?
  - How often should I have my A1C checked?
- Medications and safety
  - Are my current diabetes medications still appropriate? (If you don’t list your diabetes meds, tell me and I’ll include them.)
  - Given my other conditions and medications (especially if you’re on anticoagulants or have reduced kidney function), are there any drugs I should avoid or consider (for example, SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 receptor agonists)? Please talk about risks/benefits.
  - Do any of my current meds interact with omeprazole, calcium/vitamin D, or anticoagulants?
- Complications screening and monitoring
  - Retinopathy: When was my last eye exam with retinal imaging? Do I need an urgent ophthalmology/retina follow-up?
  - Kidney health: Can we check kidney function (serum creatinine/eGFR) and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) now?
  - Neuropathy and feet: Can you do a foot exam today or refer me for annual neuropathy/foot screening?
  - Cardiovascular risk: Do I need a lipid panel and what are my BP and lipid targets given my diabetes and hypertension?
- Prevention and lifestyle
  - Are there any immunizations I should get (flu, pneumococcal, shingles, COVID boosters)?
  - Any recommended diet/exercise, weight-loss targets, or referrals to diabetes education/nutritionist?
- Hypoglycemia and testing
  - Should I be monitoring blood glucose at home — how often and with what targets?
  - Do I need continuous glucose monitoring given my risk profile?
- Follow-up plan
  - What are the criteria for medication change escalation, and when should we follow up?

Tests to request (if not recently done)
- A1C
- Fasting glucose (optional)
- Serum creatinine, eGFR, basic metabolic panel
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR)
- Lipid panel
- Liver function tests (if medications warrant)
- If on warfarin: current INR and plan for monitoring
- Retinal eye exam referral if not done within the last year

Questions and items to consider about orthopedics referral (Northwest Orthopedics)
- Symptom-driven: first describe your symptoms to Dr. Lee — specifically mention joint(s) affected, pain severity, limitations in daily activities, instability, swelling, mechanical symptoms (catching/locking), and how long it’s been happening.
- Conservative measures first: Ask if you should try or continue:
  - Physical therapy (PT)
  - Weight loss or exercise modifications
  - NSAIDs or topical pain relievers (check interactions if you’re on anticoagulants)
  - Joint injections (corticosteroid or hyaluronic acid) — discuss bleeding risk if on anticoagulation
- When to refer to orthopedics:
  - Persistent pain that limits ADLs despite 6–12 weeks of conservative care
  - Suspected structural problem (torn meniscus, rotator cuff, severe osteoarthritis, instability)
  - Recurrent falls, progressive deformity or mechanical locking
  - Consider imaging (X-ray initially, MRI if soft-tissue concern) before or after referral
- Bone health: since you take calcium + vitamin D, ask if you need a DEXA (bone density) scan given age and any menopausal status / fracture history
- If you want me to ask for a referral specifically: request that PCP coordinate the referral and order any necessary imaging or DEXA beforehand

Practical things to bring / prepare for the visit
- Updated medication list (including over-the-counter and supplements) and doses
- Recent home BP/glucose logs if you have them
- Any recent lab results or the date of the last A1C, lipids, creatinine, INR
- A short symptom summary for orthopedics (body part, pain scale 0–10, what helps/worsens it, how it affects daily life)
- Note of last eye exam and who performed it
- Ask for copies or electronic orders/referrals for ophthalmology, PT, orthopedics or DEXA as needed

