Great — I can help you get organized for the visit. Below are (A) clarifying items I’d check before the appointment, (B) specific tests to request/update, (C) suggested questions to bring for diabetes management, (D) what to review about retinopathy and anticoagulation, and (E) guidance on whether/when a referral to Northwest Orthopedics makes sense plus questions to ask the PCP about that.

Please confirm a few quick facts so I can tailor these further:
- Do you already have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes, or are you asking about screening/concern for it?
- Are you currently taking an anticoagulant (warfarin)? Your “INR was 320” — did you mean INR 3.20?
- Can you confirm sex (M/F/other) and any current antihypertensive medications (names/doses)?
- Are you having joint pain or mobility issues right now, or is the orthopedics concern purely preventive?

A. Items to clarify/bring to the visit
- Current, complete medication list with doses (including OTC supplements): I noticed omeprazole 20 mg, and calcium + vitamin D — these aren’t typical treatments for hypertension/anemia, so bring everything to clarify.
- Recent lab results (A1c, fasting glucose, lipids, CBC, BMP, INR if on warfarin, prior DEXA or x-rays if any).
- List of symptoms: polyuria, polydipsia, fatigue, unintentional weight change, neuropathic symptoms, joint pain or functional limits, falls or balance problems.
- Eye care: date of last ophthalmology/retina visit.

B. Tests to request or update at the annual (especially given your history)
- HbA1c (or fasting glucose) — screen/monitor diabetes control.
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) — kidney microvascular complication screening.
- Basic metabolic panel (BMP) — kidney function and electrolytes before certain diabetes drugs.
- Lipid panel.
- CBC (evaluate anemia) and iron studies/B12/folate if anemia unclear.
- INR (if on warfarin) — confirm target and recent value.
- ECG if indicated by cardiovascular risk or new symptoms.
- Consider DEXA scan (bone density) — you’re 52 and taking calcium/vitamin D; consider if female/postmenopausal or other risk factors for osteoporosis.
- A1c plus foot exam and monofilament test if diabetes confirmed.
- Vaccinations review (influenza, pneumococcal, shingles) depending on history.

C. Suggested diabetes-management questions to ask Dr. Lee
- Do I have type 2 diabetes now? What is my current A1c and what target should we set for me personally?
- If I have diabetes (or prediabetes), should we start or change medication? (e.g., metformin first-line if appropriate) — mention preferences: avoiding weight gain, minimizing hypoglycemia.
- Given my age, hypertension, and retinopathy history, would SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 receptor agonists be appropriate for cardiovascular/renal/weight benefits?
- Are there any kidney function, GI, or drug-interaction reasons I shouldn’t take metformin/SGLT2/GLP-1?
- Should I be referred to endocrinology for medication management or complex glycemic control?
- How often should I get A1c checks, urine microalbumin, and foot/neuropathy screening?
- Any lifestyle or diet referrals (diabetes education, nutritionist, diabetes prevention program) you recommend?

D. Retinopathy, anemia, and anticoagulation — topics to raise
- Retinopathy: confirm schedule for eye exam (annual retinal specialist screening is usually recommended). Ask if tighter BP or glucose targets are needed to slow progression.
- Anemia: ask what type of anemia you have and whether your calcium + vitamin D are for osteoporosis rather than anemia. Request a workup (CBC trends, ferritin, B12/folate) if the cause isn’t clear.
- INR/anticoagulant: confirm whether you’re on warfarin and the proper target INR. If you truly had an INR of ~3.2, discuss bleeding risk given retinopathy and whether anticoagulation management or monitoring should change. (If you meant something else, clarify that.)

E. Orthopedics — when a referral makes sense and what to ask
When to consider referral to Northwest Orthopedics (or orthopedics in general):
- You have persistent joint pain or functional limitation that interferes with day-to-day activities after trying conservative care (NSAIDs as appropriate, physical therapy, activity modification) for several weeks/months.
- Objective findings on imaging (x-ray or MRI) showing advanced arthritis, labral tears, rotator cuff pathology, or structural problems that may need specialist intervention.
- Recurrent falls, instability, or concern for fracture risk (especially if osteoporosis risk or low DEXA score).
- Preoperative evaluation or if considering injections/surgery.

Questions to ask your PCP about orthopedics referral:
- Given my age and risk factors (retinopathy, possible bone-health meds), should I get DEXA now? Do I have osteoporosis or osteopenia risk that would push for ortho referral?
- I’m experiencing (describe any joint pain, stiffness, limitations). What conservative steps should we try first (PT, injections, bracing, analgesics)? If these fail, when would you refer to orthopedics?
- If you refer, do you recommend Northwest Orthopedics specifically? If so, please include any relevant imaging or notes with the referral.
- Any precautions because of my other conditions (retinopathy, anticoagulation, anemia) that would affect orthopedic evaluation or treatment options?

F. Red flags to bring up immediately
- New visual changes or sudden worsening of vision.
- New chest pain, shortness of breath, neurologic changes.
- New or severe joint swelling, redness, fever (possible infection).
- Recurrent falls or loss of mobility.

Would you like:
- A printable one-page checklist of the above to bring to your appointment?
- A draft message/email to Dr. Jennifer Lee summarizing these questions and requesting specific tests (I can draft and send or save as a draft for you)?
- Help organizing your current meds and last labs into a concise summary to bring?

If you confirm the clarifying items above (especially whether you have diabetes and whether you’re on warfarin with INR 3.20), I’ll tailor the checklist and can draft the email to Dr. Lee or create a one-page sheet you can print.

