I can help you prepare a clear, focused list of questions and items to bring up at your annual physical with Dr. Jennifer Lee. Before I draft anything for you to send, a couple of clarifying points will help me tailor the list precisely:
- Do you have a formal diagnosis of type 2 diabetes? If yes, what is your most recent A1c and what diabetes medications (if any) are you taking now?
- Are you taking warfarin (or another anticoagulant)? You wrote “last INR was 320” — did you mean INR = 3.20, or something else?
- Do you currently have joint pain, mobility limits, or repeated falls that make you worry about orthopedics?

While you answer, here’s a ready-to-use checklist and suggested questions to guide the visit.

Suggested agenda to bring up with Dr. Lee
- Confirm diagnoses and meds:
  - Review current problem list (hypertension, anemia, retinopathy, any diagnosis of type 2 diabetes).
  - Review current medication list and indications (I noticed omeprazole, calcium + vitamin D — please confirm other meds, especially any anticoagulant).
  - Clarify the INR result and anticoagulation plan if applicable.

Type 2 diabetes — questions to ask
- Do I have type 2 diabetes (if not already diagnosed) or prediabetes? If yes:
  - What is my current A1c target given my age, retinopathy, and other conditions? (Typical target is ~<7% but should be individualized.)
  - Based on my A1c and overall risk, should we change or add diabetes medications? Are SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 receptor agonists appropriate for me given their heart and kidney benefits?
  - Do I need an endocrinology referral for complex management or for medication changes?
  - How often should we monitor A1c and blood glucose (self-monitoring) going forward?
  - Are there any adjustments to my blood pressure or cholesterol goals because of diabetes? (E.g., BP target <130/80 in many cases; high-intensity statin consideration if diabetes >40.)
  - Any specific diet, exercise, or structured program you recommend?

Diabetes-related screenings and monitoring to request
- Labs:
  - A1c (or just request if not recently done)
  - Fasting glucose (if needed)
  - Basic metabolic panel (BMP) including kidney function/eGFR
  - Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) for kidney damage
  - Lipid panel
  - CBC (for your anemia)
  - Iron studies, ferritin, B12/folate if anemia persists
- Exams:
  - Annual dilated eye exam with ophthalmology (especially with history of retinopathy)
  - Foot exam and neuropathy screen
  - Blood pressure checks at home and review of readings
- Vaccines: ask about influenza (seasonal), RZV (Shingrix — recommended for adults 50+), Tdap if due, and pneumococcal vaccination based on risk.

Retinopathy and ophthalmology
- Ask for an up-to-date referral to ophthalmology (retina specialist if previously treated for retinopathy) for an annual dilated exam.
- Ask what symptoms (vision changes, floaters, flashes) should prompt urgent evaluation.

Anemia
- Ask for labs to evaluate the cause of your anemia (CBC, iron panel, ferritin, B12, folate, possibly GI evaluation if iron-deficiency suspected).
- Confirm the appropriateness of your calcium + vitamin D (these do not treat anemia — ask what they were prescribed for).

INR / anticoagulation
- Clarify whether you are on warfarin and the intended INR target. If your INR was 3.20 (or another value), ask whether that is within target and whether monitoring frequency needs adjustment.
- Ask if any medications (e.g., antibiotics, PPIs, new prescriptions) or diet changes affect INR.

Orthopedics referral — how to decide (questions and criteria)
- Do I need a referral to Northwest Orthopedics given my age and risk factors?
  - If you have persistent joint pain, reduced mobility, repeated falls, deformity, progressive functional decline, or imaging showing advanced osteoarthritis, ask for an orthopedics referral.
  - If your main concern is bone health, ask whether a DEXA scan (bone density) is indicated and whether you need a referral to an osteoporosis specialist rather than orthopedics.
  - Ask if conservative care (PT, weight management, analgesics, joint injections) is appropriate first and how long to try it before referral for surgical opinion.
  - If you have new or worsening hip/knee/shoulder/back pain limiting ADLs or sleep, request expedited evaluation and imaging (X-ray or MRI as indicated).

Red flags to mention that warrant faster evaluation or referral
- Sudden visual changes or new floaters/flashes
- Sudden severe joint pain after a fall or concern for fracture
- Progressive loss of mobility or inability to perform ADLs
- Signs of infection, unexplained weight loss, or new neurologic symptoms

Practical requests you can make at the appointment
- Order lab panel above (A1c, BMP, UACR, lipid panel, CBC with iron studies)
- Request DEXA scan if risk factors for low bone density (age, meds, family history, previous fractures)
- Annual ophthalmology referral for retinopathy monitoring
- Consider endocrinology referral if diabetes control is suboptimal or medication changes are complex
- Orthopedics referral if persistent symptomatic joint disease or imaging shows advanced arthritis
- Review and reconcile all medications; ask about interactions (including PPI and anticoagulants if applicable)

What to bring to the visit
- Up-to-date medication list (including supplements and over-the-counter drugs)
- Any recent lab results or outside notes
- Home BP readings if you take them
- Description of symptoms (joint pain location, severity, timing; vision issues; falls)
- Questions from this checklist

Would you like me to:
- Draft a concise message or email to Dr. Lee summarizing these questions and requesting the labs/referrals? OR
- Create a one-page checklist you can print/take to the appointment?

Also please confirm the clarifying items above (diabetes diagnosis and current A1c, whether you’re on warfarin and the INR reading, and whether you have joint symptoms). That will let me make the message/referral wording precise.

