Great — I can help you get ready. Below I’ve organized (A) a short list of clarifying details I need from you, (B) suggested questions to bring to your annual physical about type 2 diabetes management, (C) what tests to request/check, (D) how to think about whether you need a referral to Northwest Orthopedics (and when to ask for one), (E) what to bring to the visit, and (F) sample wording you can use when talking to Dr. Jennifer Lee.

A — Quick clarifying questions
- Do you have a formal diagnosis of type 2 diabetes? If so, what was your last A1c and when was it done?
- You wrote “my last INR was 320.” Did you mean INR = 3.20, or another lab/test? Are you taking warfarin or another blood thinner?
- You listed omeprazole 20 mg for hypertension — is that accurate? (Omeprazole is a PPI; what blood‑pressure med do you take?)
- For your anemia, you mentioned calcium and vitamin D — do you actually take iron, B12, or folate supplements (or has your doctor told you the anemia is from something else)?
- Do you have joint pain, decreased mobility, falls, or foot problems (ulcers, numbness, deformity)?

B — Key questions to ask about type 2 diabetes management
- What is my target A1c given my age (52), hypertension, retinopathy, and other conditions?
- What was my last A1c and how often should we repeat it? (Suggest every 3 months if changing therapy; every 6 months if stable.)
- Do I need changes in my diabetes medications? Are there benefits for me from SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP‑1 agonists for heart/kidney protection?
- How is my kidney function (eGFR)? Is metformin still safe/useful for me if I’m on it?
- Should I be on a statin or aspirin for cardiovascular risk reduction (given hypertension and diabetes)?
- Given my retinopathy, do I need a referral/update for ophthalmology, and how often should I get dilated eye exams?
- Do I have any signs of peripheral neuropathy? Should I get a monofilament foot exam and baseline foot care education?
- How should I monitor blood glucose at home (frequency, targets, when to check fasting vs post‑prandial)?
- What are my blood pressure goals with diabetes? Any medication adjustments needed?
- Any concerns about hypoglycemia risk with current meds?
- Do I need diabetes education (DSME) or nutrition counseling / referral to an endocrinologist?

C — Tests and screenings to request or confirm
- A1c (if >3–6 months since last)
- Fasting glucose and/or CMP (electrolytes, liver, kidney function, eGFR)
- Lipid panel
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) for diabetic kidney disease screening
- Urine microalbumin if recommended
- Complete blood count (CBC) if anemia history — to check current hemoglobin/iron indices
- Dilated eye exam referral (if not seen within past year)
- Foot exam and monofilament test (document neuropathy)
- Baseline ECG if cardiovascular risk unclear (ask PCP)
- Vaccination status (flu, pneumococcal, Tdap, shingles)
- Consider DEXA scan if you have bone‑health risk factors (you said calcium + vitamin D — discuss this)

D — Deciding whether you need an orthopedics referral (Northwest Orthopedics)
When to ask for a referral:
- Persistent joint pain that limits daily activities (walking, stairs, work) despite conservative therapy (NSAIDs, PT, activity modification) for ~6–12 weeks
- Significant loss of function, mobility, or range of motion
- Instability, recurrent falls, or mechanical symptoms (locking, catching)
- Suspected fracture, acute injury, or deformity
- Imaging (X‑ray, MRI) shows advanced osteoarthritis or another structural problem potentially requiring procedures
- Foot deformity, persistent ulcers, or complex diabetic foot problems — consider podiatry and/or orthopedics

What to ask Dr. Lee:
- “I’m 52 and have diabetes/retinopathy and hypertension — given my age and health, do you recommend an orthopedics referral for my joint/back/hip/knee/shoulder pain?”
- “Would you order X‑rays or other imaging first, or send me directly to orthopedics?”
- “If I have diabetic neuropathy or foot ulcers, should I see podiatry or orthopedics?”
- “Would physical therapy or an orthopedic evaluation be the next step?”

E — What to bring or prepare for the visit
- Current, complete medication list (including doses — PPIs, supplements, blood thinners, diabetes meds)
- Record of last A1c, recent blood pressure readings at home, home glucose logs
- List of symptoms (pain location, onset, how it limits activities, prior treatments tried)
- Any prior imaging or recent ophthalmology/podiatry notes
- Questions you want answered (use the lists above)
- If you want, ask me and I can draft a short email or message to Dr. Lee summarizing these points

F — Sample phrases you can use with Dr. Lee
- Diabetes:
  - “Given my history of retinopathy and hypertension, what A1c target do you recommend for me, and should we change any of my medications for better cardiovascular or kidney protection?”
  - “Can we check A1c, UACR, lipid panel, and kidney function today?”
  - “Can you refer me to diabetes education or endocrinology if my A1c is above target?”
- Orthopedics:
  - “I’ve had persistent [knee/hip/back/shoulder] pain for X months that limits my activities; I’ve tried [PT/NSAIDs/ice], but it’s not improved. Could you refer me to orthopedics or order imaging?”
  - “Because I have diabetes and retinopathy, I’m concerned about fall risk and bone/joint health — should I see orthopedics or get a DEXA scan?”

Quick safety notes
- Clarify the INR value and whether you’re on warfarin — if INR truly was 3.20 or high, that affects medication choices and procedures.
- Make sure your eye care is up-to-date given the history of retinopathy — diabetic retinopathy needs regular ophthalmology follow-up.
- If you have foot wounds, numbness, or deformity, request an urgent podiatry/orthopedics referral.

If you’d like, I can:
- Turn these into a one-page checklist you can print or bring on your phone.
- Draft a brief message/email to Dr. Lee summarizing your concerns and requesting the tests/referrals above — tell me whether you want it sent as a draft or emailed now and what tone (brief/formal) you prefer.

