I can help you get organized for the visit. First, a couple of quick clarifying questions so I tailor this correctly:

- Do you already have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes, and if so what is your most recent A1c and current diabetes medicines (e.g., metformin, insulin, SGLT2, GLP‑1, sulfonylurea)?
- Can you confirm your medication list (you said hypertension and listed omeprazole 20 mg — omeprazole is for reflux; is there another BP med you take?) and whether you’re on any blood thinners (warfarin)? Your note “last INR was 320” looks like a typo — did you mean INR 3.20, or was that a different lab (platelets, etc.)?
- Are you male or female (for some screening guidance like bone density)?

Once you confirm, I can customize further. Below is a solid set of questions and topics to bring up with Dr. Jennifer Lee at Harbor General, grouped by theme.

Questions about type 2 diabetes management
- Diagnostic / control status
  - Do I have (or still have) type 2 diabetes? If yes, what is my current A1c and what target A1c should we aim for given my age, retinopathy, and heart/blood‑pressure history?
  - How often should I have an A1c checked?
- Medication review and options
  - Should my medications be changed now? (Mention current meds so she can comment.)
  - Given my age and cardiovascular/renal risk, am I a candidate for SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP‑1 receptor agonists for cardiovascular/renal protection? What are the risks/benefits for me?
  - If I’m on metformin or insulin, are dose adjustments needed? Are there interactions with my other meds?
- Blood glucose monitoring and hypoglycemia
  - Do you recommend home glucose checks? If so, when and how often?
  - What signs of low blood sugar should I watch for and how should I treat it?
- Complications screening and prevention
  - How often should I have:
    - Retinal eye exams (given history of retinopathy)
    - Urine microalbumin / spot albumin:creatinine ratio and serum creatinine/eGFR
    - Foot exam and neuropathy screening (monofilament testing)
    - Lipid panel and blood pressure targets in the setting of diabetes
  - Do I need an aspirin or statin for cardiovascular risk reduction?
  - Vaccinations relevant to diabetes (flu, pneumococcus, shingles) — are any due?
- Lifestyle and referrals
  - Any specific dietary, weight, or exercise targets you recommend?
  - Would a referral to diabetes education / nutritionist or an endocrinologist be helpful?

Questions about retinopathy and eye care
- When is my next comprehensive eye exam with ophthalmology recommended?
- Given my retinopathy history, any additional treatments or monitoring needed now?

Questions about anemia and bone health
- Are the current supplements (calcium + vitamin D) appropriate for my anemia — is further testing (iron studies, ferritin, B12, folate) needed?
- Should we check vitamin D level or repeat CBC before the visit?
- At age 52, do I need a DEXA bone density scan now (depending on sex, menopausal status, steroid use, fracture history)? If not now, when?

Clarify anticoagulation / INR
- If you are on warfarin, confirm target INR and last value — if INR was unusually high, discuss bleeding risk and whether any medication changes or urgent follow‑up are needed before the appointment.

Considerations for a Northwest Orthopedics referral
When to consider an ortho referral (ask Dr. Lee):
- Persistent joint pain limiting daily activities (walking, climbing stairs), sleep, or work despite conservative measures
- Recurrent falls, instability, or mechanical symptoms (locking, catching) in a joint
- Visible deformity, progressive loss of range of motion, or inability to do basic tasks
- Significant imaging abnormalities (if you have recent X‑rays/MRIs that show advanced arthritis or structural problems)
- Suspected fracture or structural problem that won’t improve with conservative care

What to try first (ask whether these have been tried and whether referral is next)
- Trial of physical therapy, tailored strengthening/ROM program
- Weight management, footwear modification, orthotics if lower‑extremity problems
- Short course of NSAIDs (if safe with your other meds) or topical analgesics
- In‑office injections (steroid or viscosupplementation) if appropriate
- Imaging: do you need X‑ray or MRI before a referral? If you already have relevant imaging, bring it to the appointment

Questions to ask if Dr. Lee recommends a referral
- What specifically should the orthopedist evaluate (knee/hip/back/shoulder)?
- Should I see Northwest Orthopedics now or try more conservative care first?
- If referred, what information or imaging should I bring?
- Any precautions (e.g., if anticoagulated) before injections or procedures?

Recommended tests and items to bring to the annual visit
- Labs to consider (discuss with Dr. Lee; many are reasonable given your history):
  - A1c (or fasting glucose)
  - Lipid panel
  - CBC (follow anemia), iron studies if not recently done
  - CMP (electrolytes, liver, kidney function)
  - Urine microalbumin (kidney screening)
  - Vitamin D level if unsure about supplementation adequacy
  - TSH if indicated
  - INR check if on warfarin
- Bring:
  - Current medication list (doses and frequency), including OTC and supplements
  - Recent glucose meter log or CGM reports if you monitor sugars
  - Any recent imaging (X‑rays/MRI) and reports if you plan to discuss orthopedics
  - Notes on symptoms: when they started, what makes them worse/better, functional limits
  - Any recent specialist reports (ophthalmology, cardiology)

How I can help next
- I can draft a concise message/email to Dr. Lee summarizing your concerns and asking the specific diabetes/orthopedics questions and requesting any pre‑visit labs. Would you like me to draft that? If so, tell me:
  - Your current diabetes status (diagnosis? A1c? meds?)
  - Exact med list (including any blood thinners)
  - Whether you want Dr. Lee to order labs before the visit and whether you want a referral to Northwest Orthopedics now
- Or I can make a printable one‑page checklist you can bring to the appointment.

Which would you prefer — and can you confirm the three clarifications I asked up front (diabetes status/A1c; full med list including BP meds and anticoagulants; sex and whether “INR 320” was meant to be 3.20)?

