I can help — first a couple quick clarifying questions so my suggestions are tailored and safe:

- Do you already have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes, or are you asking about screening/early management?  
- What are your current diabetes medications (if any) and your most recent A1C or home glucose readings?  
- You said your last “INR was 320” — did you mean INR 3.20 (and are you on warfarin/another blood thinner)? Or was that a different lab (e.g., platelet count or something else)?  
- Do you have joint pain, stiffness, falls, limited walking, or any specific orthopedic symptoms right now that make you worry about a referral?

While you answer, below is a practical checklist of items, tests, and focused questions to bring up at your annual physical with Dr. Jennifer Lee — grouped into diabetes management, retinopathy/eye care, cardiovascular/other risk management, and orthopedic referral considerations. I’ve also included suggested phrasing you can use.

A. Diabetes — screening / management questions and items to request
(Use if you have T2D or are at risk and want proactive management)

Tests to request / confirm at the visit
- A1C (if diagnosed) or fasting glucose (if screening)  
- Basic metabolic panel (BMP) / creatinine and eGFR (kidney function)  
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (microalbumin) — annual for diabetics  
- Lipid panel (cholesterol)  
- Hepatic panel if considering certain diabetes meds  
- Vitamin B12, especially if you’re on metformin or long-term PPI (omeprazole can reduce B12 absorption)  
- Review of home glucose logs or CGM if you use one

Questions to ask Dr. Lee (phrasing examples)
- “Given my age and health history (hypertension, retinopathy, anemia), should we screen for diabetes now or repeat an A1C? What target A1C is appropriate for me?”  
- “If I have T2D (or if I get diagnosed), what first-line medication do you recommend for me and why? Are there reasons to avoid metformin in my case?”  
- “Do I have any kidney or heart disease that would make SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 receptor agonists preferred?”  
- “What are my current cardiovascular risk targets (A1C, BP, LDL), and do we need to change my treatment to meet them?”  
- “How often should I be monitored for hypoglycemia if we intensify therapy?”  
- “Are there medication interactions or concerns with my current meds (omeprazole, calcium/vitamin D, any blood thinners) I should know about?”  
- “Can you review whether my vaccines (flu, pneumococcal, shingles) are up to date for diabetes/age?”

Things to discuss about newer diabetes drugs (so you and Dr. Lee can weigh pros/cons)
- SGLT2 inhibitors: benefits for heart failure and CKD in many patients, but require checking kidney function and have some side effects (genital infections, volume depletion).  
- GLP-1 receptor agonists: help with A1C and weight and have cardiovascular benefits in some people; injections or newer oral options—discuss retinopathy risk or rapid glucose changes if relevant.  
Ask your PCP whether these are appropriate based on your kidney function and cardiovascular history.

B. Retinopathy / eye care — given your history
- Confirm when your last ophthalmology/retina exam was. Diabetic retinopathy typically needs at least annual eye exams.  
- Ask: “Do I need an urgent eye visit before/after any diabetes medication changes?” (Some rapid A1C changes can temporarily affect eyes.)  
- Ensure documentation/coordination: ask Dr. Lee to confirm the plan for retina follow-up and any needed referrals.

C. Cardiovascular and other annual checks (important with hypertension and retinopathy)
Tests and targets
- BP goal to discuss (commonly <130/80 for many adults with diabetes/hypertension, individualized)  
- Lipid management and statin consideration based on ASCVD risk  
- CBC (anemia workup) — you mentioned anemia and that you take calcium & vitamin D (ask whether iron/B12 tests needed)  
- Smoking status, exercise, weight, and fall risk

Questions to ask Dr. Lee
- “Is my blood pressure target still the same given my retinopathy and overall risk?”  
- “Do I need changes to my lipid therapy or aspirin based on my overall cardiovascular risk?”  
- “My anemia is being managed with calcium and vitamin D — should we check iron studies, ferritin, B12, folate, or a GI workup?”  
- “Does omeprazole raise any concerns for long-term nutrient absorption (B12, magnesium, calcium) that we should monitor?”

D. Orthopedics — when a referral makes sense and what to ask
When to consider a referral to Northwest Orthopedics (or similar)
- Persistent joint pain that limits activities of daily living (walking, climbing stairs, dressing) despite conservative measures (NSAIDs, physical therapy, home exercise)  
- Progressive loss of function, instability, or recurrent falls  
- Mechanical symptoms (locking, catching), significant deformity, or suspected fracture  
- If imaging (X-ray/MRI) shows progressive osteoarthritis or structural problems and you want surgical options discussed

What to request from your PCP if you want a referral
- “I’ve had [describe symptoms: e.g., months of right knee pain limiting walking X blocks and climbing stairs], tried [PT/NSAIDs], and it hasn’t improved — can you refer me to Northwest Orthopedics for evaluation?”  
- Ask PCP to order plain X-rays of the affected joint(s) before the ortho visit, if appropriate (“Please order standing AP & lateral X-rays of both knees/hip as needed.”) — that speeds workup.  
- If you’re on blood thinners (clarify INR), ask about peri-procedure management if surgery could be considered.

Questions to ask the orthopedist (phrasing)
- “Given my age (52) and medical history (hypertension, diabetes risk/diagnosis), what non-surgical options should I try first?”  
- “Do I need imaging beyond X-rays (MRI) and what would that add?”  
- “If conservative care fails, what are the realistic outcomes and recovery times for procedures you might recommend?”  
- “Any special considerations for wound healing or infection risk given my medical history?”

E. Practical checklist to bring to the appointment
- Current medication list (prescription, OTC, supplements — dose and frequency)  
- Recent labs or values you know (A1C, creatinine/eGFR, lipids, last INR or anticoagulation info)  
- A brief symptom list (joint pain: location, onset, what makes it worse/better; vision changes; dizziness/falls)  
- Recent eye doctor report if you have it  
- Home BP or glucose logs, if available

F. Safety notes and clarifying your labs/meds
- If you truly have an INR of 320, that would indicate a lab unit error or a severe emergency — please clarify. If you are on warfarin and your INR is >10 or very high, contact medical care immediately. If you meant INR 3.20, that’s a high-ish therapeutic range and we should discuss anticoagulation management with your PCP.  
- Omeprazole can reduce absorption of B12 and magnesium and may contribute to anemia/low B12 over time — mention this to Dr. Lee.  
- Calcium + vitamin D do not treat anemia; if you are taking them for bone health, ask about a bone density (DEXA) scan (especially if female or other osteoporosis risk factors).

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short message/email to Dr. Lee summarizing these questions and requesting specific tests/referrals, or  
- Create a short one-page checklist you can print and bring to your appointment, or  
- Help you search your inbox for Dr. Lee’s contact info or past visit notes so you can reference earlier labs.

Which of those would be most useful right now? And please answer the clarifying questions so I can personalize the advice.

