Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is a great list to prepare before your annual physical. A few quick clarifying questions first so I give the most useful, accurate suggestions:
- Do you have an established diagnosis of type 2 diabetes (and if so, what are your most recent A1C and current diabetes medications)?
- Are you currently on anticoagulation (warfarin)? You wrote “INR was 320” — did you mean 3.20?
- Am I right that you take omeprazole daily for reflux? (It’s not a hypertension med.)
- Do you have joint pain or mobility problems now that are making you consider orthopedics, or is this a preventive question because of age/risk factors? Also, do you identify as female or male (for DEXA/osteoporosis risk guidance)?

While you answer, here are practical, clinician-ready questions and items to bring up at the visit, broken into diabetes-focused and orthopedics-focused sections — plus tests and red flags.

Questions and tests about type 2 diabetes management
- Confirm diagnosis and recent control
  - “Can we review my most recent A1C and what target A1C you recommend for me (given my age, comorbidities and retinopathy)?”
  - Ask for an A1C if none in the last 3 months, or point-of-care A1C at the visit.
- Glucose monitoring and hypoglycemia
  - “Should I be doing home blood glucose checks or using continuous glucose monitoring (CGM)?”
  - “What are the signs of low blood sugar I should watch for, and how should I treat them?”
- Medication review and cardiovascular/renal protection
  - “Given my history (hypertension, retinopathy, age), should we consider or continue an SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 receptor agonist for cardiovascular/renal benefit?”
  - “Any interactions or concerns with my current medicines (including anticoagulation or omeprazole)?”
- Kidney and urine testing
  - “Can we check kidney function (serum creatinine/eGFR) and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) to assess diabetic kidney disease?”
- Eye and foot care
  - “My chart notes retinopathy — is my ophthalmology follow-up schedule up to date? Do I need an urgent retinal specialist appointment?”
  - “When should I get a diabetic foot exam and what should I check at home?”
- Blood pressure, lipids, and aspirin
  - “What are my blood pressure and LDL cholesterol targets given my diabetes and age?”
  - “Do I need treatment changes or a statin/ACE inhibitor/ARB for risk reduction?”
- Vaccination and preventive care
  - “Are my vaccinations (flu, pneumococcal, Hep B, COVID boosters) current?”
- Lifestyle and referrals
  - “Would you recommend diabetes education, nutrition counseling, or a diabetes prevention/management program?”
- Monitoring schedule
  - “How often should we repeat A1C, lipids, kidney testing, and retinal exams?”

Tests to request (if not recently done)
- A1C (3-month control)
- Fasting lipid panel
- Serum creatinine with eGFR
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR)
- Basic metabolic panel (electrolytes)
- Urine or blood glucose as appropriate
- Ophthalmology (retina) visit if not seen within recommended interval

Orthopedics referral — do you need one?
When to consider a referral to Northwest Orthopedics (or an orthopedic surgeon):
- You have persistent joint pain that limits activities of daily living despite conservative care (NSAIDs, physical therapy, weight management).
- Symptoms of mechanical joint failure: frequent locking, instability, giving way, or significant swelling.
- Progressive functional decline (difficulty walking, climbing stairs) or loss of range of motion.
- Abnormal x-ray or suspicion of structural damage (torn meniscus, ligament injury, degenerative joint disease) that may require procedural/surgical evaluation.
- Suspected fracture, infection, or rapidly worsening symptoms.

Conservative options your PCP may try/authorize first
- Plain X-ray of the symptomatic joint (usually the first imaging step)
- Trial of physical therapy
- Weight management and activity modification
- Pain management: topical/oral meds, joint injection (steroid or hyaluronic acid) if appropriate
- If osteoporosis or fracture risk is a concern: DEXA scan

Questions to ask your PCP about orthopedics
- “Given my age and health history, do you recommend a DEXA scan for bone density?”
- “I’m noticing [describe symptoms: pain location, duration, triggers, functional limits] — should we get X-rays or refer to orthopedics now?”
- “Should I try a course of physical therapy first, or is a referral to orthopedics warranted?”
- “Are there red flags (e.g., fever, rapid worsening, numbness, bowel/bladder changes) that would require urgent imaging or referral?”

Red flags warranting urgent evaluation (contact PCP or urgent care)
- New neurological symptoms (numbness/weakness, loss of coordination)
- Severe, unrelenting pain not responding to medication
- Fever with a painful joint (possible infection)
- Signs of fracture after trauma

Other relevant points given your history
- Retinopathy: continue annual retinal eye exams; some diabetes meds may affect eye health indirectly (coordinate care with ophthalmology).
- INR/anticoagulation: confirm whether you are on warfarin and your therapeutic target. If your INR is 3.20 and you’re anticoagulated, that affects decisions around injections or procedures (e.g., joint injections, surgical referrals) — your PCP and orthopedist will need this info.
- Long-term PPI use (omeprazole) is associated with small increases in fracture risk — another reason to consider bone health screening if other risk factors exist.
- Calcium and vitamin D: useful for bone health; confirm doses and adherence.

What I can do next
- Make a concise one-page “talking points” sheet for your appointment with the exact questions above.
- Draft an email/message to Dr. Jennifer Lee at Harbor General requesting specific tests (A1C, UACR, lipids, creatinine) and asking whether she recommends ophthalmology follow-up or orthopedics referral — if you’d like that sent, tell me what tone and any extra details to include.
- Or prepare a checklist you can print/take to the visit.

Tell me:
- Answers to the clarifying questions above (diabetes diagnosis/A1C, anticoagulant and correct INR, current joint symptoms, gender).
- Whether you want a draft email/message to Dr. Lee or a printable visit checklist.

