Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
This is a great set of things to prepare before your annual physical. A few quick clarifying questions before I make a tailored checklist:
- Do you already have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes, or are you asking about management because of risk factors (family history, labs, symptoms)?  
- Are you taking an anticoagulant (warfarin) — you wrote “last INR was 320” which I suspect may mean 3.20 or is a typo. If you are on warfarin we should treat that as important to confirm before any procedures or changes.
- Any current joint pain, stiffness, swelling, or limitations that make you think about orthopedics now?

Below is a practical, visit-ready plan you can use at your appointment with Dr. Jennifer Lee (Harbor General), plus suggested questions to ask about diabetes management and whether to refer to Northwest Orthopedics.

Pre-visit checklist (what to bring/prepare)
- Current medication list (including OTCs, supplements: calcium, vitamin D, omeprazole). Note doses.
- Clarify anticoagulation: name of drug and most recent INR result (confirm whether 3.20). Bring any anticoagulation clinic contact info.
- Recent blood pressure readings (home logs) and any symptoms (dizziness, lightheadedness, hypoglycemia).
- Brief summary of vision status since you have retinopathy and date of last retina/ophthalmology visit.
- Notes about joint symptoms: location, onset, what makes it worse/better, how it limits activities.
- Any recent labs or imaging you’ve had (bring copies if available).

Suggested labs and tests to request before/at the visit
- HbA1c (or fasting glucose if A1c not available)
- Lipid panel (LDL, HDL, triglycerides)
- Basic metabolic panel (including creatinine, eGFR, electrolytes)
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) for kidney screening if diabetic/at risk
- CBC (to follow your anemia)
- Iron studies, ferritin, B12/folate if anemia not clarified
- Vitamin D level if you’re supplementing but unsure of adequacy
- Liver function tests
- If on warfarin: INR check and medication reconciliation
- Consider baseline ECG if cardiovascular risk factors are present
- Consider DEXA if you have osteoporosis risk factors or discuss bone health (age, medications, personal/family fracture history)

Key targets/management items to discuss for type 2 diabetes (questions to ask)
- Do I currently meet criteria for type 2 diabetes or prediabetes based on these results? (Ask Dr. Lee to review recent labs with you.)
- What is my individualized A1c target? Typical ranges are ~7% for many adults, but targets can be tighter or looser based on age, comorbidities, hypoglycemia risk, and life expectancy.
- Are my current medications appropriate, or should we consider adding/changing any diabetes medications? (Ask specifically about cardioprotective/renal-protective options such as SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 receptor agonists if you have cardiovascular or kidney risk.)
- What is my current cardiovascular risk and do I need a statin or aspirin? (Diabetes often changes lipid/ASCVD prevention strategy.)
- Any need for a referral to endocrinology or diabetes education? (Good reasons: A1c persistently above target despite meds, recurrent hypoglycemia, need for insulin initiation, complex regimen, or difficulty self-managing.)
- Screening and prevention: confirm annual retinal exam schedule (you have retinopathy so ophthalmology follow-up is important), foot exam, and microalbuminuria screening frequency.
- Would continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) or a structured diabetes education program benefit me?

Medication and safety questions
- Review interactions: is omeprazole appropriate to continue with my meds? (It’s for acid reflux—ask if any interactions affect mineral/vitamin absorption or other meds.)
- If you are on warfarin or another anticoagulant: how will this be managed for any procedures or if new meds are started that can change INR?
- For anemia: is current supplement regimen adequate? Should we check iron/ferritin or other causes?

When to consider referral to Northwest Orthopedics (questions & red flags)
Useful to consider orthopedic referral if any of the following apply:
- Persistent joint pain or stiffness that limits activities of daily living despite 6–12 weeks of conservative treatment (NSAIDs if appropriate, physical therapy, activity modification).
- Mechanical symptoms: locking, catching, giving way, or instability.
- Significant functional decline (difficulty walking, climbing stairs, dressing) or difficulty working.
- Imaging suggests severe structural disease (advanced osteoarthritis, severe deformity, fracture nonunion) — ask PCP to order plain X-ray of the symptomatic joint first.
- Progressive deformity or neurologic symptoms (numbness, radiating leg/arm pain with weakness) — urgent evaluation may be needed.

Questions to ask Dr. Lee about orthopedics referral
- Given my age and medical history, do you recommend conservative treatment first (PT, weight management, meds, injections), or is a referral to Northwest Orthopedics appropriate now?
- Would you like an X-ray or other imaging before an ortho referral (X-ray first; MRI if soft tissue suspected)?
- Are there any modifiable risk factors I should address first (weight loss plan, optimize blood pressure/blood sugar, smoking cessation)?
- If I'm on anticoagulation, what precautions should we take before any injections/procedures?

Red flags to mention or act on right away
- Sudden, severe joint swelling with fever or inability to bear weight (seek urgent care).
- New sudden vision changes (because of retinopathy risk).
- Signs of severe anemia (syncope, chest pain, severe shortness of breath) or a very high INR if anticoagulated — confirm INR value and contact your anticoag clinic/PCP urgently if it’s out of range.

Practical next steps I can help with
- I can draft a concise message/email to Dr. Lee summarizing: (1) your medical history (HTN, anemia, retinopathy), (2) clarification about anticoagulation/INR, (3) request for pre-visit labs (A1c, UACR, CBC, iron studies, CMP, lipids, INR), and (4) ask whether she thinks endocrinology or orthopedics referrals are warranted now. Would you like me to draft that?
- Or I can format a one-page “questions to ask” sheet you can bring to the visit.

Would you like me to:
A) Draft an email to Dr. Lee now?  
B) Create a printable visit checklist / question sheet for your appointment?  
C) Or both?

Also please confirm: do you have a formal diagnosis of type 2 diabetes now, and are you on any anticoagulant (warfarin, DOAC)? And did you mean INR 3.20?

