I can help you prepare. First, a couple quick clarifying questions before I make a tailored checklist and suggested questions to bring to Dr. Lee:

- Did you mean your last INR was 3.20 (not 320)? Are you taking an anticoagulant (warfarin)?  
- Do you have a confirmed diagnosis of type 2 diabetes already, and if so, what is your most recent A1c (and any recent fasting glucose readings)?  
- Any current joint pain, stiffness, falls, or functional limitations that are prompting you to consider orthopedics?

While you answer, here’s a focused, practical guide you can take to your annual physical with Dr. Jennifer Lee at Harbor General.

What to bring / prepare for the visit
- A current medication list (drug name, dose, frequency), including OTC supplements (you mentioned omeprazole, calcium, vitamin D). Note any allergies.  
- Recent lab results (A1c, CMP, lipid panel, CBC, INR) or dates if done elsewhere.  
- Blood pressure log if you measure at home and any home blood glucose log (if you monitor).  
- A short symptom list: vision changes, numbness/tingling in feet, new joint pain, decreased mobility, falls, fatigue, bleeding or easy bruising.  
- Questions you want answered (see suggested scripts below).

Diabetes management — important topics & suggested questions
(Assumes you have type 2 diabetes; if not, substitute “screening” questions)

Clinical monitoring and labs to confirm/update
- Ask for/update these tests: A1c, fasting glucose, CMP (kidney & liver), lipid panel, urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (ACR), and an annual foot exam.  
  Suggested question: “Can we get an A1c, CMP, lipid panel, and urine ACR today so we can check blood sugar control and kidney status?”

Targets and medication review
- Typical A1c targets are individualized (commonly ~7.0% for many adults, relaxed depending on age/comorbidities).  
  Suggested question: “What A1c target should I aim for given my age (52), hypertension, anemia, and retinopathy?”  
- Medication review: ask whether metformin is still first-line (if not already on it), and whether SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 receptor agonists would be appropriate because of cardiovascular/renal benefits (discuss risks and benefits).  
  Suggested question: “Given my other conditions and risk profile, should we consider starting or changing any diabetes medicines (metformin, SGLT2, GLP‑1, insulin)? What are the pros and cons for me?”

Retinopathy and vision considerations
- Because you have a history of retinopathy, emphasize timely eye care: annual dilated retinal exam (or more frequent if active disease). Rapid changes in glucose or starting insulin can sometimes transiently affect retinopathy — coordinate with your ophthalmologist.  
  Suggested question: “My chart shows retinopathy — is my diabetes control adequate for eye health, and should I see my retina specialist before we change my diabetes medicines?”

Complication prevention and counseling
- Foot care and neuropathy screening: monofilament exam, vibration testing if indicated. If any numbness/ulcers, ask about podiatry referral.  
  Suggested question: “Do I need a podiatry referral or neuropathy screening today?”  
- Vaccinations: confirm influenza, pneumococcal, and shingles (age-appropriate).  
  Suggested question: “Am I up to date on vaccines recommended for people with diabetes?”

Lifestyle, monitoring, and referrals
- Diet, weight management, exercise, sleep, alcohol/smoking counseling.  
  Suggested question: “What self-monitoring should I do at home (BG checks, BP checks), and how often should we follow up on A1c and labs?”

Medication interactions & safety
- Mention omeprazole and calcium/vit D so Dr. Lee can reconcile all meds and supplements. Ask about interactions with diabetes meds or anticoagulants.  
  Suggested question: “Are any of my current meds or supplements interacting with diabetes drugs or affecting lab tests?”

Orthopedics referral — when it may be needed and what to ask
When to consider a referral to orthopedics
- Persistent joint pain or stiffness that limits daily activities despite conservative care (months-long), mechanical symptoms (locking, catching), acute injuries, suspected fractures, progressive deformity, or failure of conservative therapy (PT, injections, meds).  
- Age 52 in itself is not an automatic reason to refer, but comorbidities (diabetes, retinopathy, osteoporosis risk) raise concern for bone/joint problems and slower healing.

Questions to ask Dr. Lee about orthopedics
- “I’m 52 with diabetes, hypertension, and a history of retinopathy — do you think I need an orthopedics referral for my [hip/knee/back/shoulder] pain (if applicable)?”  
- “Should I get X‑rays or a DEXA (bone density) before a referral? Am I at increased risk of osteoporosis or fracture?”  
- “If I need a joint evaluation, would Northwest Orthopedics be the right place given my age and medical history? Do you recommend any specific orthopedist there?”  
- “If I’m on anticoagulation (confirm warfarin/INR), how would that affect imaging or procedures?”

Tests/assessments orthopedics will likely want
- Targeted X‑rays of the painful joint, possibly MRI if soft-tissue issue suspected.  
- DEXA scan if you’re at risk for osteoporosis (postmenopausal status, low body weight, history of fragility fracture, chronic steroid use). Given you’re 52, ask if a DEXA is indicated now.  
- Functional assessment (range of motion, gait, ADLs) and conservative therapy trial (PT, injections) before surgery in many cases.

Red flags for urgent referral
- Sudden inability to bear weight, deformity, signs of infection (fever, severe redness), progressive neurologic deficits (numbness/weakness), or acute severe pain after trauma.

Other items to address at the annual physical
- Clarify your INR and anticoagulation plan if you’re on warfarin — that affects bleeding risk for procedures and orthopedics. If your INR is 3.20, ask whether that level is intentional for your indication and whether it needs tighter control.  
- Check CBC for anemia (you mentioned anemia); consider iron studies, B12, folate depending on results.  
- Bone health: calcium/vitamin D are helpful but ask about DEXA and fall prevention measures.  
- Screening up to date: mammogram, colon cancer screening, cervical screening as appropriate for age and history.

Suggested scripts you can use at the appointment
- “Can we review my diabetes control (A1c) and kidney function today and discuss whether I need a medication change for cardio‑renal protection?”  
- “I have a history of retinopathy—should we coordinate with ophthalmology before intensifying glucose-lowering therapy?”  
- “I’ve been having [describe joint symptoms] — should I try PT/X‑rays or get an orthopedic referral now? If a referral is indicated, do you recommend Northwest Orthopedics?”

Would you like:
- A one-page printout of these questions formatted for the visit?  
- A drafted message/email to Dr. Lee summarizing these items and requesting labs/referrals (I can draft that and leave it as a draft or send it now if you want)?

If you confirm your INR and whether you have an established diabetes diagnosis (and latest A1c), I’ll make the list even more specific and can draft the exact wording to send to Dr. Lee.

