Great — I can help you prepare. Below I’ll (A) flag a few details I want you to confirm, (B) give a focused list of diabetes-management questions and tests to bring up with Dr. Jennifer Lee, and (C) lay out when an orthopedic referral to Northwest Orthopedics makes sense and what to ask. I’ll finish with a short checklist you can bring to the visit and offer to draft an email or message to your PCP if you want.

A — quick clarifications (please confirm)
- You wrote “hypertension (omeprazole 20 mg daily)”: omeprazole is a PPI (acid reflux), not a blood-pressure medicine. What blood-pressure medication(s) are you actually taking?
- You wrote “anemia (calcium and vitamin D)”: calcium & vitamin D are for bone health — are you taking any iron, B12, folate, or other treatments for anemia?
- Last INR “320”: do you mean 3.20 (INR 3.2)? If you’re on warfarin or another anticoagulant, confirming the target INR and anticoagulant name will be important.

B — Questions and topics to bring up about type 2 diabetes management
(Use the ones most relevant to you; you can read them aloud or hand the list to Dr. Lee.)

Diagnosis/control and targets
- What A1c target should I aim for given my age (52), hypertension, retinopathy, and overall health? (E.g., <7% vs. a more relaxed target)
- When was my last A1c and fasting glucose? Can we check A1c at this visit?

Medication review and options
- Based on kidney function and cardiovascular risk, is metformin appropriate for me (if not already taking it)? If I am on metformin, is my dose optimized?
- Should I be on an SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 receptor agonist for cardiovascular/renal protection given my risk factors?
- If my sugars aren’t controlled, when would you refer me to endocrinology?
- Are there interactions between my current meds (including omeprazole and any anticoagulants) and diabetes medicines?

Monitoring for complications
- Please order these tests now (or confirm last results):
  - A1c
  - Basic metabolic panel (BMP) or CMP (to check kidney function / electrolytes)
  - eGFR and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (ACR) for diabetic kidney disease
  - Lipid panel
  - Foot exam / monofilament for neuropathy
  - Dilated retinal exam referral or confirmation of retinal follow-up (given retinopathy)
- How often should I get these repeated?

Lifestyle and self-monitoring
- Do you recommend home blood-glucose monitoring or continuous glucose monitoring for me? If so, what targets and frequency?
- Any specific diet, exercise, or weight-loss goals you recommend?
- Are there specific blood-pressure and lipid targets given my diabetes?

Cardiovascular, kidney, and eye protection
- Should I be on a statin or aspirin for primary/secondary prevention?
- Should I start or continue an ACE inhibitor or ARB for kidney protection / BP control?
- Can you coordinate an updated ophthalmology appointment for monitoring retinopathy?

Vaccines and other preventive care
- Which vaccines do I need (flu, pneumococcal, shingles, COVID boosters)?
- Any other screenings recommended now (colon, mammogram, bone density)?

Safety and side effects
- If you start an SGLT2 or GLP-1, what side effects should I watch for (UTIs, genital infections, GI effects)?
- How to recognize/treat hypoglycemia and when to call you?

C — Thinking through an orthopedic referral (Northwest Orthopedics)
When a referral is typically appropriate
- Persistent joint pain or stiffness that limits daily activities after 6–8 weeks of conservative care (rest, NSAIDs if safe, physical therapy, activity modification).
- Pain that interferes with sleep, walking, work, or exercise.
- Mechanical symptoms (locking, catching, giving way), visible deformity, or suspected fracture.
- Progressive loss of function or weakness, or neurological symptoms (numbness, tingling, progressive leg/arm weakness).
- Imaging concerns: abnormal or progressive findings on X-ray/MRI that need surgical or specialist evaluation.
- For osteoporosis concern (you’re 52 and taking calcium + vitamin D): if you have risk factors (low-trauma fracture, prolonged steroid use, family history), consider a DEXA scan — orthopedics may co-manage fractures or advanced joint disease but a PCP or endocrinologist/geriatrician can order DEXA as well.

Questions to ask your PCP about orthopedics
- I have [describe location: knee/hip/back/shoulder] pain for [duration]. Can you examine me and order X-ray (and/or MRI) if needed?
- Should I try a structured course of physical therapy first? If so, for how long before considering referral?
- Given my diabetes and retinopathy, are there extra infection/wound-healing risks you want the orthopedic team to know about?
- If imaging shows osteoarthritis, what conservative measures should we try, and when would you refer me to Northwest Orthopedics?
- Should I get a DEXA scan for bone density given age, calcium/vitamin D use, and any other risk factors?

Tests and trials before referral
- Plain X-rays of the symptomatic joint(s) (weight-bearing knees, hips, spine as appropriate).
- Short trial (6–8 weeks) of physical therapy, targeted exercise, and weight management if applicable.
- Consider analgesic/anti-inflammatory plan (avoid NSAIDs if kidney disease or on anticoagulant — discuss with PCP).
- DEXA scan if concern for low bone density.

D — Medication and lab points to bring up now
- Confirm antihypertensive medication (name and dose) and current BP readings at home.
- If you’re on warfarin: confirm target INR and that the last INR was indeed 3.2; ask whether omeprazole could affect INR or interact with any meds you may start.
- Get current labs: CBC (for anemia), iron studies, B12/folate, A1c, BMP/CMP, eGFR, urine ACR, lipid panel, and INR if on warfarin.
- Clarify anemia management — if you’re still anemic, ask about causes and appropriate treatment (iron, B12, etc.).

E — Quick “scripted” questions you can read to Dr. Lee
- “Given my retinopathy, hypertension, and age, what should my A1c target be and which diabetes medication would you recommend next if lifestyle changes aren’t enough?”
- “Can we check A1c, CMP, eGFR, urine ACR, lipid panel, CBC, iron studies, and an up-to-date INR today?”
- “Do you recommend referral to endocrinology for complex medication changes or if A1c remains above target after X months?”
- “I have [describe joint pain]. Could you evaluate and order X-rays? If symptoms persist, would you refer me to Northwest Orthopedics or start PT first?”
- “Should I be concerned about omeprazole interacting with my anticoagulant or other meds?”

F — What to bring to the appointment
- Current full medication list (prescription, OTC, supplements — esp. omeprazole, calcium, vitamin D, iron/B12 if used).
- Recent home blood pressure and glucose logs (last 2–4 weeks).
- Any recent lab results you have.
- Brief symptom notes: when joint pain started, what makes it better/worse, limitations.
- This checklist or a printed copy of the key questions above.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a concise message or email to Dr. Lee summarizing these issues and requesting specific tests/referrals.
- Turn this into a one-page printable checklist you can bring to the visit.
- Help you phrase the joint-symptom description for the orthopedic referral.

Which would you like me to do next? And please confirm the medication/INR details so I can tailor things precisely.

