I can help — this is a good time to get organized before your annual. A few quick clarifying questions so I tailor the recommendations correctly:
- Do you have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes now? If so, what is your most recent A1c and what diabetes medications (if any) are you taking?
- You listed hypertension and “omeprazole 20 mg daily.” Omeprazole is a PPI (for reflux) — what blood‑pressure medicine are you taking?
- You wrote “last INR was 320.” Do you mean 3.20 (INR) or something else (e.g., a lab value measured in different units)? Are you taking warfarin or another anticoagulant?
- For retinopathy: is this diabetic retinopathy? When was your last eye exam?
- For anemia: what is the cause (iron deficiency, chronic disease, etc.) and are you taking iron or B12/folate supplements in addition to calcium/vitamin D?

Below are two focused sections: (A) questions and tests to cover about type 2 diabetes management at your visit, and (B) how to think about whether you need an orthopedics referral to Northwest Orthopedics.

A. Diabetes — what to ask, check, and request
Essential baseline items to have/ask
- Confirm diagnosis and most recent A1c. Ask: “What is my current A1c and what should my individualized A1c target be given my age, retinopathy, and other conditions?”
- Current glucose monitoring: ask whether you should check fasting, post‑prandial, or use a continuous glucose monitor.
- Medications: review current diabetes drugs, side effects, weight changes, kidney function limits, and hypoglycemia risk. Ask whether you should consider adding/ switching to SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP‑1 receptor agonists (cardio/renal benefits) depending on cardiac/renal status.
- Cardiovascular risk: confirm statin use and blood‑pressure targets (often <130/80 mmHg for people with diabetes, individualized).
- Kidney screening: urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) and serum creatinine / eGFR. Ask: “Is my kidney function stable and do I need nephrology or med changes?”
- Retinopathy: ask whether your eye exam frequency is up to date; if retinopathy present, ask for ophthalmology follow‑up this year.
- Neuropathy and feet: request foot exam and monofilament testing; ask about symptoms of neuropathy.
- Immunizations: influenza, pneumococcal (if indicated), and shingles (Shingrix) if not already done.
- Lifestyle and referral: ask about referrals to diabetes education, nutrition/diabetes educator, or exercise program.
- Hypoglycemia safety: ask how to recognize and what to do; if on insulin/sulfonylureas, discuss rescue plan.

Suggested labs to request at the annual:
- A1c
- BMP (electrolytes, glucose, creatinine) and eGFR
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR)
- Lipid panel
- CBC (to follow anemia)
- Iron studies / ferritin / B12 / folate if anemia unexplained
- LFTs if starting/changing many diabetes meds
- INR or coagulation panel if you are on warfarin (clarify the INR value)

Practical things to bring or track to the visit:
- List of all medications (including doses), supplements (calcium, vitamin D), and recent home BP and glucose logs.
- Any recent eye or kidney reports.
- Symptoms to report: dizziness, fainting, syncope, falls, neuropathy, vision changes, swelling, chest pain.

B. Orthopedics — when a referral makes sense and what to ask
Referral is usually based on symptoms and function, not age alone. Questions to consider:
- Do you have persistent joint/back/hip/knee pain limiting daily activities or sleep?
- Has the pain lasted >3 months or progressively worsened despite conservative care (NSAIDs as appropriate, physical therapy, activity modification)?
- Any mechanical symptoms (locking, giving way), deformity, or recurrent instability?
- Any prior imaging (X‑ray, MRI) — if not, PCP can usually order plain X‑rays first.

When to consider a referral to orthopedics (or at least get imaging)
- Persistent moderate-to-severe pain limiting function after conservative treatment (PT, meds, injections)
- Progressive functional loss (cannot walk/climb stairs, cannot work)
- Mechanical instability, deformity, or suspicion of fracture
- X‑ray findings of moderate–severe osteoarthritis and you want surgical options discussed
- Recurrent falls, suspected bone fragility, or prior fragility fracture (then also evaluate bone health / DEXA)

Specific questions to ask your PCP if you’re thinking of Northwest Orthopedics:
- “Given my age and medical history (hypertension, anemia, retinopathy, possible diabetes), do you recommend I see orthopedics for [site of pain — e.g., right knee]? If so, should I get X‑rays or MRI first?”
- “Have I exhausted conservative measures (PT, injections)? If not, can we start those?”
- “Do I need a DEXA scan for bone density screening given I’m 52 and taking calcium/vitamin D?”
- “Are there any increased surgical risks in me (e.g., from anticoagulation, uncontrolled diabetes) that we should consider before a referral?”

Other relevant points for your annual visit
- Bone health: at 52, consider DEXA screening if risk factors (family history, early menopause, fragility fractures). Calcium/vitamin D are supportive but DEXA gives objective risk.
- Anticoagulation/surgery planning: if your INR or anticoagulation is in play (you mentioned INR), clarify anticoagulant, target INR, and bleeding risk before any invasive procedures.
- Coordinate retinopathy care with diabetes control — stricter glucose control and regular ophthalmology follow‑up matter.
- Falls risk and home safety if neuropathy/vision issues are present.

Next steps I can help with
- Draft a concise message or visit checklist to send to Dr. Jennifer Lee summarizing these questions and asking for specific labs/referrals.
- Create a one‑page list you can print and take to the appointment.
- Help draft a referral request to Northwest Orthopedics if you decide you want one.

Tell me how you’d like to proceed (e.g., answer the clarifying questions above, or I can draft a message to Dr. Lee with the suggested labs and questions).

