I can help you prepare a focused list for your annual physical. First, a couple of quick clarifying questions so I tailor this precisely:
- Do you identify as female or male (so I can include sex-specific screening reminders)?  
- What diabetes medications (if any) are you currently taking, and what was your last A1C (if you know)?  
- Are you on an anticoagulant such as warfarin (that would explain an INR)? You wrote “last INR was 320” — did you mean 3.20?

If you prefer, I can proceed without those details. Below is a compact, visit-ready set of questions, tests to request, and notes about the orthopedics referral.

Questions to ask Dr. Jennifer Lee about type 2 diabetes management
- Current status and targets
  - What should my individualized A1C target be, given my age, retinopathy, hypertension, and other conditions?
  - How often should we check A1C going forward?
- Medications and safety
  - Can we review my full medication list (I take omeprazole 20 mg daily; please check for interactions or side effects)?
  - Are my current diabetes medications optimal for preventing complications (retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy) and for my cardiovascular risk?
  - Do any of my meds increase hypoglycemia risk and how should I prevent/manage it?
- Blood pressure and lipids
  - What is my blood pressure goal (target for people with diabetes is often <130/80 depending on circumstances)?
  - Should I be on or change statin therapy given my age and diabetes?
- Kidney and eye monitoring
  - Do you recommend urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) and serum creatinine/eGFR now?
  - I have a history of retinopathy — is my retinal screening current and should I see ophthalmology yearly?
- Neuropathy and foot care
  - Should I have a routine foot exam / monofilament testing at each visit? Any referral to podiatry?
- Vaccinations and preventative care
  - Which immunizations do you recommend now (influenza, Tdap, shingles, pneumococcal, COVID boosters, Hep B if applicable)?
- Lifestyle and referrals
  - Are there diet, exercise, or weight-loss programs you recommend (diabetes education, lifestyle clinic)?
  - Should we consider referral to endocrine/diabetes specialist if control or complications persist?
- Labs and monitoring frequency
  - How often should I repeat A1C, CMP, lipid panel, TSH (if indicated), and B12/iron studies given my anemia and PPI use?

Questions and phrasing about anticoagulation/INR
- I have a recorded INR of “320” — can we confirm the correct INR value and review whether I’m on warfarin or another anticoagulant and what my target INR should be?
- If I’m on warfarin, can we review recent INRs and bleeding/thrombotic risk and consider alternatives if appropriate?

Should you ask for an orthopedics referral (Northwest Orthopedics)?
Useful to ask Dr. Lee:
- I’m age 52 with diabetes, retinopathy (vision issues increase fall risk) and bone-health supplements — given my age and risk factors, do you recommend a referral to Northwest Orthopedics?
- Do you recommend imaging (x-ray) or a DEXA bone density scan first?
- If I have persistent joint pain, functional limitations, mechanical symptoms (locking/catching), instability, or falls, would you refer to orthopedics or try physiotherapy first?
- If I have pain limiting daily activities despite conservative care, what criteria would trigger a direct orthopedics referral?

When orthopedics is appropriate vs alternatives
- Appropriate for: persistent severe pain, progressive loss of function, suspected structural damage (torn meniscus, advanced osteoarthritis), deformity, instability, or failed conservative treatment.
- Alternatives to request first: physical therapy, targeted imaging (X-ray, MRI if needed), rheumatology if inflammatory disease suspected, pain management, or a falls-risk assessment/occupational therapy if vision/balance are issues.
- If bone health is a concern, request a DEXA (especially if you have risk factors for osteoporosis) — orthopedics may be less helpful for metabolic bone disease than primary care/endocrinology.

Pre-visit checklist — labs and items to bring up
- Labs to request (if not recent): A1C, fasting lipid panel, CMP (including creatinine, electrolytes), urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio, CBC (investigate anemia), iron studies/ferritin, vitamin B12 (PPIs can lower B12), vitamin D level if concerned, TSH if clinically indicated, and confirm INR if you’re on anticoagulation.
- Tests/screens to confirm: dilated retinal exam within last year, foot exam, DEXA if risk factors for osteoporosis, colon cancer screening status, mammogram/PAP/PSA per your sex and history.
- Bring: an up-to-date medication list (dose/frequency), home blood pressure and glucose logs (if you check at home), notes about symptoms (joint pain severity, falls, vision changes, numbness/tingling), and any questions prioritized.

Specific phrasing you can use during the visit
- “Given my diabetes and retinopathy, what is our A1C goal and how will we adjust meds if I’m not at goal?”
- “My last INR reading is listed as ‘320’ — can we confirm the correct value and review anticoagulation management?”
- “I’ve been taking calcium and vitamin D; do you recommend a DEXA scan now? Any other bone-health measures?”
- “I’ve had [describe joint pain or functional limits] — would you refer me to Northwest Orthopedics, or should we do X-ray/try PT first?”

Safety/other considerations to mention
- PPI use (omeprazole) can affect B12 absorption — consider checking B12 if you have anemia or neuropathy.
- Diabetes increases risk for cardiovascular disease — ask specifically about statin use, aspirin only if appropriate.
- Retinopathy: ensure yearly ophthalmology follow-up and report vision changes promptly.
- Fall risk: with retinopathy and any neuropathy, consider balance/falls screening and home safety review.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short message/email you can send to Dr. Lee summarizing these points and requesting specific labs and referrals?  
- Produce a printable one-page checklist you can bring to the appointment?

Tell me which option you prefer (and answer the quick clarifying questions if you want the most tailored version).

