Great — I can help you get organized for your annual physical. Below I’ve put together (A) a checklist of information to have ready, (B) specific questions to ask about type 2 diabetes management (and why each matters), (C) what to discuss about a referral to Northwest Orthopedics (when it’s reasonable and what to request), and (D) a short suggested message you can send or say to Dr. Jennifer Lee. Before I finalize anything, a few quick clarifying questions are below.

A. Quick clarifying questions
- Do you have a formal diagnosis of type 2 diabetes already? If so, what is your current A1c and most recent blood glucose values?
- Which diabetes medications (if any) are you taking now?
- Are you currently on anticoagulation (warfarin or others)? You mentioned “INR was 320” — do you mean INR 3.20, and if so, are you on warfarin?
- What sex do you identify as? (This helps with screening timing, bone-health recommendations.)
- Do you have any joint pain, stiffness, or functional limitations right now? If yes, where and how long?

B. What to bring to the visit / have available
- Current medication list (prescription, OTC, supplements — doses and frequency). Include omeprazole, calcium, vitamin D, and any anticoagulants.
- Recent lab results (A1c, fasting glucose, lipid panel, creatinine/eGFR, urine albumin if available, last INR result).
- Date of last retinal/ophthalmology exam.
- A short symptom list (energy, thirst, urination changes, neuropathy symptoms, foot problems, joint pain).
- List of specific questions/concerns you want addressed.

C. Diabetes management — questions to ask and why
1. “What is my current A1c and target A1c for my age/comorbidities?”
   - Why: target A1c can differ by age, comorbidities, hypoglycemia risk, and life expectancy.

2. “Should we check A1c today and repeat basic labs: fasting glucose, lipid panel, creatinine/eGFR, and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio?”
   - Why: these guide med choices (kidney function affects dosing) and assess cardiovascular & kidney risk.

3. “Based on my A1c and other risks, should we change or add diabetes medications (metformin, SGLT2 inhibitor, GLP-1 RA, insulin, etc.)?”
   - Why: newer agents (SGLT2, GLP-1) have heart and kidney benefits for many patients; need eGFR info and discussion of side effects/cost.

4. “Given my history of retinopathy, when should I next see ophthalmology and do I need tighter glycemic control?”
   - Why: retinopathy requires regular retinal exams and glucose control can slow progression.

5. “Can we screen for neuropathy and do a foot exam today? Should I receive education on foot care?”
   - Why: neuropathy and foot problems are common and preventable with screening.

6. “Are my blood pressure and lipid goals appropriate? Do I need adjustment in antihypertensives or start/continue a statin/aspirin?”
   - Why: BP and lipids are major drivers of diabetes-related complications.

7. “Any medication interactions or safety concerns with my other meds (e.g., anticoagulant, omeprazole, supplements)?”
   - Why: important to reconcile interactions, especially if on warfarin.

8. “Do I need vaccinations (flu, COVID, pneumococcal, shingles) or referral to diabetes education/registered dietitian?”
   - Why: immunizations and lifestyle support reduce complications.

D. Tests and monitoring to request if not recent
- A1c (if >3 months since last)
- Lipid panel
- Basic metabolic panel / creatinine / eGFR
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (microalbumin)
- Complete metabolic panel if on SGLT2/GLP-1 or insulin
- Annual retinal exam (or referral) and foot exam
- Blood pressure check and weight/BMI
- Consider EKG if cardiac risk high or symptomatic

E. Orthopedics referral — what to consider and suggested questions
When to consider a referral to orthopedics or to ask your PCP to refer:
- Persistent joint pain, functional limitation, or loss of mobility that interferes with daily activities despite conservative measures (rest, NSAIDs if appropriate, home exercises).
- Mechanical symptoms: locking, catching, instability, or deformity.
- Progressive pain or disability despite a trial of physical therapy and conservative care.
- Neurologic signs: worsening numbness, weakness, or bowel/bladder changes.
- Suspected significant structural disease (advanced osteoarthritis, torn tendon/ligament, severe rotator cuff tear).

What to ask Dr. Lee:
- “Given my age and risk factors, would you recommend imaging (X‑ray, MRI) for my [knee/shoulder/back/etc.] pain?”
- “Should I try a trial of physical therapy first before orthopedics, or is referral to Northwest Orthopedics appropriate now?”
- “Do I have risk factors for osteoporosis or fracture that would make a DEXA scan appropriate?”
- “Are there non-surgical options (PT, injections) we should try first? If so, for how long before referring?”

When PCP referral makes sense vs. PCP management:
- PCP can initiate conservative care (PT referral, home exercise program, NSAIDs if safe, lifestyle and weight-loss counseling) and order plain X-rays.
- Refer to orthopedics if conservative care fails (typically 6–12 weeks depending on severity), imaging suggests surgically treatable pathology, or there are red flags (instability, neuro deficit).

F. Red flags (seek timely medical attention or request expedited evaluation)
- Sudden severe joint swelling/redness/fever (possible infection).
- New weakness or numbness that’s worsening or affecting walking.
- New visual changes (with retinopathy history).
- Signs of bleeding or very high/low INR if on anticoagulation (easy bruising, severe bleeding, black stools).

G. Suggested short message to Dr. Jennifer Lee (you can copy/paste, edit, or ask me to draft an email/draft message)
