While you reply, here are organized items you can use at your visit and questions to ask Dr. Jennifer Lee. I’ll also include a ready-to-send draft email to your PCP (Harbor General) if you want me to send or save as a draft.

1) Key diabetes-related things to check / ask (if you have or may have T2D)
- Screening / baseline labs
  - A1c (or fasting glucose if A1c not available)
  - Basic metabolic panel (BMP) for kidney function and electrolytes (eGFR)
  - Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (microalbumin) for early kidney disease
  - Lipid panel (ASCVD risk)
  - Liver tests if starting some diabetes meds
- Management questions to ask
  - What is my individualized A1c target given my age, comorbidities (retinopathy, HTN), and hypoglycemia risk?
  - Based on my last A1c and kidney function, should I start metformin or another agent? (If you already take meds, ask whether escalation/change is needed.)
  - Should I be considered for an SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 receptor agonist for cardiovascular/renal protection given age and HTN (if appropriate)?
  - Are there reasons to avoid any diabetes drugs given my retinopathy or other conditions?
  - How often should I have A1c testing and urine albumin testing going forward?
  - Do you recommend home glucose monitoring or continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) for me?
  - Are there lifestyle programs (diabetes education, dietician, weight management) you recommend or can refer me to?
  - Do I need vaccination updates (influenza, pneumococcal, hepatitis B) in the context of diabetes risk?
- Eye & foot care
  - Because you have a history of retinopathy, ask whether you need an updated retinal exam (often annually) and whether tighter glucose control or medication changes could affect your eyes.
  - Ask about a foot exam and footwear advice if neuropathy is a concern.

2) Labs and screening you may want done around the visit
- A1c
- BMP (eGFR, electrolytes)
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio
- Lipid panel
- CBC (you mentioned anemia)
- Vitamin D level, calcium (since you take supplements)
- Thyroid function if clinically indicated
- Clarify and repeat INR if you are anticoagulated
- Consider DEXA (bone density) screening if you have risk factors for osteoporosis (age, low body weight, prolonged steroid use, family history, etc.)

3) Orthopedics — when a referral to Northwest Orthopedics makes sense
Questions to consider (to tell your PCP or ask yourself):
- What symptoms do you have? (Location: hips/knees/shoulders/spine; pain severity; stiffness; decreased range of motion; instability; falls; difficulty with ADLs)
- How long have symptoms lasted? (Referral more likely for persistent pain >6–12 weeks that limits function)
- Have you already tried conservative measures? (NSAIDs if safe, physical therapy, home exercise, weight loss, activity modification, bracing, topical treatments)
- Is there progressive deformity, suspected fracture, or neuro symptoms (numbness, weakness, bowel/bladder changes)?
- Are you on anticoagulation or bleeding risk? (Surgeons need to know for imaging or procedures.)
When to ask for a referral:
- Persistent significant pain or functional limitation despite conservative care
- Suspected structural problem on exam or X-ray (e.g., osteoarthritis with joint space loss)
- Recurrent instability or mechanical symptoms (locking, catching)
- If you want a surgical opinion for joint replacement or interventional options
What your PCP can do first:
- Order targeted X-ray(s) of the painful joint(s) (often enough to decide if referral is needed)
- Trial of physical therapy and analgesics
- DEXA if bone health is a concern (age 52 — screening depends on sex/menopausal status and other risk factors)
- Refer to orthopedics when imaging + symptoms suggest surgical or specialist management

4) Retinopathy and interactions with diabetes management
- Rapid glucose lowering can sometimes transiently worsen retinopathy — important to coordinate with your ophthalmologist before major medication changes.
- Ensure you have a current retinal exam (annual check is common for diabetic retinopathy).
- If starting GLP-1 receptor agonists or other agents, discuss with PCP/endocrinologist any eye-specific considerations.

5) Practical checklist to bring to your annual physical
- Current med list (including over‑the‑counter and supplements) and doses
- Recent home BP readings if you track them
- Any joint pain notes: onset, location, what worsens/helps, prior treatments
- Any bleeding/bruising or anticoagulant history
- Date of last eye exam and any notes from ophthalmologist
- Questions for PCP: diabetes screening/management, need for endocrinology referral, repeat INR, order labs above, consider DEXA, and whether to refer to Northwest Orthopedics

6) Suggested email to Dr. Jennifer Lee (Harbor General)
