Quick clarifying items to check before the visit
- Confirm your current medications (name, dose, frequency). You mentioned omeprazole 20 mg and calcium + vitamin D — please confirm any other meds (especially blood pressure meds, diabetes meds, iron or B12, and any blood thinners).  
- Clarify the INR: “last INR was 320” looks like a typo — do you mean 3.20? If you are on warfarin (or another anticoagulant), note the indication and your target INR.  
- Tell me your sex (male/female) and whether you have symptoms you want to mention (joint pain, mobility limits, neuropathy, vision changes, falls).

Questions and topics to cover about type 2 diabetes management
- Current status & targets
  - “What should my A1c target be at 52, given my retinopathy and other health issues?” (Typical individualized targets: often <7% for many adults but looser or tighter depending on comorbidities and hypoglycemia risk.)
  - “Is my current regimen meeting that target? If not, what are the next steps?”
- Recent testing to request (if not already done)
  - A1c (if not within last 3 months)
  - Fasting glucose or continuous glucose data if helpful
  - Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (microalbumin) and serum creatinine/eGFR (kidney screening)
  - Lipid panel (fasting or non-fasting)
  - Liver function tests and basic metabolic panel (if considering new diabetes meds)
  - Foot exam / monofilament testing for neuropathy
  - Retinal exam status (ask whether ophthalmology follow-up is due)
- Medication questions
  - “Given my retinopathy and cardiovascular risk, should I be on metformin (if not already) as first-line?” (Metformin is first-line unless contraindicated.)
  - “Do I qualify for an SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 receptor agonist for heart/kidney/weight benefits?” (These are often recommended if there’s ASCVD, CKD, or high CV risk.)
  - “Are there any diabetes drugs you would avoid given my retinopathy or other conditions?” (Rapid A1c lowering can sometimes worsen retinopathy; discuss careful monitoring.)
  - “If my A1c remains high, when should we consider adding/starting insulin vs. other agents?”
- Cardiovascular and kidney risk management
  - “Do I need a statin? What LDL goal should we aim for?” (Most adults with diabetes ≥40yo are on at least moderate-intensity statin unless contraindicated.)
  - “Should I be on an ACE inhibitor or ARB for kidney protection / BP control?” (Especially if microalbuminuria is present.)
- Monitoring and preventive care
  - Frequency of A1c checks (usually every 3 months if changing therapy or uncontrolled; every 6 months if stable).
  - Frequency of retinal screening: confirm annual eye exam for retinopathy (or sooner if changes).
  - Foot care education and annual foot exam.
  - Vaccinations: influenza annually; pneumococcal and shingles per age/guidelines; COVID boosters per current guidance.

Specific phrasing you can use with Dr. Lee about diabetes
- “Given my history of retinopathy, what’s the safe A1c target for me and how quickly should we aim to lower my A1c?”
- “Can we check A1c, urine albumin/creatinine, basic metabolic panel, and a lipid panel today?”
- “Would I benefit from adding an SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP‑1 receptor agonist for cardiovascular or kidney protection?”
- “Do I need a referral to endocrinology, or can we manage changes together?”

Questions about retinopathy to confirm/ask
- “When was my last ophthalmology/retinal exam? Do I need one before we adjust diabetes therapy?”
- “Any special monitoring required if we start medications that change glucose rapidly?”

Should you get a referral to Northwest Orthopedics?
- Consider a referral if any of these apply:
  - You have persistent, progressive joint pain (eg knees, hips, shoulders, back) limiting daily activities despite conservative care (NSAIDs, physical therapy, weight management).
  - You have mechanical symptoms (locking, catching), instability, or recurrent falls.
  - Significant functional decline (difficulty walking stairs, getting in/out of car, bathing).
  - X‑ray or imaging is needed and you want an orthopedic specialist to interpret and recommend management (injection, surgery, joint replacement).
  - You are considering surgery and need pre-op evaluation.
- If your complaints are mild and manageable, start with:
  - Primary care-directed conservative therapy: targeted physical therapy, supervised strengthening, weight loss, activity modification, trial of NSAIDs (if okay with your medical history), and imaging (X-ray) as needed.
  - Bone health assessment: ask about a DEXA scan if you have risk factors for osteoporosis (age, long-term steroids, low body weight, smoking, family history). (Screening age depends on sex and risk factors.)
- Questions to ask Dr. Lee if considering referral
  - “I’m having [describe location, severity, functional impact]. Can you examine me and order X‑rays? If pain limits activity or conservative care fails, could you refer me to Northwest Orthopedics?”
  - “Do I need a DEXA scan for bone density given my age and calcium/vitamin D therapy?”
  - “Are there specific red flags that would make you send me directly to orthopedics now?”

What to bring / prepare for your annual physical
- Up-to-date medication list (names, doses, frequency), including over-the-counter supplements.
- Recent lab results (especially A1c, lipid panel, kidney tests, INR).
- Blood pressure log if you check at home.
- A short symptom list (vision changes, numbness in feet, joint pain — where, how long, what worsens/improves).
- Any questions about referrals or specialist follow-ups.

