  - Do I need vaccinations (flu, pneumococcal, shingles) or lipid/aspirin recommendations tailored to my CV risk?
- Lifestyle and support
  - Would a referral to a diabetes educator, dietitian, or weight-management/physical-therapy program be helpful?
  - What are appropriate home glucose targets and how should I respond to high or low readings?

When to consider referral to orthopedics (questions to ask about a referral)
- Do I need a referral to Northwest Orthopedics?
  - Describe current joint pain, mobility limits, or fall history: ask whether your symptoms warrant imaging (X-ray or MRI) or a specialist referral.
  - Are there conservative options to try first (physical therapy, weight management, NSAIDs, activity modification, corticosteroid injection)?
  - If you have osteoporosis risk or prior low bone density: should we order a DEXA scan and consider osteoporosis medications?
  - If imaging shows arthritis or structural problems, when is a surgical opinion appropriate (failed conservative care, significant functional impairment, progressive deformity)?
  - Ask: “Given my age and risk factors, do you recommend PT and conservative care first, or should I see orthopedics now?”

Pre-visit labs and tests to consider asking for (so results are ready at the visit)
- A1C (if diabetes diagnosed or suspected)
- Fasting lipid panel
- Basic metabolic panel (BMP) including creatinine / eGFR
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR)
- CBC (to check anemia) and iron studies / ferritin / B12 if anemia not worked up recently
- LFTs (if meds under consideration)
- INR (if you are on warfarin) — confirm why INR is being monitored
- DEXA scan if you have osteoporosis risk or it hasn’t been done recently
- Recent ophthalmology report for retinopathy (or request updated referral)

Practical items to bring / prepare for the visit
- Complete med list (prescription, OTC, supplements — include doses and frequency)
- Any recent home blood pressure readings and home glucose logs
- Dates/comments of last eye exam, DEXA, labs, and any imaging of joints
- A short symptom list: joint(s) with pain, severity, limits on daily activities, falls, neuropathy symptoms (numbness/tingling), vision changes
- Specific goals you want to discuss (e.g., avoid more vision loss, reduce A1C, avoid surgery)

Suggested phrasing you can use with Dr. Lee
- “I have type 2 diabetes — my most recent A1C was X. Given my age and retinopathy history, what A1C target do you recommend, and do we need to change medications?”
- “Are SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP‑1 agonists appropriate for me given my cardiovascular and kidney risk?”
- “Can we check UACR and eGFR today and update my eye and foot exams?”
- “I have joint pain/limited mobility in [location]. Should I try PT first, or would you refer me to orthopedics now? If a referral is needed, could you send it to Northwest Orthopedics?”

When orthopedics referral is more clearly indicated
- Severe, persistent pain despite months of conservative care
- Significant functional limitations (can’t do work/daily tasks)
- Recurrent falls or mechanical symptoms (catching, locking)
- Imaging that suggests structural lesions that might need surgical review
- Progressive deformity or neurological signs (weakness, loss of reflexes)

Next steps I can take for you
- Draft a concise message/email to Dr. Jennifer Lee at Harbor General with these questions and a request for labs/referrals
- Create a printable checklist of items/questions to bring to your appointment
- Help you schedule labs (if you want me to prepare a list to send) or draft a message to Northwest Orthopedics if you decide to request a referral

Tell me:
1) If you want me to draft an email to Dr. Lee (and whether to include the lab/referral requests), and
2) The clarifications above about diabetes diagnosis, the “INR 320” number, and your current BP and anemia medications.

