This is a great set of things to prepare before your annual — here’s a concise, usable plan you can bring to your visit with Dr. Jennifer Lee, plus a few quick clarifying questions so I can tailor this further.

Quick clarifying questions
- When you wrote “my last INR was 320,” did you mean 3.20 (or something else)? An INR of 320 would be a recording error and would be an urgent safety issue. If it’s >4 or you have bleeding, contact your clinician or urgent care right away.
- Do you already have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes? If so, what medications (if any) are you taking for it, and what was your most recent A1c?
- Do you have joint pain, decreased mobility, or any specific orthopedic symptoms you’re worried about (e.g., knee pain, hip pain, instability, falls)?

If you want, I can draft an email or message to Dr. Lee summarizing these concerns and requesting specific tests/referrals — tell me if you want it sent/drafted and whether to use Harbor General contact info.

Questions to ask about type 2 diabetes management (bring these to your PCP)
- Do I have diabetes or prediabetes based on my labs? What is my current A1c and target A1c for my age/health status?
- Given my retinopathy and hypertension, should my A1c target be stricter or more relaxed?
- What diabetes meds do you recommend (metformin, SGLT2 inhibitor, GLP-1 RA, insulin, etc.) given my cardiovascular and kidney risk? (Mention any cost or injection concerns.)
- Could an SGLT2 or GLP-1 be beneficial for me for cardiovascular/renal protection or weight control?
- Should I be on a statin or other heart-protective meds given my age and risk factors?
- Any need to check or adjust blood pressure targets or antihypertensive meds (ACEi/ARB are often recommended with diabetes/microalbuminuria)?
- Do I need a referral to diabetes education or a dietitian for medical nutrition therapy and self-management training?
- Should I be using home glucose monitoring or a continuous glucose monitor (CGM) now? How often should I check?
- What signs of hypoglycemia should I watch for, and how should I treat episodes?
- What is the follow-up schedule for monitoring A1c, lipids, kidney function, and other labs?

Questions specifically about eye and microvascular complications
- With my history of retinopathy, do I need an updated dilated eye exam this year? Any change in retina care/referral to ophthalmology?
- Should I have routine screening for diabetic nephropathy (urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio) and neuropathy (monofilament exam)?

Tests and exams to consider ordering now
- A1c
- Fasting glucose (or as your clinician prefers)
- Basic metabolic panel (BMP) with eGFR
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio
- Lipid panel (fasting or nonfasting per clinic)
- CBC (you mentioned anemia) plus iron studies, B12 and folate as indicated
- Retinal/dilated eye exam or ophthalmology referral (annual if retinopathy)
- Foot exam and neuropathy screening (monofilament)
- Medication reconciliation (list all meds — you mentioned omeprazole and calcium/vitD; clarify your BP meds and any anticoagulants)
- If you’re on warfarin: repeat INR and anticoagulation review (and confirm the correct last INR)
- Vaccinations: influenza, pneumococcal if indicated, shingles (Shingrix recommended age 50+), COVID booster per guidelines

When to consider a referral to orthopedics (Northwest Orthopedics) — questions to ask / reasons
- Do I need an ortho referral now given my age and risk factors? (Explain you’re 52 with retinopathy and possible anticoagulation.)
- What symptoms would justify an ortho referral: persistent moderate–severe joint pain >6–12 weeks, mechanical symptoms (locking/catching), instability, progressive loss of function, or failed conservative therapy?
- Have we tried conservative management first (physical therapy, targeted exercise, weight loss, topical/systemic anti-inflammatories if safe with your meds, joint injections)? How long should these be tried before referring?
- If I’m on anticoagulation (warfarin), how would that affect the timing or safety of certain procedures (injections, arthroscopy, surgery)?
- Should I get imaging first (X-ray, maybe MRI) of the painful joint before an ortho consult?
- Given my age, bone health and fall risk: do I need a DEXA scan and/or osteoporosis management before considering joint surgery?

When an ortho referral is commonly appropriate
- Ongoing pain that limits activities of daily living despite months of conservative care
- X-ray evidence of moderate–severe osteoarthritis or structural damage
- Mechanical symptoms (locking, catching) or instability
- Significant deformity, or pain that interferes with sleep/work
- Consider PT, injections, bracing, and weight management first; ortho referral if those don’t help or imaging shows treatable structural problems

Practical phrasing you can use in the appointment
- “Given my retinopathy and age 52, do you recommend an A1c target of X%? What do you recommend for glucose medications considering CV and kidney protection?”
- “Can we check A1c, urine albumin, lipids, BMP, CBC, and a repeat INR today (or before any invasive procedures)?”
- “I have (or don’t have) joint pain in [location]. Should I try 6–8 weeks of PT or get imaging and an ortho referral now?”
- “Should I have a DEXA scan for bone density assessment given my age and supplements?”

Safety flags / urgent items
- Clarify the INR immediately if it was recorded incorrectly. If you have active bleeding or INR >4, reach out urgently.
- If you have sudden vision changes, new severe pain, numbness, weakness, or signs of infection — seek urgent care.

What to bring to the visit
- Current medication list (prescription, OTC, supplements; include doses)
- Any glucose logs or blood pressure logs
- Previous lab reports (if you have them) or dates of last tests
- A short list of your top symptoms and priorities (diabetes management, vision, joints)

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short message to Dr. Jennifer Lee summarizing these concerns and requesting specific labs/referrals? (I can draft and either save as a draft or send it if you want.)
- Make a printable checklist of the questions/tests to bring to your physical?
- Or tailor the questions to a specific joint problem if you tell me which joint is bothering you?

Tell me which of those you’d like, and please confirm the INR value so I can include appropriate urgency.

